(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e3a.1ddd919c6e.js"]={"22":[{"id":"sdl22_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the clinical presentations and diagnostic criteria across the spectrum of Obsessive-Compulsive and Related Disorders (including OCD, Body Dysmorphic Disorder, Hoarding Disorder, Trichotillomania, and Excoriation Disorder) based on symptom domain, time burden (> 1hour/day), and degree of patient insight.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient reports intrusive thoughts about contamination followed by repetitive handwashing that together consume more than an hour a day, and she recognizes the thoughts are excessive. Which diagnosis best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder","B":"Body dysmorphic disorder","C":"Hoarding disorder","D":"Trichotillomania","E":"Excoriation disorder"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This presentation, intrusive contamination obsessions paired with compulsive handwashing that are time-consuming and impairing, with preserved insight, is the classic symptom domain and time-burden profile of obsessive-compulsive disorder. Body dysmorphic disorder centers on preoccupation with a perceived appearance flaw, not contamination fears or handwashing rituals. Hoarding disorder centers on difficulty discarding possessions and resulting clutter, not contamination-driven washing rituals. Trichotillomania centers on repetitive hair-pulling, not contamination obsessions or washing compulsions. Excoriation disorder centers on repetitive skin-picking, not contamination-driven obsessions and washing rituals.","boardPrep":"Step 1: contamination obsessions paired with washing compulsions that are time-consuming and impairing is the classic OCD symptom pattern. COMLEX: apply the time-burden and symptom-domain criteria explicitly when distinguishing OCD from other obsessive-compulsive and related disorders.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 1 — the same trigger/timeline/impairment differential framework, applied here to the OCD and related disorder spectrum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the clinical presentations and diagnostic criteria across the spectrum of Obsessive-Compulsive and Related Disorders (including OCD, Body Dysmorphic Disorder, Hoarding Disorder, Trichotillomania, and Excoriation Disorder) based on symptom domain, time burden (> 1hour/day), and degree of patient insight.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient repeatedly pulls out his own hair from his scalp, resulting in noticeable hair loss, and reports the behavior is not done for cosmetic reasons and is difficult to resist. Which disorder best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder","B":"Body dysmorphic disorder","C":"Hoarding disorder","D":"Trichotillomania","E":"Excoriation disorder"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Trichotillomania, also called hair-pulling disorder, is defined by recurrent hair-pulling resulting in hair loss that is not motivated by cosmetic reasons and is difficult to resist, matching this patient's presentation precisely. Obsessive-compulsive disorder centers on obsessions paired with compulsions, not specifically the repetitive hair-pulling behavior described here. Body dysmorphic disorder centers on preoccupation with a perceived appearance flaw, not repetitive hair-pulling itself. Hoarding disorder centers on difficulty discarding possessions, unrelated to hair-pulling behavior. Excoriation disorder centers on repetitive skin-picking rather than hair-pulling, a related but distinct behavior.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hair-pulling resulting in hair loss, not done for cosmetic reasons and difficult to resist, is the defining pattern of trichotillomania. COMLEX: distinguish trichotillomania from cosmetic hair removal by the compulsive, difficult-to-resist quality of the behavior.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 1 — the same trigger/timeline/impairment differential framework, applied here to the OCD and related disorder spectrum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the clinical presentations and diagnostic criteria across the spectrum of Obsessive-Compulsive and Related Disorders (including OCD, Body Dysmorphic Disorder, Hoarding Disorder, Trichotillomania, and Excoriation Disorder) based on symptom domain, time burden (> 1hour/day), and degree of patient insight.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient acquires large amounts of possessions he cannot bring himself to discard, resulting in severely cluttered living spaces and significant distress, with no accompanying obsession driving the behavior. Which disorder best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder","B":"Hoarding disorder","C":"Body dysmorphic disorder","D":"Trichotillomania","E":"Excoriation disorder"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hoarding disorder is defined by excessive attachment to possessions, distress related to being separated from them, and excessive accumulation resulting in significant clutter and functional impairment, without an accompanying obsession driving the behavior, matching this patient's presentation. Obsessive-compulsive disorder can occasionally include hoarding as a compulsive response to an obsession, but this patient's presentation specifically lacks an accompanying obsession, pointing away from OCD. Body dysmorphic disorder centers on preoccupation with a perceived appearance flaw, unrelated to accumulating possessions. Trichotillomania centers on repetitive hair-pulling, unrelated to possession accumulation. Excoriation disorder centers on repetitive skin-picking, unrelated to possession accumulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: excessive possession accumulation and distress over discarding, without an accompanying obsession, is the defining pattern of hoarding disorder distinct from OCD-related hoarding. COMLEX: confirm the absence of a driving obsession before diagnosing hoarding disorder rather than OCD.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 1 — the same trigger/timeline/impairment differential framework, applied here to the OCD and related disorder spectrum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish primary Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder from secondary medical/neurologic conditions (e.g., PANDAS following group A streptococcal infection, basal ganglia lesions/Sydenham chorea, and dermatitis artefacta)","batch":1,"stem":"A previously healthy 7-year-old boy develops abrupt-onset obsessive-compulsive symptoms and tics within weeks of a documented group A beta-hemolytic streptococcal throat infection. Which secondary cause of OCD-like symptoms best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Sydenham chorea","B":"Huntington disease","C":"Post-streptococcal PANDAS","D":"Dermatitis artefacta","E":"Idiopathic OCD"},"correct":"C","explanation":"PANDAS, pediatric autoimmune neuropsychiatric disorders associated with streptococcus, is characterized by abrupt-onset OCD-like symptoms and tics in children following a documented group A streptococcal infection, thought to arise from an autoimmune process affecting the basal ganglia. Sydenham chorea is also a poststreptococcal basal ganglia disorder but presents with choreiform movements as its hallmark rather than this specific abrupt obsessive-compulsive and tic presentation. Huntington disease is a genetic basal ganglia disorder with a much later typical onset, unrelated to a recent streptococcal infection. Dermatitis artefacta involves self-inflicted skin lesions, unrelated to obsessive-compulsive symptoms or streptococcal infection. Idiopathic obsessive-compulsive disorder would not have this abrupt, infection-triggered onset pattern in a previously healthy child.","boardPrep":"Step 1: abrupt-onset OCD-like symptoms and tics in a child following streptococcal infection is the classic PANDAS presentation. COMLEX: consider PANDAS specifically when pediatric OCD symptoms have an abrupt, infection-associated onset.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 2 — the same primary-versus-secondary medical mimic framework used for secondary mania, applied here to OCD-like presentations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish primary Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder from secondary medical/neurologic conditions (e.g., PANDAS following group A streptococcal infection, basal ganglia lesions/Sydenham chorea, and dermatitis artefacta)","batch":1,"stem":"A child with a recent history of rheumatic fever develops obsessive-compulsive symptoms along with involuntary, irregular, dance-like movements of the limbs. Which secondary cause of OCD-like symptoms best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"PANDAS syndrome","B":"Huntington disease","C":"Dermatitis artefacta","D":"Idiopathic OCD","E":"Sydenham chorea"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sydenham chorea, a basal ganglia disease associated with rheumatic fever, presents with obsessive-compulsive symptoms alongside the hallmark involuntary, irregular, dance-like choreiform movements, matching this patient's presentation precisely. PANDAS without any movement abnormality does not include the choreiform movement component that defines this specific presentation. Huntington disease is a genetic basal ganglia disorder with a distinct inheritance pattern and typical adult onset, not linked to rheumatic fever. Dermatitis artefacta involves self-inflicted skin lesions, unrelated to choreiform movements or obsessive-compulsive symptoms. Idiopathic obsessive-compulsive disorder would not include the rheumatic-fever-associated choreiform movement component described here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: obsessive-compulsive symptoms with choreiform movements following rheumatic fever is the classic Sydenham chorea presentation and a key basal ganglia mimic of OCD. COMLEX: screen for a rheumatic fever history when obsessive-compulsive symptoms accompany involuntary movements.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 2 — the same primary-versus-secondary medical mimic framework used for secondary mania, applied here to OCD-like presentations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish primary Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder from secondary medical/neurologic conditions (e.g., PANDAS following group A streptococcal infection, basal ganglia lesions/Sydenham chorea, and dermatitis artefacta)","batch":1,"stem":"A young woman presents with bizarre, geometric, sharply angulated skin lesions on easily reachable areas, surrounded by entirely healthy skin, and gives a vague history about how the lesions developed. Which condition best fits this presentation, distinct from primary excoriation disorder?","choices":{"A":"Dermatitis artefacta","B":"PANDAS syndrome","C":"Sydenham chorea","D":"Huntington disease","E":"Idiopathic excoriation disorder"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Dermatitis artefacta, or factitious dermatitis, presents with bizarre, geometric, sharply angulated skin lesions surrounded by healthy, unaffected skin, along with a vague and detail-poor history of how the lesions developed, distinguishing it from simple excoriation disorder's more typical skin-picking pattern. PANDAS presents with obsessive-compulsive symptoms and tics following streptococcal infection, not with self-inflicted bizarre skin lesions. Sydenham chorea presents with choreiform movements following rheumatic fever, not with skin lesions. Huntington disease presents with progressive chorea and cognitive decline, not with self-inflicted skin lesions. Idiopathic excoriation disorder typically produces more superficial picking lesions without the bizarre, geometric, self-inflicted-injury pattern and vague history characteristic of dermatitis artefacta.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bizarre, geometric skin lesions surrounded by healthy skin with a vague history is the classic presentation of dermatitis artefacta, distinct from excoriation disorder. COMLEX: recognize the morphologic clues that distinguish factitious dermatitis from primary excoriation disorder.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 2 — the same primary-versus-secondary medical mimic framework used for secondary mania, applied here to OCD-like presentations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish primary Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder from overlapping psychiatric disorders (e.g., ego-syntonic Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder and Generalized Anxiety Disorder)","batch":1,"stem":"A patient describes a lifelong pattern of perfectionism, excessive concern for detail, and rigid rule-following that he views as simply the correct way to do things, without any true intrusive obsessions or compulsions he feels driven to perform. Which condition best fits this presentation, distinct from OCD?","choices":{"A":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder","B":"Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder","C":"Generalized anxiety disorder","D":"Body dysmorphic disorder","E":"Hoarding disorder"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This patient's lifelong, ego-syntonic pattern of perfectionism and rigid rule-following, viewed by the patient as simply correct rather than as unwanted or distressing, matches obsessive-compulsive personality disorder, which lacks the genuine, ego-dystonic obsessions and compulsions that define OCD. Obsessive-compulsive disorder requires genuine obsessions and compulsions that the patient typically experiences as unwanted and distressing, not the ego-syntonic personality traits described here. Generalized anxiety disorder involves excessive worry about real-life concerns, not the specific perfectionism and rule-rigidity pattern described. Body dysmorphic disorder centers on preoccupation with a perceived appearance flaw, unrelated to this pattern. Hoarding disorder centers on difficulty discarding possessions, unrelated to this perfectionism and rule-rigidity pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ego-syntonic perfectionism and rigid rule-following without genuine obsessions or compulsions distinguishes obsessive-compulsive personality disorder from OCD. COMLEX: assess whether traits are ego-syntonic or ego-dystonic when distinguishing OCPD from OCD.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 1 — the same DSM-5 differential-diagnosis framework, applied here to distinguishing OCD from overlapping personality and anxiety presentations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish primary Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder from overlapping psychiatric disorders (e.g., ego-syntonic Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder and Generalized Anxiety Disorder)","batch":1,"stem":"A patient reports persistent worry about her job performance, her finances, and her family's health, describing the worry as excessive but tied to genuine, realistic concerns, without ritualized compulsions performed to neutralize the worry. Which condition best fits this presentation, distinct from OCD?","choices":{"A":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder","B":"Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder","C":"Body dysmorphic disorder","D":"Generalized anxiety disorder","E":"Hoarding disorder"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This patient's worry about real-life concerns such as job performance, finances, and family health, without ritualized compulsions performed to neutralize the worry, matches generalized anxiety disorder, whose worries tend to be less irrational and less ego-dystonic than the intrusive obsessions of OCD. Obsessive-compulsive disorder involves obsessions that are typically irrational or excessive relative to genuine real-life concerns and are neutralized by compulsions, which are absent in this patient's presentation. Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder involves ego-syntonic perfectionism and rigidity as a personality trait, not worry about specific real-life concerns like this patient's. Body dysmorphic disorder centers on preoccupation with a perceived appearance flaw, unrelated to this patient's presentation. Hoarding disorder centers on difficulty discarding possessions, unrelated to this patient's presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: worry about real-life concerns without neutralizing compulsions, less irrational than OCD obsessions, distinguishes generalized anxiety disorder from OCD. COMLEX: assess for the presence of neutralizing compulsions when distinguishing GAD from OCD.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 1 — the same DSM-5 differential-diagnosis framework, applied here to distinguishing OCD from overlapping personality and anxiety presentations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish primary Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder from overlapping psychiatric disorders (e.g., ego-syntonic Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder and Generalized Anxiety Disorder)","batch":1,"stem":"Which feature most reliably distinguishes true obsessive-compulsive disorder from obsessive-compulsive personality disorder?","choices":{"A":"The patient's age at the time of clinical presentation to a psychiatrist","B":"The patient's overall level of general intelligence and educational background","C":"The patient's specific occupation or current employment status","D":"The patient's marital status at the time of clinical presentation","E":"Genuine ego-dystonic obsessions and compulsions versus ego-syntonic traits"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The most reliable distinguishing feature is the presence of genuine, ego-dystonic obsessions and compulsions in OCD, experienced as unwanted and distressing, compared with the ego-syntonic perfectionism and rigidity that the person with obsessive-compulsive personality disorder views as simply correct rather than as a source of distress. The patient's age at presentation does not reliably distinguish these two conditions, since both can present across a range of ages. General intelligence and educational background have no established role in distinguishing OCD from obsessive-compulsive personality disorder. Occupation or employment status is not a reliable distinguishing feature between these two conditions. Marital status has no established role in distinguishing OCD from obsessive-compulsive personality disorder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ego-dystonic obsessions and compulsions versus ego-syntonic personality traits is the single most reliable distinguishing feature between OCD and OCPD. COMLEX: ask directly whether symptoms feel unwanted and distressing or simply correct when differentiating these two conditions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 1 — the same DSM-5 differential-diagnosis framework, applied here to distinguishing OCD from overlapping personality and anxiety presentations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Select the appropriate step-wise treatment plan for Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder combining high-dose SSRIs, Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP) therapy, and adjunctive atypical antipsychotics (e.g., risperidone) for treatment-resistant or tic-related cases.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the recommended first-line pharmacologic approach for a newly diagnosed patient with moderate obsessive-compulsive disorder and no history of tics?","choices":{"A":"A benzodiazepine used as definitive, long-term monotherapy","B":"An SSRI, requiring higher doses than typically used for depression","C":"A tricyclic antidepressant other than clomipramine, lacking clomipramine's specific serotonergic potency","D":"An atypical antipsychotic used without an SSRI trial preceding it","E":"Electroconvulsive therapy, reserved for severe, treatment-resistant illness"},"correct":"B","explanation":"An SSRI is the standard pharmacologic approach for OCD, often requiring higher doses than those typically used for depression to achieve an optimal effect, reflecting OCD's distinct dose-response pattern compared with depressive disorders. A benzodiazepine used alone as definitive, long-term monotherapy is not an established treatment for OCD. An atypical antipsychotic used alone, without an SSRI trial preceding it, skips the step that antipsychotics are meant to augment; they are reserved as adjuncts for treatment-resistant or tic-related cases rather than standalone therapy. A tricyclic antidepressant other than clomipramine lacks the specific serotonergic potency that gives clomipramine, and not tricyclics generally, efficacy comparable to the SSRIs. Electroconvulsive therapy is reserved for severe, treatment-resistant presentations, not for newly diagnosed OCD.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SSRIs at higher-than-antidepressant doses as first-line OCD pharmacotherapy is a frequently tested dosing nuance. COMLEX: anticipate needing higher SSRI doses for OCD than would typically be used for depression.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 1 — the same stepped treatment-selection framework, applied here to combining SSRIs, ERP, and antipsychotic augmentation for OCD","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Select the appropriate step-wise treatment plan for Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder combining high-dose SSRIs, Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP) therapy, and adjunctive atypical antipsychotics (e.g., risperidone) for treatment-resistant or tic-related cases.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the recommended first-line psychotherapy for OCD that has patients confront avoided triggers while resisting the urge to perform their usual ritual?","choices":{"A":"Psychodynamic psychotherapy","B":"Habit reversal training","C":"Exposure and response prevention","D":"Interpersonal psychotherapy","E":"Prolonged exposure therapy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Exposure and response prevention, or ERP, is the recommended first-line psychotherapy for OCD, built on the principle of minimizing avoidance of distressing cues through gradual, structured exposure while preventing the usual compulsive response that would otherwise follow. Psychodynamic psychotherapy lacks rigorous evidence of efficacy specifically for OCD, unlike the well-established evidence base for ERP. Habit reversal training builds awareness of an urge and a competing response to it, and is the behavioral treatment for tic disorders, trichotillomania, and excoriation disorder rather than OCD. Interpersonal psychotherapy has no exposure component and is not an established first-line psychotherapy for OCD. Prolonged exposure therapy is the trauma-focused protocol for PTSD: it confronts trauma memories and reminders but has no response-prevention component aimed at rituals.","boardPrep":"Step 1: exposure and response prevention as the first-line, evidence-based psychotherapy for OCD is a frequently tested treatment-selection point. COMLEX: recommend ERP specifically, rather than a general supportive or psychodynamic approach, for OCD psychotherapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 1 — the same stepped treatment-selection framework, applied here to combining SSRIs, ERP, and antipsychotic augmentation for OCD","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Select the appropriate step-wise treatment plan for Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder combining high-dose SSRIs, Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP) therapy, and adjunctive atypical antipsychotics (e.g., risperidone) for treatment-resistant or tic-related cases.","batch":1,"stem":"Which augmentation strategy is best supported for OCD patients with comorbid tics who have not fully responded to an adequate SSRI trial?","choices":{"A":"Switching immediately to a benzodiazepine used as monotherapy","B":"Adding an atypical antipsychotic such as risperidone","C":"Adding a stimulant medication, without any other change","D":"Discontinuing all psychiatric medication in favor of psychotherapy","E":"Adding a first-generation, high-potency typical antipsychotic"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Adding an atypical antipsychotic such as risperidone is the best-supported augmentation strategy for OCD patients with comorbid tics who have not fully responded to an adequate SSRI trial, with response to this augmentation typically occurring relatively quickly. Switching immediately to a benzodiazepine used alone as monotherapy is not an established augmentation strategy for SSRI-refractory OCD. Adding a stimulant medication alone does not address OCD symptoms and is not an established augmentation strategy for this presentation. Discontinuing all psychiatric medication and pursuing psychotherapy alone abandons a reasonable pharmacologic option rather than appropriately augmenting the existing SSRI trial. A first-generation, high-potency typical antipsychotic is not the best-supported augmentation choice; the evidence base specifically favors atypical antipsychotics such as risperidone and aripiprazole.","boardPrep":"Step 1: atypical antipsychotic augmentation, particularly risperidone, for SSRI-refractory OCD with comorbid tics is a frequently tested step-wise treatment point. COMLEX: consider atypical antipsychotic augmentation specifically when tics accompany treatment-resistant OCD.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 1 — the same stepped treatment-selection framework, applied here to combining SSRIs, ERP, and antipsychotic augmentation for OCD","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Correlate the neurobiological pathogenesis of Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder with structural and functional alterations within Cortico-Striatal-Thalamic-Cortical (CSTC) neurocircuitry, specifying the roles of the orbitofrontal cortex, anterior cingulate cortex, and striatum/caudate nucleus","batch":1,"stem":"Which neurocircuitry has been most consistently implicated in mediating the cognitive-affective impairments seen in OCD?","choices":{"A":"The hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis","B":"The mesolimbic dopamine reward pathway","C":"Cortico-striatal-thalamic-cortical circuitry","D":"The corticospinal motor tract","E":"The cerebellar-vestibular pathway"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cortico-striatal-thalamic-cortical, or CSTC, circuitry has been most consistently implicated in mediating the cognitive-affective impairments seen in OCD, with activation or inhibition of different components of this circuit driving the compulsive and impulsive features of the disorder. The hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis alone is more centrally implicated in stress and cortisol regulation, not the specific cognitive-affective circuitry described here. The mesolimbic dopamine reward pathway alone is more centrally implicated in reward and addiction processes, not the specific circuitry most consistently linked to OCD. The corticospinal motor tract alone carries voluntary motor signals and is not the circuitry implicated in OCD's cognitive-affective features. The cerebellar-vestibular pathway alone is involved in balance and motor coordination, not the cognitive-affective circuitry implicated in OCD.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CSTC circuitry as the most consistently implicated neurocircuitry in OCD's cognitive-affective impairments is a frequently tested neurobiology point. COMLEX: link CSTC circuitry specifically to OCD rather than to other neurocircuitry frameworks.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 6 — the same neuroimaging/structural-correlate framework, applied here to CSTC neurocircuitry in OCD","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Correlate the neurobiological pathogenesis of Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder with structural and functional alterations within Cortico-Striatal-Thalamic-Cortical (CSTC) neurocircuitry, specifying the roles of the orbitofrontal cortex, anterior cingulate cortex, and striatum/caudate nucleus","batch":1,"stem":"Which three brain regions have been consistently implicated within CSTC circuitry in the pathophysiology of OCD?","choices":{"A":"The orbitofrontal cortex, anterior cingulate cortex, and striatum","B":"The hippocampus, amygdala, and cerebellar vermis","C":"The primary visual cortex, occipital pole, and lateral geniculate nucleus","D":"The brainstem reticular formation, pons, and medulla","E":"The parietal association cortex, precuneus, and posterior cingulate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The orbitofrontal cortex, anterior cingulate cortex, and striatum, including the caudate nucleus, have been consistently implicated within CSTC circuitry in the pathophysiology of OCD, with these regions showing structural and functional alterations on neuroimaging studies. The hippocampus, amygdala, and cerebellar vermis are more centrally implicated in memory, fear processing, and motor coordination, not the specific CSTC regions most consistently linked to OCD. The primary visual cortex, occipital pole, and lateral geniculate nucleus are visual processing structures, not the CSTC regions implicated in OCD. The brainstem reticular formation, pons, and medulla are involved in arousal and basic autonomic functions, not the CSTC regions implicated in OCD. The parietal association cortex, precuneus, and posterior cingulate are more associated with attention and self-referential processing, not the specific CSTC regions most consistently linked to OCD.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the orbitofrontal cortex, anterior cingulate cortex, and striatum as the key CSTC regions implicated in OCD is a frequently tested neuroanatomy-to-disorder correlate. COMLEX: identify these three specific regions when asked about OCD's structural neurobiology.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 6 — the same neuroimaging/structural-correlate framework, applied here to CSTC neurocircuitry in OCD","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Correlate the neurobiological pathogenesis of Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder with structural and functional alterations within Cortico-Striatal-Thalamic-Cortical (CSTC) neurocircuitry, specifying the roles of the orbitofrontal cortex, anterior cingulate cortex, and striatum/caudate nucleus","batch":1,"stem":"Early imaging work in OCD demonstrated that which two treatment modalities were both able to normalize functional alterations within CSTC circuitry?","choices":{"A":"Electroconvulsive therapy and deep brain stimulation","B":"Neurosurgical cingulotomy and capsulotomy","C":"Transcranial magnetic stimulation and vagus nerve stimulation","D":"Bright light therapy and sleep deprivation therapy","E":"Pharmacotherapy and cognitive-behavioral therapy"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Early imaging work in OCD showed that both pharmacotherapy and cognitive-behavioral therapy were able to normalize functional alterations within CSTC circuitry, supporting the idea that distinct treatment modalities can converge on the same underlying neurocircuit-level target. Electroconvulsive therapy and deep brain stimulation are reserved for severe, treatment-refractory cases and are not the two modalities specifically shown in early imaging work to normalize CSTC functional alterations. Neurosurgical cingulotomy and capsulotomy are reserved for highly treatment-refractory patients and are not the two modalities specifically referenced in this early normalization finding. Transcranial magnetic stimulation and vagus nerve stimulation are not the two modalities specifically referenced in this early CSTC normalization finding. Bright light therapy and sleep deprivation therapy are used for other conditions such as seasonal depression, not established treatments shown to normalize CSTC circuitry in OCD.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pharmacotherapy and CBT both normalizing CSTC functional alterations is a frequently tested neurobiology-to-treatment integration point. COMLEX: recognize that distinct first-line OCD treatments can converge on the same neurocircuit-level target.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 -> Obj 6 — the same neuroimaging/structural-correlate framework, applied here to CSTC neurocircuitry in OCD","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the clinical presentations and diagnostic criteria across the spectrum of Obsessive-Compulsive and Related Disorders (including OCD, Body Dysmorphic Disorder, Hoarding Disorder, Trichotillomania, and Excoriation Disorder) based on symptom domain, time burden (> 1hour/day), and degree of patient insight.","batch":2,"stem":"24F -> outpatient psychiatry -> persistent preoccupation with the size and shape of her nose, which appears unremarkable to others -> spends over 3 hours daily checking mirrors, camouflaging her nose with makeup, and comparing her appearance to others online, causing significant distress. Which diagnosis best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder","B":"Hoarding disorder","C":"Body dysmorphic disorder","D":"Trichotillomania","E":"Excoriation disorder"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This patient's persistent preoccupation with a perceived, objectively unremarkable appearance flaw, paired with repetitive checking, camouflaging, and comparing behaviors that consume much of the day and cause significant distress, matches body dysmorphic disorder precisely. Obsessive-compulsive disorder involves obsessions and compulsions that are not specifically centered on appearance concerns, unlike this patient's presentation. Hoarding disorder centers on difficulty discarding possessions, unrelated to appearance preoccupation. Trichotillomania centers on repetitive hair-pulling, unrelated to this patient's appearance-focused presentation. Excoriation disorder centers on repetitive skin-picking, unrelated to this patient's appearance-focused presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: preoccupation with a perceived, objectively minor appearance flaw paired with time-consuming checking and camouflaging behaviors is the classic BDD presentation. COMLEX: apply the time-burden and symptom-domain criteria to distinguish BDD from other obsessive-compulsive and related disorders.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — Applying symptom domain and time-burden criteria to diagnose body dysmorphic disorder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the clinical presentations and diagnostic criteria across the spectrum of Obsessive-Compulsive and Related Disorders (including OCD, Body Dysmorphic Disorder, Hoarding Disorder, Trichotillomania, and Excoriation Disorder) based on symptom domain, time burden (> 1hour/day), and degree of patient insight.","batch":2,"stem":"38F -> outpatient psychiatry -> for 2 years she has kept every used container, receipt, and scrap of paper because she fears that throwing one away will cause a family member to be harmed -> she wishes she could discard them, calls the fear senseless, and spends over 3 hours a day checking that nothing was thrown out -> rooms of her home are now cluttered. Which diagnosis best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Hoarding disorder","B":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder","C":"Body dysmorphic disorder","D":"Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder","E":"Excoriation disorder"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Kaplan and Sadock note that some patients with OCD hoard as a response to an unwanted obsession, often fears of harm, contamination, symmetry, or incompleteness, whereas in hoarding disorder there is no associated obsession and the person wishes to keep the items and is distressed by the thought of discarding them. This patient saves objects because of an intrusive, ego-dystonic fear of harm, wants to discard them, recognizes the fear as senseless, and spends more than an hour a day on checking rituals, so OCD is the diagnosis. Hoarding disorder would show attachment to the items themselves and little wish to throw them away. Body dysmorphic disorder centers on a perceived flaw in appearance. Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder can include trouble discarding worn-out objects, but it is a lifelong ego-syntonic pattern of perfectionism and control without intrusive fears of harm. Excoriation disorder is recurrent skin picking.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OCD = ego-dystonic obsessions and compulsions taking more than 1 hour a day or causing impairment; hoarding disorder = difficulty discarding because of a perceived need to save items, often with poor insight. COMLEX: ask why a patient keeps things; saving to prevent a feared harm points to OCD, while saving because the items feel needed points to hoarding disorder.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 4 — SSRI and exposure and response prevention once OCD is identified","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the clinical presentations and diagnostic criteria across the spectrum of Obsessive-Compulsive and Related Disorders (including OCD, Body Dysmorphic Disorder, Hoarding Disorder, Trichotillomania, and Excoriation Disorder) based on symptom domain, time burden (> 1hour/day), and degree of patient insight.","batch":2,"stem":"30F -> outpatient dermatology, referred to psychiatry -> reports repetitive skin-picking at areas of minor irregularity, resulting in visible lesions and scarring -> has made multiple unsuccessful attempts to stop, and the behavior consumes over an hour daily, causing significant distress. Which diagnosis best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder","B":"Body dysmorphic disorder","C":"Hoarding disorder","D":"Trichotillomania","E":"Excoriation disorder"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This patient's repetitive skin-picking resulting in visible lesions and scarring, multiple unsuccessful attempts to stop, and a substantial daily time burden with significant distress matches excoriation, or skin-picking, disorder precisely. Obsessive-compulsive disorder involves obsessions paired with compulsions that are not specifically centered on skin-picking, unlike this patient's presentation. Body dysmorphic disorder can include skin-picking as a response to appearance concerns specifically, but this patient's picking is described as targeting areas of minor irregularity rather than being driven by an appearance preoccupation, pointing toward primary excoriation disorder instead. Hoarding disorder centers on difficulty discarding possessions, unrelated to skin-picking. Trichotillomania centers on repetitive hair-pulling rather than skin-picking, a related but distinct behavior.","boardPrep":"Step 1: repetitive skin-picking resulting in lesions, with failed attempts to stop and a substantial daily time burden, is the classic excoriation disorder presentation. COMLEX: distinguish primary excoriation disorder from BDD-related skin-picking by the absence of an underlying appearance preoccupation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 1 — Applying symptom domain and time-burden criteria to diagnose excoriation disorder","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish primary Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder from secondary medical/neurologic conditions (e.g., PANDAS following group A streptococcal infection, basal ganglia lesions/Sydenham chorea, and dermatitis artefacta)","batch":2,"stem":"64M -> outpatient primary care -> no prior psychiatric history -> over the past 4 months has developed intrusive fears that he left the stove on, with repeated checking that now takes over an hour each day, and he recognizes the fears as excessive -> no recent infection, no mood symptoms, no substance use -> general medical examination is unremarkable. Which next step is most appropriate before diagnosing primary obsessive-compulsive disorder?","choices":{"A":"A neurologic examination with brain imaging for a basal ganglia process","B":"Antistreptococcal antibody titers to evaluate for a post-infectious cause","C":"An empiric high-dose SSRI trial, since primary OCD is the likely cause","D":"Exposure and response prevention as the sole initial step in his care","E":"A skin examination for self-inflicted lesions of dermatitis artefacta"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Kaplan and Sadock note that OCD often develops before age 30 and that new-onset OCD in an older individual should raise questions about neurologic contributions; because OCD-like syndromes occur with basal ganglia diseases such as Sydenham chorea and Huntington disease, clinicians should search for neurologic signs, so a focused neurologic examination and imaging come first in a 64-year-old with new symptoms. Antistreptococcal titers belong to the evaluation of PANDAS, a pediatric post-streptococcal syndrome, and he has had no recent infection. Starting a high-dose SSRI or exposure and response prevention may be right later, but both assume a primary disorder before a secondary cause has been excluded in this late-onset case. Dermatitis artefacta is self-inflicted skin injury and has no bearing on checking rituals.","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary OCD-like syndromes arise from basal ganglia disease, including PANDAS, Sydenham chorea, and Huntington disease, all linked to cortico-striatal-thalamic circuitry. COMLEX: first-onset obsessions or compulsions after about age 30 call for a neurologic examination before a primary OCD diagnosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 5 — cortico-striatal-thalamic-cortical circuitry in OCD","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish primary Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder from secondary medical/neurologic conditions (e.g., PANDAS following group A streptococcal infection, basal ganglia lesions/Sydenham chorea, and dermatitis artefacta)","batch":2,"stem":"16F -> outpatient dermatology -> 6 months of irregular patches of hair loss on the scalp with hairs broken at different lengths -> she denies pulling her hair, and her parents have never seen her do so -> no rash, scaling, or itch -> thyroid and iron studies are normal -> the dermatologist suspects hair-pulling disorder but cannot exclude alopecia areata or tinea capitis. Which test is most useful for making this distinction?","choices":{"A":"Antistreptococcal antibody titers","B":"Magnetic resonance imaging of the brain","C":"An empiric trial of a high-dose SSRI","D":"A biopsy of an affected scalp patch","E":"A serum ceruloplasmin concentration"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Kaplan and Sadock note that many medical disorders cause alopecia and that a biopsy may be necessary to distinguish hair-pulling disorder from alopecia areata and tinea capitis; a scalp biopsy is the test that separates these when the history is unclear and the patient denies pulling. Antistreptococcal titers are used in the evaluation of PANDAS, which presents with abrupt OCD symptoms or tics, not patchy hair loss. Brain imaging looks for basal ganglia disease in late-onset or neurologically atypical OCD and does not identify the cause of alopecia. An SSRI trial is a treatment, not a diagnostic test, and trials of SSRIs in hair-pulling disorder have not consistently shown efficacy. Ceruloplasmin screens for Wilson disease, which does not cause patchy scalp alopecia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: trichotillomania = recurrent pulling with hair loss and repeated attempts to stop; dermatologic mimics include alopecia areata and tinea capitis. COMLEX: when a patient denies pulling, use dermatologic evaluation including biopsy before giving a psychiatric label; habit reversal training has shown efficacy once trichotillomania is confirmed.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 1 — trichotillomania within the obsessive-compulsive and related disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish primary Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder from secondary medical/neurologic conditions (e.g., PANDAS following group A streptococcal infection, basal ganglia lesions/Sydenham chorea, and dermatitis artefacta)","batch":2,"stem":"19M -> student health clinic -> 3 weeks of intense itching that is worst at night, with scratched and picked papules between his fingers, on his wrists, and along his waistband -> his roommate has begun itching as well -> he picks only at itchy spots and has no concerns about his appearance, no contamination fears, and no history of self-injury. Which explanation for his skin picking is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Excoriation disorder, with picking that he cannot stop","B":"Dermatitis artefacta, with deliberately produced lesions","C":"Scabies infestation, with picking driven by intense itch","D":"Body dysmorphic disorder, with picking at perceived flaws","E":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder, with contamination picking"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Kaplan and Sadock note that many dermatologic conditions cause skin picking, especially conditions associated with itching such as scabies, so a medical cause must be excluded before diagnosing excoriation disorder. Nocturnal itch, lesions in the finger webs, wrists, and waistband, and an itchy roommate point to scabies, and picking limited to itchy spots is secondary to the infestation. Excoriation disorder is recurrent picking with repeated attempts to stop that is not explained by a dermatologic condition. Dermatitis artefacta involves deliberately produced, bizarre, geometric lesions next to healthy skin with a vague history. Picking in body dysmorphic disorder targets perceived appearance flaws. Picking in OCD is a response to contamination obsessions, which he denies.","boardPrep":"Step 1: scabies (Sarcoptes scabiei) causes nocturnal pruritus with burrows in finger webs, wrists, and the waistline and spreads to close contacts; treat with permethrin. COMLEX: rule out itching dermatoses and other medical causes before diagnosing excoriation disorder.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 → Obj 1 — excoriation disorder diagnostic criteria","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish primary Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder from overlapping psychiatric disorders (e.g., ego-syntonic Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder and Generalized Anxiety Disorder)","batch":2,"stem":"45F -> outpatient psychiatry -> recurrent major depressive disorder, now in her third episode with low mood, insomnia, and poor appetite -> during each episode she has persistent, guilty thoughts that she has failed her children, which she broods over for hours -> she performs no rituals to undo the thoughts, and family confirms the thoughts disappear completely between episodes -> she asks whether she also has OCD. Which diagnosis best accounts for her repetitive thoughts?","choices":{"A":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder","B":"Major depressive disorder","C":"Generalized anxiety disorder","D":"Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder","E":"Hoarding disorder"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Kaplan and Sadock explain that major depression is often associated with obsessive thoughts that border on obsessions, but they occur only during a depressive episode, and the ruminations of depression are mood-congruent and are not neutralized by compulsions. Her guilty brooding appears exclusively during depressive episodes, matches her depressed mood, and has no neutralizing rituals, so it is part of major depressive disorder and a separate OCD diagnosis is not needed. OCD would show ego-dystonic obsessions, usually with compulsions, that persist outside mood episodes. Generalized anxiety disorder involves worry about multiple real-life concerns for at least 6 months, not episode-bound guilt. Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder is a lifelong, ego-syntonic pattern of perfectionism and rigidity. Hoarding disorder is difficulty discarding possessions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: depressive rumination is mood-congruent and episode-bound; OCD obsessions are ego-dystonic, intrusive, and usually neutralized by compulsions. COMLEX: when obsessive-sounding thoughts appear, check whether they occur only during mood episodes before adding an OCD diagnosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 1 — DSM-5 criteria for a major depressive episode","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish primary Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder from overlapping psychiatric disorders (e.g., ego-syntonic Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder and Generalized Anxiety Disorder)","batch":2,"stem":"29F -> outpatient primary care -> describes ongoing worry about her upcoming performance review, her aging parents' health, and her mortgage payments -> the worry feels excessive to her but is grounded in real, ongoing life circumstances, and she has no rituals performed to neutralize the worry. Which diagnosis best fits this presentation, distinct from OCD?","choices":{"A":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder","B":"Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder","C":"Body dysmorphic disorder","D":"Hoarding disorder","E":"Generalized anxiety disorder"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This patient's worry about real, ongoing life circumstances such as her performance review, her parents' health, and her mortgage, without any neutralizing rituals, matches generalized anxiety disorder, whose worries tend to be grounded in real-life concerns and are less irrational than the intrusive obsessions of OCD. Obsessive-compulsive disorder involves obsessions that are typically irrational or excessive relative to genuine real-life concerns and are neutralized by compulsions, which are absent here. Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder involves ego-syntonic perfectionism and rigidity as a personality trait, not worry about specific real-life concerns like this patient's. Body dysmorphic disorder centers on preoccupation with a perceived appearance flaw, unrelated to this presentation. Hoarding disorder centers on difficulty discarding possessions, unrelated to this presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: worry grounded in real-life concerns without neutralizing rituals distinguishes generalized anxiety disorder from OCD. COMLEX: confirm the absence of neutralizing compulsions when distinguishing GAD from OCD in a worry-focused presentation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 3 — Distinguishing generalized anxiety disorder from OCD through real-life-grounded worry without neutralizing rituals","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish primary Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder from overlapping psychiatric disorders (e.g., ego-syntonic Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder and Generalized Anxiety Disorder)","batch":2,"stem":"26M -> outpatient psychiatry -> washes his hands and scrubs his apartment for hours each day because he is certain neighbors pump poison gas through the vents -> when asked, he insists the belief is completely reasonable and cannot consider any other explanation -> he also hears a voice commenting on his actions, and his speech is tangential. Which feature best distinguishes a psychotic disorder from OCD in this patient?","choices":{"A":"The number of hours each day that he spends washing","B":"A fear whose content centers on contamination or on poisoning","C":"Fixed conviction plus hallucinations and tangential speech","D":"The presence of repetitive washing and cleaning behavior","E":"An age at onset that falls within the third decade of life"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Kaplan and Sadock note that psychotic symptoms often lead to obsessive thoughts, compulsive behaviors, or hoarding that can be hard to tell apart from OCD, but patients with psychotic disorders cannot acknowledge the unreasonableness of their behavior and typically have a host of other features not seen in OCD. His fixed conviction, auditory hallucination, and tangential speech point to a psychotic disorder. Time spent washing shows severity and can exceed an hour a day in either condition. Contamination content is common in OCD and does not by itself signal psychosis. Repetitive washing and cleaning occur in both conditions. Onset in the twenties is typical of both OCD, which often begins before age 30, and schizophrenia, so age does not separate them.","boardPrep":"Step 1: OCD insight is specified as good or fair, poor, or absent with delusional beliefs, but hallucinations and disorganized speech point to a primary psychotic disorder. COMLEX: in a patient with compulsive behavior, ask about hallucinations, thought disorder, and how firmly the belief is held before choosing between an SSRI and an antipsychotic.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 2 — positive and disorganized symptom domains of schizophrenia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Select the appropriate step-wise treatment plan for Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder combining high-dose SSRIs, Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP) therapy, and adjunctive atypical antipsychotics (e.g., risperidone) for treatment-resistant or tic-related cases.","batch":2,"stem":"27F -> outpatient psychiatry -> newly diagnosed moderate OCD, no tics, no comorbid depression -> she and her psychiatrist are deciding on an initial treatment plan. Which approach best reflects appropriate step-wise, first-line management?","choices":{"A":"Immediate combination of an SSRI with an atypical antipsychotic from the very first visit","B":"Immediate neurosurgical cingulotomy as the appropriate first-line treatment approach for newly diagnosed OCD","C":"Immediate electroconvulsive therapy as the appropriate first-line treatment approach for newly diagnosed OCD","D":"Either an SSRI or exposure and response prevention by itself as a reasonable first-line start","E":"A benzodiazepine used as definitive, long-term first-line monotherapy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"For this patient with newly diagnosed moderate OCD, no tics, and no comorbid depression, either an SSRI or exposure and response prevention alone is a reasonable first-line start, since clinical guidelines indicate that either pharmacotherapy or psychotherapy alone is an appropriate initial intervention before escalating to combination or augmentation strategies. Immediately combining an SSRI with an atypical antipsychotic from the very first visit skips ahead of the step-wise approach, since antipsychotic augmentation is reserved for treatment-resistant or tic-related cases rather than first-line use. Neurosurgical cingulotomy is reserved for highly treatment-refractory patients, not as a first-line approach for newly diagnosed moderate OCD. Electroconvulsive therapy is reserved for severe, treatment-resistant presentations, not as the first-line approach here. A benzodiazepine alone as definitive, long-term monotherapy is not an established first-line treatment for OCD.","boardPrep":"Step 1: either an SSRI or ERP alone as a reasonable first-line start for OCD, reserving combination and augmentation strategies for later steps, is a frequently tested step-wise treatment framework. COMLEX: avoid skipping ahead to combination or augmentation therapy before a first-line monotherapy trial.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 4 — Applying step-wise, first-line treatment selection to newly diagnosed moderate OCD","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Select the appropriate step-wise treatment plan for Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder combining high-dose SSRIs, Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP) therapy, and adjunctive atypical antipsychotics (e.g., risperidone) for treatment-resistant or tic-related cases.","batch":2,"stem":"31M -> outpatient psychiatry -> OCD with severe symptoms and significant comorbid depression -> he and his psychiatrist are deciding on an initial treatment approach. Which consideration should most influence the choice to begin with pharmacotherapy rather than psychotherapy alone?","choices":{"A":"His symptom severity and comorbid depression, which favor starting medication first","B":"His personal preference should outweigh his symptom severity and comorbid depression","C":"His occupation, since demanding jobs favor psychotherapy over medication","D":"His age, since younger patients respond poorly to SSRIs","E":"His insurance coverage, since cost outweighs clinical severity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"For a patient with severe OCD symptoms and significant comorbid depression, it is reasonable to begin with pharmacotherapy first, since guidelines note that more severe symptoms or comorbid depression favor starting with medication rather than psychotherapy alone as the initial step. His personal preference is a relevant factor in shared decision-making but should not outweigh his symptom severity and comorbid depression when choosing an initial treatment approach. His specific occupation is not established as the single deciding factor in this treatment choice. His age alone is not established as the single deciding factor in this treatment choice. His insurance coverage is a practical consideration but is not established as the single clinical deciding factor in this treatment choice.","boardPrep":"Step 1: symptom severity and comorbid depression favoring an initial pharmacotherapy-first approach is a frequently tested step-wise treatment consideration. COMLEX: weigh symptom severity and comorbidity explicitly when choosing between pharmacotherapy-first and psychotherapy-first approaches for OCD.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 4 — Weighing symptom severity and comorbid depression when selecting an initial OCD treatment approach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Select the appropriate step-wise treatment plan for Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder combining high-dose SSRIs, Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP) therapy, and adjunctive atypical antipsychotics (e.g., risperidone) for treatment-resistant or tic-related cases.","batch":2,"stem":"19M -> outpatient psychiatry -> moderate OCD with comorbid motor and vocal tics, ongoing since childhood -> he has not yet started any treatment. Which step-wise treatment consideration is most specific to his presentation with comorbid tics?","choices":{"A":"His tics should be entirely ignored throughout his OCD treatment planning process","B":"Antipsychotic augmentation may help if his tics or OCD prove treatment-resistant","C":"His tics indicate that pharmacotherapy for OCD is absolutely contraindicated","D":"His tics indicate that exposure and response prevention is absolutely contraindicated","E":"His tics require immediate neurosurgical intervention before any other treatment is considered"},"correct":"B","explanation":"For this patient with comorbid tics, atypical antipsychotic augmentation, such as risperidone, may be particularly useful if his OCD symptoms or tics prove treatment-resistant to an initial SSRI or ERP trial, reflecting the specific step-wise consideration that tic-related cases are one of the named indications for this augmentation strategy. His tics should not be entirely ignored during OCD treatment planning, since they are a clinically relevant comorbidity that specifically informs later augmentation decisions. His tics do not make pharmacotherapy for OCD absolutely contraindicated; an SSRI remains a reasonable first-line option even with comorbid tics. His tics do not make exposure and response prevention absolutely contraindicated; ERP remains a reasonable first-line psychotherapy option even with comorbid tics. His tics do not require immediate neurosurgical intervention before any other treatment; neurosurgical approaches are reserved for highly treatment-refractory presentations, not as an initial step.","boardPrep":"Step 1: atypical antipsychotic augmentation being particularly indicated for tic-related, treatment-resistant OCD is a frequently tested step-wise treatment nuance. COMLEX: anticipate antipsychotic augmentation specifically as a later step for tic-related OCD rather than a contraindication to first-line treatment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 4 — Applying step-wise treatment considerations specific to OCD with comorbid tics","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Correlate the neurobiological pathogenesis of Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder with structural and functional alterations within Cortico-Striatal-Thalamic-Cortical (CSTC) neurocircuitry, specifying the roles of the orbitofrontal cortex, anterior cingulate cortex, and striatum/caudate nucleus","batch":2,"stem":"26F -> outpatient psychiatry -> established OCD -> undergoes functional neuroimaging as part of a research study before and after a successful course of SSRI treatment. Which finding would best support the CSTC circuitry model of OCD pathogenesis?","choices":{"A":"Normalization of CSTC circuit alterations following treatment response","B":"Complete absence of detectable brain activity on the pretreatment scan","C":"Abnormal activity confined to the occipital visual cortex","D":"Abnormal activity confined to the cerebellar vermis","E":"No change in any brain region after her treatment response"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Normalization of functional alterations within CSTC circuitry following successful SSRI treatment would best support the CSTC circuitry model of OCD pathogenesis, consistent with early imaging work showing that effective treatment can normalize these specific functional alterations. Complete absence of any detectable brain activity anywhere before treatment is not a realistic or expected neuroimaging finding and would not support this specific model. A finding entirely isolated to the occipital visual cortex would not support the CSTC model, since this region is not part of the circuitry implicated in OCD. A finding entirely isolated to the cerebellar vermis would not support the CSTC model, since this region is not part of the circuitry implicated in OCD. No change whatsoever in any brain region regardless of treatment response would actually argue against, rather than support, the CSTC circuitry model of OCD pathogenesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: normalization of CSTC functional alterations after successful treatment is the key neuroimaging finding supporting the CSTC model of OCD. COMLEX: link successful OCD treatment response to expected normalization within CSTC circuitry on neuroimaging.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 5 — Interpreting neuroimaging normalization within CSTC circuitry as support for the CSTC model of OCD","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Correlate the neurobiological pathogenesis of Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder with structural and functional alterations within Cortico-Striatal-Thalamic-Cortical (CSTC) neurocircuitry, specifying the roles of the orbitofrontal cortex, anterior cingulate cortex, and striatum/caudate nucleus","batch":2,"stem":"33M -> outpatient psychiatry -> established OCD -> a researcher explains that structural imaging studies have consistently implicated his orbitofrontal cortex, anterior cingulate cortex, and striatum. Which explanation best describes how this circuitry is thought to relate to his symptoms?","choices":{"A":"This circuitry has been definitively proven to be the sole and complete cause of his OCD","B":"This circuitry governs his visual processing and has no bearing on his OCD symptoms","C":"This circuitry governs his basic motor coordination and has no bearing on his OCD symptoms","D":"Activation or inhibition of this circuitry is thought to drive his compulsive symptoms","E":"This circuitry has no established relationship to his obsessive-compulsive symptoms"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Activation or inhibition of different components of CSTC circuitry, involving the orbitofrontal cortex, anterior cingulate cortex, and striatum, is thought to drive the compulsive and impulsive features of OCD, representing the current working model of this circuitry's role rather than a fully settled, singular causal explanation. This circuitry has not been definitively proven to be the sole and complete cause of his OCD; multiple neurotransmitter systems and genetic factors are also implicated, and the field's understanding remains an evolving hypothesis rather than a settled singular cause. This circuitry does not govern visual processing; the regions described are specifically implicated in cognitive-affective and compulsive symptom generation, not vision. This circuitry does not govern basic motor coordination; its role in OCD is specifically tied to cognitive-affective and compulsive symptom generation. This circuitry does have an established, well-documented relationship to obsessive-compulsive symptoms, contrary to this statement.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CSTC circuitry activation and inhibition patterns driving compulsive and impulsive features is the current working model of OCD neurobiology, not a fully proven singular cause. COMLEX: describe CSTC circuitry's role in OCD as a working model integrating multiple regions rather than a single isolated cause.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 5 — Explaining how CSTC circuitry activation and inhibition patterns are thought to drive OCD symptom features","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl22_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Correlate the neurobiological pathogenesis of Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder with structural and functional alterations within Cortico-Striatal-Thalamic-Cortical (CSTC) neurocircuitry, specifying the roles of the orbitofrontal cortex, anterior cingulate cortex, and striatum/caudate nucleus","batch":2,"stem":"46F -> outpatient psychiatry -> established OCD, poor response to SSRI treatment -> her psychiatrist explains that beyond serotonergic mechanisms, other neurotransmitter systems including dopaminergic and glutamatergic pathways are also implicated in OCD. How does this broader neurotransmitter involvement clinically inform her treatment options?","choices":{"A":"It means serotonergic medications such as SSRIs were the wrong choice for her OCD from the start","B":"It means her OCD diagnosis itself was fundamentally incorrect from the very beginning","C":"It means no further pharmacologic treatment of any kind should be attempted for her OCD","D":"It means neurosurgical intervention is the last remaining option that can help her OCD","E":"It explains why dopamine receptor blocker augmentation can help treatment-refractory OCD"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The broader involvement of dopaminergic pathways alongside serotonergic mechanisms in OCD helps explain why augmenting a serotonin reuptake inhibitor with a dopamine receptor blocker, such as an atypical antipsychotic, can be a useful strategy specifically for treatment-refractory OCD like this patient's presentation. This broader neurotransmitter involvement does not mean SSRIs were the wrong choice for her OCD; SSRIs remain the appropriate first-line choice, with the dopaminergic and glutamatergic evidence informing augmentation strategies for refractory cases rather than replacing first-line treatment. This broader neurotransmitter involvement does not mean her original OCD diagnosis was fundamentally incorrect; it instead reflects a more complete understanding of OCD's underlying neurobiology. This broader neurotransmitter involvement does not mean no further pharmacologic treatment should be attempted; it specifically supports pursuing an evidence-based augmentation strategy. This broader neurotransmitter involvement does not mean neurosurgical intervention is the last remaining option; pharmacologic augmentation remains an appropriate next step before neurosurgical approaches are considered.","boardPrep":"Step 1: dopaminergic and glutamatergic involvement in OCD explaining the rationale for dopamine receptor blocker augmentation in refractory cases is a frequently tested neurobiology-to-treatment link. COMLEX: connect OCD's multi-neurotransmitter neurobiology to specific, evidence-based augmentation strategies for refractory cases.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 22 Obj 5 — Connecting OCD's dopaminergic and glutamatergic neurobiology to augmentation strategies for treatment-refractory cases","isHighYield":false}],"23":[{"id":"sdl23_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate Acute Stress Disorder, Posttraumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD), and Adjustment Disorders based on required symptom domain clusters (intrusion, avoidance, negative cognitions, hyperarousal), timing of onset, and total symptom duration thresholds following an external stressor or traumatic event.","batch":1,"stem":"A 30-year-old man reports intrusive memories, avoidance of reminders, negative mood, and hyperarousal beginning 5 weeks after a car accident. Which diagnosis best fits this timeline?","choices":{"A":"Acute stress disorder","B":"Adjustment disorder","C":"Posttraumatic stress disorder","D":"Generalized anxiety disorder","E":"Brief psychotic disorder"},"correct":"C","explanation":"PTSD requires symptoms of intrusion, avoidance, negative cognitions, and hyperarousal persisting beyond 1 month. Acute stress disorder is limited to 3 days-1 month post-trauma. Adjustment disorder lacks the specific symptom-cluster requirement and defined stressor severity. GAD lacks intrusion/avoidance tied to a discrete trauma. Brief psychotic disorder requires psychotic features.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the 1-month threshold is the classic PTSD-vs-acute-stress-disorder discriminator. COMLEX: both PTSD and acute stress disorder are named Trauma and Stressor-Related Disorders on the Blueprint.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 1","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate Acute Stress Disorder, Posttraumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD), and Adjustment Disorders based on required symptom domain clusters (intrusion, avoidance, negative cognitions, hyperarousal), timing of onset, and total symptom duration thresholds following an external stressor or traumatic event.","batch":1,"stem":"A 22-year-old survives a workplace explosion and 10 days later has dissociative symptoms, intrusion, and avoidance causing marked distress. What is the required minimum symptom count for this diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"One symptom of intrusion and one of avoidance","B":"Two symptoms of negative mood and two of arousal alterations","C":"Any single symptom regardless of category","D":"Six symptoms from three categories","E":"Nine symptoms from among five categories"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acute stress disorder requires nine total symptoms drawn from any of five categories (intrusion, negative mood, dissociation, avoidance, arousal) within 3 days to 1 month of trauma exposure, distinguishing it from PTSD, which requires a specific minimum per domain.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute stress disorder = 9 symptoms from 5 categories, no domain minimum, within 3 days-1 month. COMLEX: contrast this threshold with PTSD's domain-specific minimums.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 1","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate Acute Stress Disorder, Posttraumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD), and Adjustment Disorders based on required symptom domain clusters (intrusion, avoidance, negative cognitions, hyperarousal), timing of onset, and total symptom duration thresholds following an external stressor or traumatic event.","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old develops anxious mood and situational worry 6 weeks after being notified of a pending layoff, without meeting full criteria for an anxiety or depressive disorder. Symptoms began within how many months of the stressor for this diagnosis to apply?","choices":{"A":"Within 3 months of the stressor","B":"Within 1 month of the stressor","C":"Within 6 months of the stressor","D":"Within 3 days of the stressor","E":"Within 12 months of the stressor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Adjustment disorder symptoms must begin within 3 months of an identifiable stressor and typically resolve within 6 months once the stressor or its consequences resolve. This distinguishes it from PTSD and acute stress disorder, which have stricter trauma-specific onset windows.","boardPrep":"Step 1: adjustment disorder = onset within 3 months, resolves within 6 months of stressor resolution. COMLEX: this is a named Trauma and Stressor-Related Disorders category.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 1","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish primary trauma- and stressor-related disorders from secondary organic or psychiatric conditions (such as traumatic brain injury, acute substance withdrawal, borderline personality disorder, and uncomplicated bereavement).","batch":1,"stem":"A combat veteran with new intrusive memories and hyperarousal also sustained a blast-related head injury during the same event. Before diagnosing PTSD, which condition must first be evaluated as a possible primary contributor?","choices":{"A":"Generalized anxiety disorder","B":"Panic disorder","C":"Dissociative identity disorder","D":"Traumatic brain injury","E":"Factitious disorder"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Head injury sustained during the traumatic event is a key treatable medical contributor to posttraumatic symptomatology that must be excluded, since TBI can independently produce cognitive and behavioral symptoms overlapping with PTSD. Generalized anxiety, panic, dissociative identity and factitious disorders are psychiatric differentials in the same table, but head injury is the treatable medical contributor from the event itself, so it is checked first.","boardPrep":"Step 1: always screen for TBI when trauma involved a blast or blunt head injury before anchoring on PTSD. COMLEX: medical causes head the PTSD differential diagnosis table.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 2","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish primary trauma- and stressor-related disorders from secondary organic or psychiatric conditions (such as traumatic brain injury, acute substance withdrawal, borderline personality disorder, and uncomplicated bereavement).","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with chronic emotional dysregulation, unstable relationships, and fear of abandonment develops trauma-related symptoms that overlap heavily with PTSD. Which personality disorder is most difficult to distinguish from PTSD and may coexist with it?","choices":{"A":"Borderline personality disorder","B":"Obsessive-compulsive personality disorder","C":"Schizoid personality disorder","D":"Narcissistic personality disorder","E":"Avoidant personality disorder"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Borderline personality disorder can be difficult to distinguish from PTSD because the two disorders share affective instability and can coexist or even be causally related, unlike the other listed personality disorders.","boardPrep":"Step 1: BPD-PTSD overlap and comorbidity is a classic differential trap. COMLEX: named in the PTSD differential diagnosis table.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 2","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish primary trauma- and stressor-related disorders from secondary organic or psychiatric conditions (such as traumatic brain injury, acute substance withdrawal, borderline personality disorder, and uncomplicated bereavement).","batch":1,"stem":"A widow continues to grieve her husband's death 8 weeks later with sadness and social withdrawal but retains functioning within culturally expectable limits. Which diagnosis should NOT be applied here?","choices":{"A":"Uncomplicated bereavement","B":"Adjustment disorder","C":"Normal grief reaction","D":"No psychiatric diagnosis warranted","E":"Culturally expected mourning response"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Uncomplicated bereavement, though it produces temporary impairment, remains within expectable bounds of a reaction to loss and is therefore not classified as adjustment disorder, which requires distress or impairment out of proportion to the stressor. No psychiatric diagnosis is also accurate, since grief within expectable bounds is not a disorder, so it is not the label to avoid.","boardPrep":"Step 1: uncomplicated bereavement is explicitly excluded from adjustment disorder criteria. COMLEX: grief reaction is its own named Trauma and Stressor-Related Disorders category.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 -> Obj 2","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate clinical prognostic factors in Posttraumatic Stress Disorder to predict recovery likelihood based on onset speed, symptom duration (< 6 months), premorbid functioning, strength of social support, and presence of comorbid psychiatric, medical, or substance-related disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"Two patients develop PTSD after similar trauma. Patient A has rapid symptom onset, symptoms lasting under 6 months, strong social support, and no comorbid disorders. Patient B has delayed onset, symptoms over a year, and comorbid substance use. Which patient has the better prognosis?","choices":{"A":"Patient B, given his delayed symptom onset","B":"Both patients share an identical prognosis","C":"Patient A, given his rapid onset","D":"Patient B, given his comorbid substance use","E":"Neither patient has any identifiable prognostic factors"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Positive prognostic factors for PTSD include rapid symptom onset, short symptom duration (under 6 months), good premorbid functioning, strong social support, and absence of comorbid psychiatric, medical, or substance-related disorders — all favoring Patient A.","boardPrep":"Step 1: memorize the 5 positive PTSD prognostic factors. COMLEX: social support and comorbidity absence are recurring prognosis themes across psychiatric Clinical Presentations.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 3","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate clinical prognostic factors in Posttraumatic Stress Disorder to predict recovery likelihood based on onset speed, symptom duration (< 6 months), premorbid functioning, strength of social support, and presence of comorbid psychiatric, medical, or substance-related disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with PTSD reports living alone with minimal contact with friends or family since the trauma. How does this factor most likely influence his prognosis?","choices":{"A":"Social support has no prognostic bearing","B":"Living in isolation accelerates his recovery","C":"Isolation protects against re-exposure to trauma cues","D":"Social contact worsens his hyperarousal","E":"Social isolation worsens his outcome"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A good network of social support is associated with lower likelihood of developing severe PTSD and faster recovery; conversely, poor social support is a recognized negative prognostic factor. Isolation is not protective: avoiding reminders is itself a PTSD feature, and lack of social support raises risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: social support is both a risk-modifying and prognosis-modifying factor for PTSD. COMLEX: reinforces the biopsychosocial model of recovery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 3","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate clinical prognostic factors in Posttraumatic Stress Disorder to predict recovery likelihood based on onset speed, symptom duration (< 6 months), premorbid functioning, strength of social support, and presence of comorbid psychiatric, medical, or substance-related disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient develops PTSD symptoms that persist beyond 12 months despite treatment. Which additional factor present at onset would have predicted this more chronic course?","choices":{"A":"Comorbid depressive disorder at symptom onset","B":"Rapid symptom onset within days of trauma","C":"Strong premorbid occupational functioning","D":"Robust family and peer support system","E":"Symptom duration under 6 months at initial presentation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PTSD comorbid with other psychiatric disorders, such as depression, tends to be more severe, more chronic, and more difficult to treat, unlike the other listed factors, which are all positive prognostic indicators.","boardPrep":"Step 1: comorbidity is the single most consistent factor associated with a more chronic, treatment-resistant PTSD course. COMLEX: absence of comorbidity is one of the features that predicts a better PTSD prognosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 3","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Select appropriate first-line pharmacotherapy and evidence-based psychotherapy for Posttraumatic Stress Disorder matching specific clinical features (e.g., SSRIs for core symptoms, prazosin for trauma-related nightmares, Trauma-Focused CBT/EMDR) while identifying relatively contraindicated medication classes (e.g., benzodiazepines).","batch":1,"stem":"A 38-year-old with newly diagnosed PTSD asks about first-line medication. Which class has the strongest evidence for efficacy in PTSD?","choices":{"A":"Benzodiazepines","B":"Atypical antipsychotics","C":"Tricyclic antidepressants","D":"SSRIs such as sertraline or paroxetine","E":"Anticonvulsants"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sertraline and paroxetine, both SSRIs, have the most robust evidence for PTSD efficacy and are first-line pharmacotherapy. Benzodiazepines lack evidence and are relatively contraindicated; the other classes have only limited or promising, not first-line, evidence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sertraline/paroxetine = first-line PTSD pharmacotherapy, both FDA-approved for this indication. COMLEX: pharmacotherapeutics is a named measured outcome domain.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 3","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Select appropriate first-line pharmacotherapy and evidence-based psychotherapy for Posttraumatic Stress Disorder matching specific clinical features (e.g., SSRIs for core symptoms, prazosin for trauma-related nightmares, Trauma-Focused CBT/EMDR) while identifying relatively contraindicated medication classes (e.g., benzodiazepines).","batch":1,"stem":"A veteran with PTSD reports severe, recurrent trauma-related nightmares disrupting sleep despite an adequate SSRI trial. Which agent has the strongest evidence specifically for this symptom?","choices":{"A":"Lorazepam as needed nightly","B":"Fluoxetine at a higher dose","C":"Quetiapine as monotherapy","D":"Diphenhydramine at bedtime","E":"Prazosin at bedtime"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Prazosin, an alpha-1 adrenergic antagonist, shows the strongest specific evidence for reducing trauma-related nightmares in PTSD, while benzodiazepines lack efficacy evidence and are relatively contraindicated in this population. A higher SSRI dose, quetiapine and diphenhydramine are not the agents the course names for nightmares; prazosin is the symptom-specific choice, and atypical antipsychotics are listed only for treatment-resistant PTSD.","boardPrep":"Step 1: prazosin -> nightmares is a classic symptom-specific PTSD pairing. COMLEX: alpha-1 antagonism as a targeted adjunctive mechanism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 3","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Select appropriate first-line pharmacotherapy and evidence-based psychotherapy for Posttraumatic Stress Disorder matching specific clinical features (e.g., SSRIs for core symptoms, prazosin for trauma-related nightmares, Trauma-Focused CBT/EMDR) while identifying relatively contraindicated medication classes (e.g., benzodiazepines).","batch":1,"stem":"A patient asks why her psychiatrist declined to prescribe a benzodiazepine for her PTSD anxiety symptoms. What is the most accurate explanation?","choices":{"A":"Benzodiazepines are withheld to avoid masking the diagnosis of PTSD","B":"Benzodiazepine evidence is lacking, and they are relatively contraindicated","C":"Benzodiazepines are held back until two SSRIs have failed","D":"Benzodiazepines interact dangerously with every SSRI used for PTSD","E":"Benzodiazepines are avoided in PTSD solely for their abuse potential"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Meta-analyses show a lack of evidence for benzodiazepine effectiveness in PTSD, and one analysis considered them relatively contraindicated in this population, independent of abuse-potential concerns alone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: benzodiazepines are explicitly named as lacking evidence for PTSD, a frequently tested negative. COMLEX: reinforces evidence-based prescribing over symptomatic reflex prescribing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 3","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze predisposing risk factors and genetic vulnerabilities (such as FKBP5 polymorphisms, serotonin transporter s/s genotypes, prior childhood adversity, and trauma severity) that influence individual susceptibility to developing PTSD following trauma exposure.","batch":1,"stem":"A researcher studying genetic vulnerability to PTSD finds a specific gene polymorphism associated with regulation of the stress-hormone response. Which gene is most classically implicated?","choices":{"A":"The BRCA1 gene","B":"The CFTR gene","C":"The APOE4 gene","D":"The FKBP5 gene","E":"The huntingtin gene"},"correct":"D","explanation":"FKBP5 polymorphisms, which affect glucocorticoid receptor sensitivity and HPA-axis regulation, are among the most consistently replicated genetic variants associated with PTSD risk, unlike the other listed genes, which are unrelated to stress-response regulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FKBP5 = classic PTSD/HPA-axis gene-environment interaction candidate. COMLEX: genetic vulnerability is a named etiologic factor for PTSD.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 6","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze predisposing risk factors and genetic vulnerabilities (such as FKBP5 polymorphisms, serotonin transporter s/s genotypes, prior childhood adversity, and trauma severity) that influence individual susceptibility to developing PTSD following trauma exposure.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with a history of severe childhood abuse develops PTSD after a relatively minor adult trauma. Which serotonin transporter genotype most classically interacts with childhood adversity to increase this risk?","choices":{"A":"The l/l homozygous genotype","B":"The s/l heterozygous genotype","C":"The s/s serotonin transporter genotype","D":"A monoamine oxidase A genotype","E":"A dopamine transporter genotype"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The s/s genotype of the serotonin transporter gene may interact with childhood adversity to increase PTSD risk, representing a gene-environment interaction distinct from the other listed genotypes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: s/s serotonin transporter genotype x childhood adversity is a classic gene-environment interaction vignette. COMLEX: reinforces childhood trauma as a predisposing vulnerability factor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 6","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze predisposing risk factors and genetic vulnerabilities (such as FKBP5 polymorphisms, serotonin transporter s/s genotypes, prior childhood adversity, and trauma severity) that influence individual susceptibility to developing PTSD following trauma exposure.","batch":1,"stem":"A twin study of combat veterans finds that monozygotic co-twins of veterans with combat-related PTSD have more mood disorder symptoms than dizygotic co-twins. What does this finding suggest?","choices":{"A":"PTSD is entirely environmentally determined","B":"Shared genetic vulnerability between PTSD and mood disorders","C":"Mood disorders directly cause PTSD","D":"Twin studies disprove any genetic contribution to PTSD","E":"Dizygotic twins share more genetic material than monozygotic twins"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The higher concordance of mood symptoms among monozygotic versus dizygotic co-twins of veterans with PTSD suggests a shared genetic vulnerability underlying both PTSD and mood disorders, consistent with genes accounting for roughly 30 percent of PTSD risk variance.","boardPrep":"Step 1: twin studies showing MZ > DZ concordance support a genetic (not purely environmental) contribution. COMLEX: family history of psychopathology is a named PTSD risk predictor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 -> Obj 6","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate Acute Stress Disorder, Posttraumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD), and Adjustment Disorders based on required symptom domain clusters (intrusion, avoidance, negative cognitions, hyperarousal), timing of onset, and total symptom duration thresholds following an external stressor or traumatic event.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old man spent several evenings watching repeated television coverage of a mass shooting in another city; he knew no one involved and was never in danger. Six weeks later he reports nightmares about the shooting, intrusive images from the footage, avoidance of the news, and feeling on edge, with mild distress. Which statement best describes his diagnostic status?","choices":{"A":"PTSD, since his symptoms have now lasted for more than 1 month","B":"Acute stress disorder, since his symptoms began soon after viewing","C":"PTSD with delayed expression, given his onset weeks after the event","D":"Not PTSD, since exposure through media does not qualify","E":"PTSD with dissociative symptoms, given his intrusive images"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Kaplan and Sadock's DSM-5 table for PTSD lists exposure through media, electronics, movies, or photographs as an exclusion, so trauma witnessed only on television does not meet the exposure criterion however typical the later symptoms are; he does not meet PTSD criteria. Lasting more than 1 month satisfies the duration rule but cannot replace a qualifying exposure. Acute stress disorder needs the same kind of exposure and is limited to 3 days to 1 month, and he is now 6 weeks out. The delayed-expression specifier applies when full criteria are not met until 6 months or more after the event, and here neither exposure nor timing fits. The dissociative specifier requires depersonalization or derealization; intrusive images are intrusion symptoms, not dissociation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PTSD and acute stress disorder require actual or threatened death, serious injury, or sexual violence experienced, witnessed, or learned about; viewing it on media does not count, and PTSD needs symptoms for more than 1 month. COMLEX: confirm the exposure criterion before applying duration rules to a patient with post-event symptoms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — applying DSM-5 duration and exclusion criteria to anxiety disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate Acute Stress Disorder, Posttraumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD), and Adjustment Disorders based on required symptom domain clusters (intrusion, avoidance, negative cognitions, hyperarousal), timing of onset, and total symptom duration thresholds following an external stressor or traumatic event.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man was informed 10 weeks ago that his position is being eliminated in 3 months. Since then he has had low mood, insomnia, and difficulty concentrating at work, impairing his performance beyond what colleagues facing similar news have experienced. He denies intrusive memories, avoidance behavior, or hyperarousal tied to a specific traumatic event. Which of the following best explains his presentation?","choices":{"A":"Adjustment disorder, given a non-traumatic stressor and 3-month onset","B":"Major depressive disorder, given his low mood and insomnia","C":"PTSD, given his functional impairment following a stressor","D":"Acute stress disorder, given his symptom onset within weeks of the stressor","E":"Generalized anxiety disorder, given his difficulty concentrating"},"correct":"A","explanation":"His symptoms began within 3 months of an identifiable, non-traumatic stressor (job loss notice) and exceed the expectable reaction, fitting adjustment disorder with depressed mood. He lacks the trauma-exposure and intrusion/avoidance/hyperarousal cluster required for PTSD or acute stress disorder, and lacks the full symptom criteria and duration for MDD or GAD.","boardPrep":"Step 1: adjustment disorder applies when a stressor (not necessarily traumatic) produces disproportionate distress without meeting another disorder's full criteria. COMLEX: named Trauma and Stressor-Related Disorders Clinical Presentation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — MDD vs. persistent depressive disorder duration criteria","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate Acute Stress Disorder, Posttraumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD), and Adjustment Disorders based on required symptom domain clusters (intrusion, avoidance, negative cognitions, hyperarousal), timing of onset, and total symptom duration thresholds following an external stressor or traumatic event.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old survivor of a mass shooting develops intrusive memories, avoidance, negative cognitions, and hyperarousal beginning immediately, but her full symptom picture does not meet diagnostic threshold until 7 months after the event. Which PTSD course specifier applies?","choices":{"A":"With dissociative symptoms","B":"Acute stress disorder converting to PTSD","C":"Chronic PTSD with early onset","D":"Subthreshold PTSD requiring no specifier","E":"With delayed expression"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The \"with delayed expression\" specifier applies when all diagnostic criteria for PTSD are not met until 6 months or more after the initial traumatic event, distinct from the dissociative-symptoms specifier, which concerns depersonalization/derealization rather than timing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: delayed expression = full criteria not met until >=6 months post-trauma, a distinct course specifier from dissociative subtype. COMLEX: course specifiers are testable alongside symptom specifiers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — anxiety disorder course and treatment-timing considerations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish primary trauma- and stressor-related disorders from secondary organic or psychiatric conditions (such as traumatic brain injury, acute substance withdrawal, borderline personality disorder, and uncomplicated bereavement).","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old presents with new intrusive thoughts, hypervigilance, and irritability 3 days after abruptly stopping long-term alcohol use following a stressful event. Vital signs show tachycardia and tremor. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Start an SSRI immediately for presumed PTSD","B":"Diagnose acute stress disorder and begin trauma-focused CBT","C":"Diagnose generalized anxiety disorder given the autonomic symptoms","D":"Evaluate for acute alcohol withdrawal first","E":"Refer for EMDR without further workup"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acute alcohol withdrawal can produce a clinical picture — autonomic arousal, irritability, intrusive-feeling thoughts — that mimics trauma-related disorders and must be excluded first, since it is a medical cause requiring its own management before any trauma-related diagnosis is assigned.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute substance/alcohol withdrawal is explicitly named in PTSD's differential diagnosis. COMLEX: always sequence medical/substance workup before psychiatric diagnosis in an acute presentation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 6 — primary versus secondary psychiatric presentations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish primary trauma- and stressor-related disorders from secondary organic or psychiatric conditions (such as traumatic brain injury, acute substance withdrawal, borderline personality disorder, and uncomplicated bereavement).","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman with a history of chronic emotional dysregulation, unstable relationships, and recurrent self-harm reports \"reliving\" past mistreatment along with avoidance and hyperarousal following a recent argument with her partner. She has had similar episodes for years, each triggered by perceived abandonment. Which of the following best distinguishes her presentation from PTSD?","choices":{"A":"The presence of any hyperarousal symptoms","B":"Her age, since PTSD is rare in patients under 30","C":"Her gender, since PTSD predominantly affects men","D":"The absence of any avoidance of trauma reminders","E":"A longstanding, pre-trauma pattern of abandonment-triggered instability"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Borderline personality disorder can closely mimic PTSD, but a longstanding pattern of instability and abandonment-triggered reactivity predating a discrete trauma favors BPD, which can coexist with or be mistaken for PTSD. Hyperarousal occurs in PTSD too; age and gender are not reliable discriminators; and avoidance is present in this vignette, so its absence cannot be the distinguishing feature.","boardPrep":"Step 1: BPD vs. PTSD hinges on chronicity/pattern versus a discrete precipitating trauma. COMLEX: BPD is explicitly named in the PTSD differential diagnosis table.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 6 — primary versus secondary psychiatric presentations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Distinguish primary trauma- and stressor-related disorders from secondary organic or psychiatric conditions (such as traumatic brain injury, acute substance withdrawal, borderline personality disorder, and uncomplicated bereavement).","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old widower reports persistent sadness and occasional tearfulness 3 months after his wife's death, but continues working, maintains friendships, and denies functional impairment beyond what is culturally expected for his loss. Which of the following is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"His presentation reflects uncomplicated bereavement","B":"His presentation reflects adjustment disorder with depressed mood","C":"His presentation reflects major depressive disorder","D":"His presentation requires urgent psychiatric hospitalization","E":"His presentation reflects a grief reaction that exceeds the expected cultural norm for his loss"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because his distress and functional level remain within expectable bounds of a normal grief reaction and he shows no impairment beyond cultural norms, this represents uncomplicated bereavement, which is explicitly excluded from the adjustment disorder diagnosis. Uncomplicated bereavement keeps functioning within expectable bounds, so it gives no reason for admission.","boardPrep":"Step 1: uncomplicated bereavement is a named exclusion criterion for adjustment disorder. COMLEX: grief reaction is its own named Trauma and Stressor-Related Disorders category.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 6 — primary versus secondary psychiatric presentations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate clinical prognostic factors in Posttraumatic Stress Disorder to predict recovery likelihood based on onset speed, symptom duration (< 6 months), premorbid functioning, strength of social support, and presence of comorbid psychiatric, medical, or substance-related disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old firefighter develops PTSD after a building collapse. He has excellent premorbid occupational functioning, an intact marriage, close friendships, and no other psychiatric or substance history. His symptoms began within days of the event. Based on these factors, which outcome is most likely?","choices":{"A":"A poor prognosis due to occupational trauma exposure severity in isolation","B":"A favorable prognosis with a higher likelihood of complete recovery","C":"An unpredictable prognosis, since premorbid functioning does not influence outcome","D":"A chronic, treatment-resistant course regardless of these factors","E":"A prognosis equivalent to a patient with comorbid substance use disorder"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Rapid symptom onset, good premorbid functioning, strong social support (marriage, friendships), and absence of comorbid disorders are all positive prognostic factors for PTSD, together predicting a favorable course and higher likelihood of complete recovery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: apply the 5 positive prognostic factors together, not in isolation, to estimate PTSD course. COMLEX: reinforces integrating psychosocial supports into prognosis assessment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 5 — psychotherapy and treatment-response principles","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate clinical prognostic factors in Posttraumatic Stress Disorder to predict recovery likelihood based on onset speed, symptom duration (< 6 months), premorbid functioning, strength of social support, and presence of comorbid psychiatric, medical, or substance-related disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old develops PTSD after an assault. One year later, she still meets full criteria, has developed a comorbid major depressive episode, and reports minimal social contact since moving to a new city alone. Which combination of factors most directly explains her prolonged, worsening course?","choices":{"A":"Her age by itself, since younger adults have uniformly worse PTSD outcomes","B":"The specific type of trauma by itself, independent of any other factor","C":"Her gender by itself, since prognosis is determined primarily by sex","D":"Rapid symptom onset, which paradoxically worsens long-term prognosis","E":"Long symptom duration, comorbid depression, and poor social support"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Symptom duration beyond 6 months, comorbid psychiatric illness (depression), and lack of social support are each negative prognostic factors for PTSD; together they explain a prolonged and worsening course, rather than any single demographic factor in isolation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: negative prognostic factors compound — duration + comorbidity + poor support predict chronicity. COMLEX: integrative biopsychosocial reasoning across risk domains.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 5 — psychotherapy and treatment-response principles","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Evaluate clinical prognostic factors in Posttraumatic Stress Disorder to predict recovery likelihood based on onset speed, symptom duration (< 6 months), premorbid functioning, strength of social support, and presence of comorbid psychiatric, medical, or substance-related disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A clinician is asked to estimate long-term outcome for a newly diagnosed PTSD patient without treatment. Based on natural history data, what is the most accurate statement to convey?","choices":{"A":"Nearly all untreated patients will recover completely within 3 months","B":"Untreated PTSD universally progresses to a chronic, unremitting course","C":"About half of patients will have recovered by 1 year without treatment","D":"Recovery without treatment occurs in less than 5 percent of patients","E":"Untreated PTSD resolves at the same rate regardless of prognostic factors"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Natural history data indicate that about 50 percent of untreated PTSD patients recover by 1 year, while the remainder continue with mild, moderate, or unchanged/worsening symptoms — a rate meaningfully influenced by the positive and negative prognostic factors already discussed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: know the natural-history recovery figures (untreated, about 30% recover completely and about 50% have recovered by 1 year). COMLEX: useful for patient counseling and informed-consent discussions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 5 — psychotherapy and treatment-response principles","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl23_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Select appropriate first-line pharmacotherapy and evidence-based psychotherapy for Posttraumatic Stress Disorder matching specific clinical features (e.g., SSRIs for core symptoms, prazosin for trauma-related nightmares, Trauma-Focused CBT/EMDR) while identifying relatively contraindicated medication classes (e.g., benzodiazepines).","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old combat veteran with PTSD has had only partial response to sertraline at an adequate dose and duration. He continues to have significant core symptoms. Which augmentation strategy has the most promising initial evidence?","choices":{"A":"Adding a scheduled benzodiazepine","B":"Adding low-dose risperidone to his regimen","C":"Switching to a tricyclic antidepressant as monotherapy","D":"Adding an over-the-counter antihistamine for anxiety","E":"Discontinuing pharmacotherapy in favor of supportive counseling"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The atypical antipsychotic risperidone has promising initial evidence as an augmentation strategy for treatment-resistant PTSD, alongside venlafaxine and topiramate, whereas benzodiazepines lack evidence and antihistamines are not an evidence-based augmentation option. Switching to a tricyclic or stopping medication for counseling alone is not among the options with evidence; the course lists venlafaxine, risperidone and topiramate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: risperidone augmentation is specifically named for treatment-resistant PTSD. COMLEX: reflects a stepped, evidence-tiered approach to pharmacotherapy escalation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 6 — safer alternatives to benzodiazepines","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Select appropriate first-line pharmacotherapy and evidence-based psychotherapy for Posttraumatic Stress Disorder matching specific clinical features (e.g., SSRIs for core symptoms, prazosin for trauma-related nightmares, Trauma-Focused CBT/EMDR) while identifying relatively contraindicated medication classes (e.g., benzodiazepines).","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old with PTSD wants a psychotherapy approach that focuses on correcting distorted trauma-related beliefs, such as viewing the world as globally dangerous and uncontrollable, rather than repeated exposure to trauma memories. Which therapy best matches this description?","choices":{"A":"Prolonged exposure therapy","B":"Eye movement desensitization and reprocessing therapy","C":"Cognitive processing therapy","D":"Present centered therapy","E":"Psychodynamic psychotherapy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cognitive processing therapy specifically emphasizes correcting faulty trauma-related attributions and overgeneralizations, distinguishing it from prolonged exposure (repeated engagement with trauma memories/reminders), EMDR (sensory input during recall), and present centered therapy (focus on current, non-trauma challenges). Psychodynamic psychotherapy works through reconstruction of the traumatic events with abreaction and catharsis rather than targeted correction of distorted beliefs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: match each PTSD psychotherapy to its core mechanism — CPT targets cognitive distortions specifically. COMLEX: psychotherapy modality differentiation is a recurring testable theme.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 6 — safer alternatives to benzodiazepines","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Select appropriate first-line pharmacotherapy and evidence-based psychotherapy for Posttraumatic Stress Disorder matching specific clinical features (e.g., SSRIs for core symptoms, prazosin for trauma-related nightmares, Trauma-Focused CBT/EMDR) while identifying relatively contraindicated medication classes (e.g., benzodiazepines).","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old with chronic PTSD has significant insomnia unresponsive to sleep hygiene measures. Her psychiatrist wants to avoid benzodiazepines given her PTSD diagnosis. Which agent is preferred for her insomnia?","choices":{"A":"Diazepam every night","B":"Alprazolam as needed","C":"Chlordiazepoxide nightly","D":"Low-dose trazodone nightly","E":"Short-course lorazepam"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Low-dose trazodone is preferred over benzodiazepines for insomnia in chronic PTSD, since benzodiazepines are relatively contraindicated in this population despite their common off-label use for sleep.","boardPrep":"Step 1: trazodone > benzodiazepines for PTSD-related insomnia — a frequently tested prescribing-safety point. COMLEX: reflects the broader relative-contraindication theme for benzodiazepines in trauma-related disorders.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 6 — safer alternatives to benzodiazepines","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze predisposing risk factors and genetic vulnerabilities (such as FKBP5 polymorphisms, serotonin transporter s/s genotypes, prior childhood adversity, and trauma severity) that influence individual susceptibility to developing PTSD following trauma exposure.","batch":2,"stem":"Two office workers develop symptoms after the same building explosion. Worker 1 was in the room where the blast occurred and was trapped under debris for 6 hours before rescue. Worker 2 was on a different floor, heard the explosion, and left the building within minutes without injury. Both have similar education, social support, and psychiatric histories. Which conclusion about their PTSD risk is best supported?","choices":{"A":"Worker 2 is at higher risk, since uncertainty about events raises PTSD risk","B":"Both carry equal risk, since they lived through the same traumatic event","C":"Worker 1 is at higher risk, given greater severity, duration, and proximity","D":"Neither is at meaningful risk once both have reached physical safety","E":"Risk is set by genotype, so their exposures cannot predict outcome"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Kaplan and Sadock state that the most important risk factors for PTSD are the severity, duration, and proximity of a person's exposure to the actual trauma, and they describe a dose-response relationship between the degree of trauma and the likelihood of symptoms. Worker 1 was closest to the blast, trapped longest, and faced a greater threat to life, so he carries the higher risk. Uncertainty from a distance is not described as outweighing direct, prolonged exposure. Being involved in the same event does not mean equal exposure, since risk scales with dose. Reaching safety does not end risk; the persistence of symptoms after the threat has ended is a core feature of PTSD. Genes account for only about 30 percent of the variance in PTSD risk, so exposure characteristics remain strong predictors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PTSD risk rises with trauma severity, duration, and proximity, along with female sex, childhood adversity, prior psychiatric history, and poor social support. COMLEX: prioritize early follow-up and screening for the survivors with the most direct and prolonged exposure after a mass-casualty event.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 3 — prognostic factors that predict PTSD recovery","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze predisposing risk factors and genetic vulnerabilities (such as FKBP5 polymorphisms, serotonin transporter s/s genotypes, prior childhood adversity, and trauma severity) that influence individual susceptibility to developing PTSD following trauma exposure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman was held at gunpoint during a store robbery. During the event she felt as if she were watching herself from outside her body and time seemed to slow down. Six weeks later she meets criteria for PTSD. She has a college degree, stable employment, strong support from her family, no prior trauma, and no family history of mental illness. Which characteristic of her history is an identified predictor of PTSD?","choices":{"A":"Marked dissociation during the trauma itself","B":"Her college education and her stable employment","C":"Strong support from her family after the event","D":"The absence of any trauma earlier in her life","E":"A family history free of psychiatric illness"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A meta-analysis cited by Kaplan and Sadock identified seven predictors of PTSD: prior trauma, prior psychological adjustment, family history of psychopathology, greater perceived life threat during the trauma, lower posttrauma social support, greater emotional distress during exposure, and higher dissociation during exposure. Her out-of-body feeling and slowed time are peritraumatic dissociation, the predictor present here, along with a high perceived threat to life. Lower education is the community risk factor, so a college degree and stable work are not predictors. Lack of social support raises risk, so strong family support is protective. Prior trauma and a family history of psychopathology are risk factors, so their absence lowers rather than raises her risk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: peritraumatic dissociation, perceived life threat, prior trauma, family psychiatric history, and poor posttrauma support predict PTSD. COMLEX: ask trauma survivors whether they felt detached or unreal during the event, and arrange closer follow-up when they did.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — acute stress disorder dissociative symptoms and the 1-month PTSD threshold","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl23_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze predisposing risk factors and genetic vulnerabilities (such as FKBP5 polymorphisms, serotonin transporter s/s genotypes, prior childhood adversity, and trauma severity) that influence individual susceptibility to developing PTSD following trauma exposure.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher is designing a study to estimate the proportion of PTSD risk attributable to genetics versus environment. Based on twin and heritability studies, which statement most accurately reflects current evidence?","choices":{"A":"Genes account for over 90 percent of PTSD risk, making environment nearly irrelevant","B":"Genes account for roughly 30 percent of PTSD risk variance","C":"PTSD has no measurable genetic component and is entirely environmentally determined","D":"Genetic and environmental contributions to PTSD risk are exactly equal at 50 percent each","E":"Heritability estimates for PTSD have never been studied in twin cohorts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Twin and heritability studies estimate that genes account for approximately 30 percent of the variance in PTSD risk, with the remaining vulnerability explained by unique environmental experiences, consistent with PTSD being moderately heritable rather than purely genetic or purely environmental.","boardPrep":"Step 1: know the approximate 30% heritability figure for PTSD as a moderately-heritable disorder. COMLEX: reinforces the gene-environment interaction framework of PTSD risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 6 — treatment failure analysis and biological risk factors","isHighYield":true}],"24":[{"id":"sdl24_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate brief psychotic disorder, schizophreniform disorder, schizophrenia, schizoaffective disorder, delusional disorder, mood disorder with psychotic features, and substance-induced psychosis by timeline and associated mood symptoms.","batch":1,"stem":"A 24-year-old has delusions and disorganized speech that began abruptly 2 weeks ago after a sudden job loss. She has never had psychotic symptoms before and has no mood symptoms. Which diagnosis best fits, assuming full resolution within the next 2 weeks?","choices":{"A":"Schizophreniform disorder","B":"Schizophrenia","C":"Schizoaffective disorder","D":"Brief psychotic disorder","E":"Delusional disorder"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Brief psychotic disorder involves sudden-onset psychotic symptoms lasting 1 day to less than 1 month with full return to baseline, often following a major stressor. Schizophreniform requires 1-6 months of symptoms, schizophrenia requires 6+ months, schizoaffective requires a mood-disorder overlap, and delusional disorder requires isolated non-bizarre delusions without disorganized speech.","boardPrep":"Step 1: brief psychotic disorder = <1 month + full remission + often stress-triggered. COMLEX: named in the Schizophrenia spectrum Clinical Presentation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 1","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate brief psychotic disorder, schizophreniform disorder, schizophrenia, schizoaffective disorder, delusional disorder, mood disorder with psychotic features, and substance-induced psychosis by timeline and associated mood symptoms.","batch":1,"stem":"A 29-year-old man has had delusions, hallucinations, and disorganized speech for 4 months without any mood episode. Which diagnosis best fits this duration?","choices":{"A":"Brief psychotic disorder","B":"Schizophreniform disorder","C":"A primary schizophrenia diagnosis","D":"Schizoaffective disorder","E":"Major depressive disorder with psychotic features"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Schizophreniform disorder requires symptoms lasting at least 1 month but less than 6 months, distinguishing it from schizophrenia (6+ months) and brief psychotic disorder (under 1 month). The absence of mood symptoms rules out schizoaffective disorder and MDD with psychotic features.","boardPrep":"Step 1: schizophreniform = 1-6 months, otherwise the same symptom picture as schizophrenia. COMLEX: named in the Schizophrenia spectrum Clinical Presentation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 1","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate brief psychotic disorder, schizophreniform disorder, schizophrenia, schizoaffective disorder, delusional disorder, mood disorder with psychotic features, and substance-induced psychosis by timeline and associated mood symptoms.","batch":1,"stem":"A 35-year-old has had persistent delusions of persecution for 8 months without hallucinations, disorganized speech, or functional impairment outside the delusion's direct impact. Which diagnosis best fits?","choices":{"A":"Schizophrenia","B":"Schizophreniform disorder","C":"Delusional disorder","D":"Brief psychotic disorder","E":"Schizoaffective disorder"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Delusional disorder is characterized by one or more non-bizarre delusions without the prominent hallucinations, disorganized speech, or broad functional impairment seen in schizophrenia, and functioning outside the delusion's direct effects remains relatively preserved.","boardPrep":"Step 1: delusional disorder = isolated delusion(s), preserved functioning, mental status otherwise unremarkable. COMLEX: named in the Schizophrenia spectrum Clinical Presentation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 1","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply positive, negative, cognitive, disorganized, and affective symptom domains to diagnose schizophrenia-spectrum presentations.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with schizophrenia shows flattened affect, alogia, and social withdrawal. Which symptom domain do these represent?","choices":{"A":"Positive symptoms","B":"Cognitive symptoms","C":"Disorganized symptoms","D":"Affective symptoms","E":"Negative symptoms"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Flattened affect, alogia (poverty of speech), and social withdrawal are defined by the absence of normal behaviors and are classified as negative symptoms, distinct from positive symptoms (added abnormal behaviors like hallucinations/delusions).","boardPrep":"Step 1: negative symptoms = absence of normal function (flat affect, avolition, alogia, anhedonia, asociality). COMLEX: negative symptoms are explicitly named in the DSM-5 schizophrenia criteria table.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply positive, negative, cognitive, disorganized, and affective symptom domains to diagnose schizophrenia-spectrum presentations.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with schizophrenia reports hearing voices commenting on his actions and believes his neighbors are monitoring him through the television. Which symptom domain do these represent?","choices":{"A":"Positive symptoms","B":"Negative symptoms","C":"Cognitive symptoms","D":"Affective symptoms","E":"Disorganized symptoms"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hallucinations and delusions are abnormal behaviors that are present and observable — classic positive symptoms — distinct from negative symptoms, which are defined by absence of normal function.","boardPrep":"Step 1: positive symptoms = added abnormal experiences (hallucinations, delusions). COMLEX: positive symptoms map directly onto the \"hallucinations\" and \"delusions\" criteria in the DSM-5 table.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply positive, negative, cognitive, disorganized, and affective symptom domains to diagnose schizophrenia-spectrum presentations.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with schizophrenia has impaired working memory, poor attention, and difficulty with concept formation, in the absence of formal thought disorder. Which symptom domain do these represent?","choices":{"A":"Positive symptoms","B":"Negative symptoms","C":"Cognitive symptoms","D":"Disorganized symptoms","E":"Affective symptoms"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Impairments in attention, working memory, and concept formation reflect the cognitive symptom domain of schizophrenia, which is distinct from disorganized symptoms (formal thought disorder in speech/behavior) and can be present even when positive and negative symptoms are well controlled.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cognitive symptoms (attention, memory, executive function) often predict functional outcome better than positive symptoms. COMLEX: cognitive dysfunction is explicitly listed in the ICD-10 schizophrenia symptom table.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze dopamine pathway involvement, including mesolimbic, mesocortical, nigrostriatal, and tuberoinfundibular circuits, in psychosis and antipsychotic effects.","batch":1,"stem":"Historical models of schizophrenia attributed the positive symptoms (hallucinations, delusions) to dysfunction in which dopamine pathway?","choices":{"A":"Mesocortical pathway","B":"Nigrostriatal pathway","C":"Tuberoinfundibular pathway","D":"Mesolimbic pathway","E":"Corticospinal pathway"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The mesolimbic pathway, projecting from the ventral tegmental area to the nucleus accumbens, was historically implicated in the positive symptoms of schizophrenia, supported by observations that amphetamine-induced psychosis affects this region and antipsychotics injected there reverse the effect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: mesolimbic hyperactivity -> positive symptoms is a classic dopamine-pathway pairing. COMLEX: reinforces mechanism-based understanding of antipsychotic drug targets.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 2","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze dopamine pathway involvement, including mesolimbic, mesocortical, nigrostriatal, and tuberoinfundibular circuits, in psychosis and antipsychotic effects.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient started on an antipsychotic develops galactorrhea and irregular menses. Blockade of dopamine in which pathway most directly explains this adverse effect?","choices":{"A":"The mesolimbic pathway","B":"The mesocortical pathway","C":"The nigrostriatal pathway","D":"The corticospinal pathway","E":"The tuberoinfundibular pathway"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Dopamine normally inhibits prolactin release via the tuberoinfundibular pathway; D2 blockade there by antipsychotics elevates prolactin, producing galactorrhea and menstrual irregularities, distinct from the other three pathways' effects (mood/positive symptoms, negative/cognitive symptoms, and motor control, respectively).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tuberoinfundibular blockade -> hyperprolactinemia -> galactorrhea/amenorrhea, a classic antipsychotic adverse-effect vignette. COMLEX: reinforces the four-dopamine-pathway framework for antipsychotic side effects.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 2","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze dopamine pathway involvement, including mesolimbic, mesocortical, nigrostriatal, and tuberoinfundibular circuits, in psychosis and antipsychotic effects.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient on a high-potency first-generation antipsychotic develops rigidity, bradykinesia, and a resting tremor. Blockade of dopamine in which pathway most directly explains these symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Nigrostriatal pathway","B":"Mesolimbic pathway","C":"Mesocortical pathway","D":"Tuberoinfundibular pathway","E":"Corticospinal pathway"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The nigrostriatal pathway, projecting from the substantia nigra to the striatum, normally facilitates smooth motor control; D2 blockade there produces parkinsonian extrapyramidal symptoms (rigidity, bradykinesia, tremor), distinct from the limbic, cortical, and hypothalamic-pituitary pathways.","boardPrep":"Step 1: nigrostriatal blockade -> extrapyramidal symptoms/parkinsonism, a classic antipsychotic side-effect pairing. COMLEX: mirrors the localizing neurologic-sign content associated with schizophrenia and its treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 2","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Interpret medical, neurologic, endocrine, autoimmune, medication-induced, and substance-related causes that can masquerade as primary psychosis.","batch":1,"stem":"A 60-year-old with new-onset delusions and hallucinations is found to have profound hyponatremia and hypercalcemia. Which category of secondary psychosis does this best represent?","choices":{"A":"Neurodegenerative disorder","B":"Infectious disease","C":"Metabolic disorder","D":"Endocrinopathy","E":"Vitamin deficiency"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hyponatremia and hypercalcemia are metabolic derangements that can directly induce psychotic symptoms, distinct from neurodegenerative, infectious, endocrine, and vitamin-deficiency causes, each of which has its own characteristic secondary-psychosis etiologies.","boardPrep":"Step 1: always screen basic metabolic panel and calcium before anchoring on primary psychosis in new-onset psychotic symptoms, especially in older adults. COMLEX: reflects the medical-workup-first principle for secondary psychotic disorders.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 2","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Interpret medical, neurologic, endocrine, autoimmune, medication-induced, and substance-related causes that can masquerade as primary psychosis.","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old with new hallucinations and catatonic symptoms is also found to have anti-NMDA receptor antibodies. Which category of secondary psychosis does this best represent?","choices":{"A":"Vascular disease","B":"Autoimmune encephalitis","C":"Vitamin deficiency","D":"Neurodegenerative disorder","E":"Substance-induced psychosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis is a classic autoimmune cause of new-onset psychosis and catatonia that must be excluded before diagnosing a primary psychotic disorder, distinct from vascular, nutritional, neurodegenerative, or substance-related causes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis is a named, treatable autoimmune mimic of primary psychosis, especially with catatonic features. COMLEX: reinforces serologic testing to rule out organic causes before a schizophrenia-spectrum diagnosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 2","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Interpret medical, neurologic, endocrine, autoimmune, medication-induced, and substance-related causes that can masquerade as primary psychosis.","batch":1,"stem":"A 50-year-old with a history of complex partial seizures develops new-onset delusions and hallucinations between seizure episodes. Which category of secondary psychosis does this best represent?","choices":{"A":"A CNS or seizure disorder","B":"A metabolic disorder","C":"An endocrinopathy","D":"An infectious disease","E":"A vitamin deficiency"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Complex partial (temporal lobe) epilepsy is a recognized central nervous system cause of secondary psychotic symptoms, distinct from metabolic, endocrine, infectious, and nutritional causes of psychosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: temporal lobe epilepsy is a classic organic mimic of primary psychosis, especially with interictal psychotic symptoms. COMLEX: reinforces neurologic causes in the secondary-psychosis differential.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 -> Obj 2","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare first-episode psychosis, chronic psychosis, catatonia, and psychotic agitation by acuity, safety risk, and initial workup.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient sits immobile and mute, exhibits waxy flexibility, and shows automatic obedience to passive movement of his limbs. Which condition does this best represent?","choices":{"A":"Chronic psychosis","B":"Catatonia","C":"First-episode psychosis","D":"Psychotic agitation","E":"Negative symptom predominance"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Immobility, mutism, waxy flexibility, and automatic obedience are classic features of catatonia, a distinct clinical syndrome that can occur across multiple psychiatric and medical conditions, distinguishing it from chronic psychosis, first-episode psychosis, and agitation, which present very differently. Negative symptoms are deficits defined by absence (flat affect, alogia, avolition), not the waxy flexibility and automatic obedience of catatonia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: waxy flexibility and automatic obedience are pathognomonic catatonia signs. COMLEX: catatonia is a named specifier applicable across schizophrenia-spectrum and mood disorders.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 5","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare first-episode psychosis, chronic psychosis, catatonia, and psychotic agitation by acuity, safety risk, and initial workup.","batch":1,"stem":"A newly psychotic patient in the emergency department is combative, shouting, and unable to be redirected verbally. What is the most appropriate immediate management priority?","choices":{"A":"Extensive neuropsychological testing","B":"Deferring treatment for a full history","C":"Immediate discharge with follow-up","D":"Prolonged exposure psychotherapy","E":"Rapid pharmacologic calming"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acute psychotic agitation requires rapid, safety-focused pharmacologic intervention — intramuscular antipsychotics or benzodiazepines like lorazepam — before extensive testing, history-taking, or psychotherapy can safely proceed. Discharge would leave an acutely agitated psychotic patient untreated; antipsychotics or benzodiazepines, intramuscular when highly agitated, give rapid calming.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute agitation management is a high-yield patient-safety topic — IM antipsychotic or benzodiazepine first. COMLEX: reflects acuity-based triage principles across psychiatric emergencies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 5","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare first-episode psychosis, chronic psychosis, catatonia, and psychotic agitation by acuity, safety risk, and initial workup.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient is experiencing his first lifetime episode of psychosis and responds well to antipsychotic treatment. How long should maintenance treatment generally continue after this first episode?","choices":{"A":"Until symptoms resolve, then stop","B":"A single dose is generally sufficient","C":"Indefinitely, same as 2+ episodes","D":"At least 1 year of treatment","E":"No more than 1 month of treatment"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Guidelines recommend maintaining antipsychotic treatment for at least 1 year after a first psychotic episode, given a substantial relapse risk over the following years, whereas indefinite treatment is generally reserved for patients with 2 or more episodes. The Kaplan psychopharmacology chapter (ch. 21, SDL 26–27 reading) gives a slightly different schedule: maintenance usually 1 to 2 years after a first episode, often 5 years after a second, and lifetime after a third.","boardPrep":"Step 1: first episode = at least 1 year maintenance; 2+ episodes = consider indefinite treatment. COMLEX: reflects evidence-based relapse-prevention duration in first-episode psychosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 5","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate brief psychotic disorder, schizophreniform disorder, schizophrenia, schizoaffective disorder, delusional disorder, mood disorder with psychotic features, and substance-induced psychosis by timeline and associated mood symptoms.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old presents with 6 weeks of delusions of persecution and disorganized speech, with no depressive or manic symptoms at any point. She has never had psychotic symptoms before. Her symptoms have not yet resolved. Which of the following best explains her presentation?","choices":{"A":"Schizophreniform disorder, given ongoing symptoms past 1 month","B":"Brief psychotic disorder, since her symptoms began abruptly","C":"Schizophrenia, since psychotic symptoms are present","D":"Schizoaffective disorder, since her mood is notably disturbed","E":"Psychotic depression, given her distress"},"correct":"A","explanation":"At 6 weeks with ongoing symptoms she has already passed the 1-month limit for brief psychotic disorder and falls in schizophreniform territory (1 to 6 months); she cannot yet be diagnosed with schizophrenia (requires 6+ months) or schizoaffective/MDD with psychotic features (both require a mood episode, which she lacks).","boardPrep":"Step 1: track duration continuously — the same symptom picture receives a different diagnosis purely based on how long it has persisted. COMLEX: schizophreniform disorder is a named Schizophrenia spectrum Clinical Presentation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 -> Obj 1","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate brief psychotic disorder, schizophreniform disorder, schizophrenia, schizoaffective disorder, delusional disorder, mood disorder with psychotic features, and substance-induced psychosis by timeline and associated mood symptoms.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old has had persistent delusions and hallucinations for 10 months. Throughout this time, she has also had a major depressive episode lasting the majority of that period, with at least a 2-week window where psychotic symptoms persisted in the complete absence of the depressive episode. Which of the following best explains her presentation?","choices":{"A":"Major depressive disorder with psychotic features, since depression has been present most of the time","B":"Schizophrenia, since psychotic symptoms by themselves have lasted over 6 months","C":"Schizoaffective disorder, given psychosis independent of her mood episode","D":"Schizophreniform disorder, since her total illness duration exceeds 1 month","E":"Brief psychotic disorder, since psychotic symptoms are prominent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Schizoaffective disorder requires psychotic symptoms present without a mood episode for at least 2 weeks (satisfied here) combined with mood symptoms present for the majority of the total illness duration (also satisfied here) — a combination that MDD with psychotic features (psychosis only during mood episodes) and schizophrenia (no significant mood component) cannot account for.","boardPrep":"Step 1: schizoaffective disorder's two-part definition (2 weeks psychosis-without-mood + majority-of-illness mood-with-psychosis) is a frequently tested diagnostic trap. COMLEX: named Schizophrenia spectrum Clinical Presentation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 -> Obj 2","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate brief psychotic disorder, schizophreniform disorder, schizophrenia, schizoaffective disorder, delusional disorder, mood disorder with psychotic features, and substance-induced psychosis by timeline and associated mood symptoms.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old has believed for 7 months that his coworkers are secretly plotting to have him fired, monitoring his emails, and following his car. He has no hallucinations, no disorganized speech, and continues to function well at work and home apart from his suspicion of coworkers. Which of the following best explains his presentation?","choices":{"A":"Schizophrenia, given the 6+ month duration of symptoms","B":"Schizophreniform disorder, given his functional preservation","C":"Brief psychotic disorder, given the plausibility of his belief","D":"Substance-induced psychotic disorder, given his social withdrawal","E":"Delusional disorder, given an isolated, non-bizarre delusion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"His presentation — an isolated, plausible (non-bizarre) persecutory delusion without hallucinations, disorganized speech, or broad functional impairment — fits delusional disorder rather than schizophrenia, which requires broader symptom criteria and typically more functional impairment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: delusional disorder = plausible, isolated delusion + preserved functioning outside the delusion's direct effects. COMLEX: named Schizophrenia spectrum Clinical Presentation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 -> Obj 1","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply positive, negative, cognitive, disorganized, and affective symptom domains to diagnose schizophrenia-spectrum presentations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old with schizophrenia speaks in a way that jumps between unrelated topics with no logical connection, and her speech is difficult to follow despite normal vocabulary. Which symptom domain does this best represent?","choices":{"A":"Disorganized symptoms, reflecting her formal thought disorder","B":"Positive symptoms, reflecting her hallucinations","C":"Negative symptoms, reflecting her alogia","D":"Cognitive symptoms, reflecting her impaired attention","E":"Affective symptoms, reflecting her blunted mood"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Loose associations and incoherent, tangential speech reflect a formal thought disorder — the disorganized symptom domain — distinct from positive symptoms (hallucinations/delusions), negative symptoms (poverty of speech content, not disorganized content), and cognitive or affective symptoms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: distinguish disorganized speech (a formal thought disorder) from alogia (poverty of speech, a negative symptom) — a classic distractor pairing. COMLEX: disorganization of speech is explicitly named in the DSM-5 schizophrenia criteria.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply positive, negative, cognitive, disorganized, and affective symptom domains to diagnose schizophrenia-spectrum presentations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old with schizophrenia is euthymic-appearing but describes feeling profound anhedonia and reduced emotional responsiveness that has persisted for years, independent of any antipsychotic-related sedation. Which symptom domain does this best represent?","choices":{"A":"Positive symptoms, reflecting delusional mood","B":"Disorganized symptoms, reflecting inappropriate affect","C":"Cognitive symptoms, reflecting impaired concept formation","D":"A medication adverse effect, reflecting parkinsonian blunting","E":"Negative symptoms, reflecting affective flattening"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Persistent anhedonia and reduced emotional responsiveness independent of medication effects reflect the negative symptom domain, distinguishing it from a medication-induced parkinsonian blunted affect, which the stem explicitly excludes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: differentiating illness-related negative symptoms from medication-induced blunting is a classic clinical challenge highlighted on boards. COMLEX: reduced emotional responsiveness is explicitly named among schizophrenia's affective symptoms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 22 -> Obj 1","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply positive, negative, cognitive, disorganized, and affective symptom domains to diagnose schizophrenia-spectrum presentations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man was hospitalized 2 months ago for his first psychotic episode; his hallucinations and delusions resolved on risperidone. He now reports 3 weeks of sad mood, guilt about how he behaved while ill, hopelessness about his future, and passive thoughts of death. He has no rigidity, tremor, or restlessness, and he still enjoys seeing friends when he can. Which symptom domain best explains his current presentation?","choices":{"A":"Negative symptoms, reflecting primary avolition and asociality","B":"Affective symptoms, as a depression following the psychotic episode","C":"Medication effect, reflecting drug-induced akinesia that mimics low mood","D":"Cognitive symptoms, reflecting slowed processing and poor attention","E":"Positive symptoms, reflecting a relapse of his earlier psychosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Kaplan and Sadock describe affective symptoms as a domain of schizophrenia and note that a clinically observable postpsychotic depression sometimes follows a psychotic episode; his sad mood, guilt, hopelessness, and death wishes after his first episode resolved fit this, and it needs active assessment because suicide is the single leading cause of premature death in schizophrenia. Negative symptoms such as avolition and asociality reduce drive and social engagement without prominent sadness, guilt, or hopelessness, and he still enjoys his friends. Drug-induced akinesia or parkinsonism would bring rigidity, tremor, or slowing, which he lacks. Cognitive symptoms involve working memory, attention, and concept formation, not mood. Positive symptoms are hallucinations and delusions, which have resolved.","boardPrep":"Step 1: schizophrenia symptom domains are positive, negative, cognitive, disorganized, and affective; postpsychotic depression and suicide risk belong to the affective domain. COMLEX: screen every patient recovering from a psychotic episode for depression and suicidal thinking, and separate true depression from negative symptoms and antipsychotic-induced parkinsonism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — managing suicidal risk and negative symptoms in schizophrenia-spectrum disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze dopamine pathway involvement, including mesolimbic, mesocortical, nigrostriatal, and tuberoinfundibular circuits, in psychosis and antipsychotic effects.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with schizophrenia is started on an antipsychotic and, within a week, notices that his auditory hallucinations have significantly diminished. Which dopamine pathway's blockade most directly explains this therapeutic effect?","choices":{"A":"The mesolimbic pathway, whose D2 blockade reduces positive symptoms","B":"The mesocortical pathway, whose D2 blockade reduces negative symptoms","C":"The nigrostriatal pathway, whose D2 blockade produces parkinsonism","D":"The tuberoinfundibular pathway, whose D2 blockade raises prolactin","E":"The corticospinal pathway, whose D2 blockade has no clinical antipsychotic role"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Antipsychotic blockade of D2 receptors in the mesolimbic pathway is responsible for the reduction of positive symptoms like hallucinations, while blockade in the other three pathways instead accounts for negative-symptom effects, extrapyramidal side effects, and hyperprolactinemia, respectively.","boardPrep":"Step 1: match therapeutic and adverse antipsychotic effects to their specific dopamine pathway — a frequently tested mechanism-to-effect mapping. COMLEX: reinforces the four-pathway framework as it applies to both efficacy and side effects.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 2 — GABA-A receptor pharmacology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze dopamine pathway involvement, including mesolimbic, mesocortical, nigrostriatal, and tuberoinfundibular circuits, in psychosis and antipsychotic effects.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient started on a high-potency first-generation antipsychotic 3 weeks ago now has cogwheel rigidity and a resting tremor. Which dopamine pathway's blockade most directly explains this adverse effect, and why does it occur alongside limited improvement in his social withdrawal?","choices":{"A":"Mesolimbic blockade causes the motor symptoms, while tuberoinfundibular blockade limits negative-symptom benefit","B":"Tuberoinfundibular blockade causes the motor symptoms, while nigrostriatal blockade limits negative-symptom benefit","C":"Mesocortical blockade causes the motor symptoms, while mesolimbic blockade limits negative-symptom benefit","D":"Nigrostriatal blockade causes the motor symptoms, while mesocortical blockade limits negative-symptom benefit","E":"All four dopamine pathways are blocked equally, explaining both findings identically"},"correct":"D","explanation":"High-potency first-generation antipsychotics strongly block D2 receptors in the nigrostriatal pathway, producing extrapyramidal symptoms like rigidity and tremor, while his negative symptoms reflect mesocortical dopamine hypoactivity, which D2 blockade does not correct (and can worsen), so social withdrawal persists despite motor side effects. Mesolimbic and mesocortical blockade do not produce parkinsonism, and tuberoinfundibular blockade raises prolactin rather than causing rigidity or tremor. The four dopamine pathways are not blocked equally, which is why these drugs separate motor, endocrine, and symptom effects.","boardPrep":"Step 1: first-generation antipsychotics disproportionately affect the nigrostriatal pathway relative to the mesocortical pathway, explaining their EPS-heavy, negative-symptom-limited side-effect profile. COMLEX: reinforces pathway-specific drug effects relevant to antipsychotic selection.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 2 — GABA-A receptor pharmacology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze dopamine pathway involvement, including mesolimbic, mesocortical, nigrostriatal, and tuberoinfundibular circuits, in psychosis and antipsychotic effects.","batch":2,"stem":"A researcher proposes that dorsal striatal dysfunction, rather than purely mesolimbic dysfunction, better explains the associative and habitual-thinking deficits seen in schizophrenia. Which observation most directly supports this revised model?","choices":{"A":"Amphetamine-induced psychosis arises from cortical glutamate release without dopamine change","B":"Neuroimaging shows the largest dopamine differences in the dorsal striatum","C":"The dorsal striatum has no known role in habit formation or integrative processing","D":"Antipsychotics show no measurable effect on dorsal striatal dopamine activity","E":"The mesolimbic pathway has been definitively ruled out in all models of psychosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Functional neuroimaging studies finding the most significant dopamine differences in the dorsal striatum and its projections — present even in those at high risk for schizophrenia — support a revised model implicating this integrative, habit-formation-related region beyond the classic mesolimbic-only hypothesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: know that current dopamine-hypothesis models have expanded beyond the mesolimbic pathway to include dorsal striatal dysfunction. COMLEX: reflects updated neurobiological models of schizophrenia pathophysiology.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 2 — GABA-A receptor pharmacology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Interpret medical, neurologic, endocrine, autoimmune, medication-induced, and substance-related causes that can masquerade as primary psychosis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old with no psychiatric history develops new visual hallucinations, fluctuating attention, and mild parkinsonian rigidity over several weeks. Laboratory workup is unremarkable. Which of the following is most important to evaluate before diagnosing a primary psychotic disorder?","choices":{"A":"A vitamin B12 deficiency, given his age and presentation","B":"A primary mood disorder, given his age and presentation","C":"Cannabis intoxication, given his age and presentation","D":"A primary anxiety disorder, given his age and presentation","E":"A neurodegenerative process, given his age and presentation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The combination of visual hallucinations, fluctuating cognition, and parkinsonism in an older adult is classic for a neurodegenerative process such as dementia with Lewy bodies, which must be evaluated before attributing new-onset psychosis in this age group to a primary psychotic disorder — visual hallucinations especially should raise suspicion for an organic or neurodegenerative cause rather than schizophrenia, which rarely has its first onset this late in life. Vitamin deficiency and cannabis are on the course's list of secondary causes, but neither explains the visual hallucinations, fluctuating attention and parkinsonism that mark dementia with Lewy bodies, and his labs are unremarkable.","boardPrep":"Step 1: visual hallucinations + parkinsonism + fluctuating cognition in an older adult should trigger a neurodegenerative workup, not a schizophrenia diagnosis. COMLEX: reinforces age-atypical-onset psychosis as a red flag for secondary causes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — secondary organic mimics of primary psychiatric presentations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Interpret medical, neurologic, endocrine, autoimmune, medication-induced, and substance-related causes that can masquerade as primary psychosis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old college student is brought in with new paranoid delusions, tachycardia, and dilated pupils after a weekend party. Symptoms began within hours and are accompanied by significant agitation. Which category of secondary psychosis is most likely?","choices":{"A":"A primary metabolic disorder, given his rapid onset","B":"A neurodegenerative disorder, given his rapid onset","C":"Substance-induced etiology, given his autonomic findings","D":"An endocrinopathy, given his autonomic findings","E":"A vitamin deficiency, given his rapid onset"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Rapid-onset psychosis with tachycardia, mydriasis, and agitation in a young adult after a social gathering is classic for stimulant (e.g., amphetamine or cocaine) intoxication, making substance-induced psychotic disorder far more likely than a metabolic, neurodegenerative, endocrine, or nutritional cause given this acute autonomic picture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute autonomic hyperactivity + rapid-onset psychosis in a young adult = screen for stimulant intoxication first. COMLEX: substances are explicitly named among the secondary causes of psychotic and delusional syndromes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — secondary organic mimics of primary psychiatric presentations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Interpret medical, neurologic, endocrine, autoimmune, medication-induced, and substance-related causes that can masquerade as primary psychosis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old on long-term corticosteroid therapy for an autoimmune condition develops new grandiose delusions and pressured speech over several days. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial step?","choices":{"A":"Start an antipsychotic without addressing the corticosteroid regimen","B":"Diagnose bipolar disorder with psychotic features and begin lithium","C":"Diagnose schizophrenia and begin long-term antipsychotic maintenance","D":"Evaluate for corticosteroid-induced psychosis first","E":"Diagnose delusional disorder and begin outpatient psychotherapy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Corticosteroids are a recognized medication cause of secondary psychosis and mood symptoms (including mania-like presentations), and this medication-induced etiology must be evaluated and addressed before assigning a primary psychiatric diagnosis in a patient on long-term steroid therapy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: corticosteroids are explicitly named among medication causes of secondary psychotic and delusional syndromes. COMLEX: reinforces medication review as a mandatory step before a new psychiatric diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — secondary organic mimics of primary psychiatric presentations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare first-episode psychosis, chronic psychosis, catatonia, and psychotic agitation by acuity, safety risk, and initial workup.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old with a 5-year history of schizophrenia and multiple prior hospitalizations presents to the emergency department shouting and attempting to strike staff. He has been off his antipsychotic for 2 weeks. Which of the following is the most appropriate immediate step, and how does his subsequent maintenance plan differ from a first-episode patient?","choices":{"A":"Rapid pharmacologic calming now, followed by 1 year of maintenance treatment as in a first episode","B":"Extensive psychotherapy now, deferring any medication until he is calm","C":"Rapid pharmacologic calming now, then consider indefinite maintenance","D":"Immediate discharge now, with medication restarted if he relapses again","E":"Neuropsychological testing now, deferring any acute intervention"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Acute psychotic agitation requires immediate rapid pharmacologic calming (antipsychotic and/or benzodiazepine) regardless of episode history; however, because he has had multiple episodes, his subsequent maintenance plan differs from a first-episode patient (who typically needs only about 1 year) — most experts recommend considering indefinite maintenance treatment after 2 or more episodes. The Kaplan psychopharmacology chapter (ch. 21, SDL 26–27 reading) gives a slightly different schedule: maintenance usually 1 to 2 years after a first episode, often 5 years after a second, and lifetime after a third.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute management (calm first) is the same across episode number, but maintenance duration recommendations differ by episode count — a frequently tested two-part reasoning question. COMLEX: reflects acuity-based triage plus longitudinal relapse-prevention planning.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 5 — suicide and violence risk factor interpretation and safety planning","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare first-episode psychosis, chronic psychosis, catatonia, and psychotic agitation by acuity, safety risk, and initial workup.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old with chronic schizophrenia and prominent negative symptoms is brought in by family for evaluation. On exam, he sits immobile, does not speak, and exhibits waxy flexibility when his arm is repositioned. Vital signs are stable. Which of the following best describes his presentation and the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Simple negative-symptom exacerbation, requiring antipsychotic dose adjustment","B":"Catatonia, warranting prompt catatonia-specific treatment","C":"Acute psychotic agitation, requiring immediate physical restraint","D":"A normal chronic-schizophrenia baseline, requiring no additional evaluation","E":"Medication nonadherence by itself, requiring outpatient follow-up"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Immobility, mutism, and waxy flexibility are specific catatonia signs that can be superimposed on chronic schizophrenia and are distinct from simple negative-symptom worsening; catatonia requires prompt recognition and specific treatment (e.g., benzodiazepines, ECT), which differs substantially from simply adjusting antipsychotic dosing or managing agitation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: catatonia can be superimposed on any psychiatric or medical condition and always requires specific catatonia-targeted evaluation, not just antipsychotic titration. COMLEX: catatonia is a named cross-cutting specifier in the schizophrenia spectrum.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 5 — suicide and violence risk factor interpretation and safety planning","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl24_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare first-episode psychosis, chronic psychosis, catatonia, and psychotic agitation by acuity, safety risk, and initial workup.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old man with chronic schizophrenia has been stable for 2 years on daily risperidone after three earlier hospitalizations. He feels well and asks what would happen to his risk of psychotic relapse over the next year if he stopped his medication. Based on reported relapse data, which estimate is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"About 2 to 5 percent on medication and 25 to 30 percent off it","B":"About 30 to 40 percent on medication and 45 to 50 percent off it","C":"About 8 to 10 percent on medication and 90 to 95 percent off it","D":"About 16 to 23 percent on medication and 53 to 72 percent off it","E":"About 45 to 50 percent on medication and 75 to 80 percent off it"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Kaplan and Sadock report that stable patients maintained on an antipsychotic have a much lower relapse rate than those whose medication is stopped: 16 to 23 percent of treated patients relapse within 1 year, compared with 53 to 72 percent without medication. Rates of 2 to 5 percent on treatment understate relapse, since even adherent patients relapse at meaningful rates. Rates of 30 to 40 percent on treatment against 45 to 50 percent off it understate the benefit of maintenance. A 90 to 95 percent relapse rate off medication overstates the reported range. Rates of 45 to 50 percent on medication overstate relapse during maintenance and would make continued treatment look far less useful than it is.","boardPrep":"Step 1: maintenance antipsychotic treatment roughly cuts 1-year relapse risk by two thirds; first-episode patients should be maintained at least 1 year, and multiple-episode patients longer, often indefinitely. COMLEX: when a stable patient asks to stop an antipsychotic, give relapse figures, review long-acting injectable options, and plan close follow-up rather than abrupt discontinuation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 6 — long-acting injectable formulations and adherence in chronic schizophrenia","isHighYield":true}],"25":[{"id":"sdl25_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply evidence-based treatment principles for schizophrenia-spectrum disorders, including antipsychotic initiation, adherence planning, relapse prevention, and psychosocial supports.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with acute schizophrenia is started on an antipsychotic. Approximately what proportion of such patients will achieve complete remission or only mild residual symptoms?","choices":{"A":"About 10 percent","B":"About 30 percent","C":"About 90 percent","D":"About 60 percent","E":"Nearly 100 percent"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Approximately 60 percent of patients with acute schizophrenia will achieve complete remission or only mild symptoms on antipsychotic treatment, while the remaining 40 percent continue to show variable, treatment-resistant positive symptoms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: know the roughly 60/40 responder split for acute antipsychotic treatment. COMLEX: reinforces realistic expectation-setting for pharmacotherapy outcomes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 5","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply evidence-based treatment principles for schizophrenia-spectrum disorders, including antipsychotic initiation, adherence planning, relapse prevention, and psychosocial supports.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient has been on an adequate dose of an antipsychotic for 2 weeks with no improvement at all. What is the most appropriate interpretation regarding continuing this specific drug?","choices":{"A":"This drug should continue, since a 6-month trial is always required","B":"ECT should start immediately, since medication trials no longer matter","C":"The dose should double indefinitely, since higher doses guarantee response","D":"A benzodiazepine should be added, since it boosts this drug's efficacy","E":"This drug is less likely to help, given no response at all by week 2"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The course reading (Kaplan & Sadock Ch 5, PDF p68) reports a meta-analysis in which patients who did not show at least some response by the second week of an adequate antipsychotic trial were less likely to benefit from that drug, so a change of agent may be the best option. The same chapter (PDF p69) calls a 4- to 6-week trial at an adequate dose a reasonable trial before calling a patient a poor responder, and notes that patients with even mild improvement in that period may keep improving for 3 to 6 months. The early signal therefore applies to a complete absence of response, as in this patient; partial responders are judged over the full 4- to 6-week trial. No course source requires a 6-month trial, abandons medication for immediate ECT, promises response from indefinite dose escalation, or says a benzodiazepine boosts the antipsychotic's efficacy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kaplan & Sadock Ch 5 — no response at all by week 2 of an adequate antipsychotic trial predicts less benefit from that drug (meta-analysis), while a 4- to 6-week trial at an adequate dose is the reasonable trial before calling a patient a poor responder. COMLEX: Patients with even mild early improvement may keep improving for 3 to 6 months, so judge complete non-response early and partial response over the full trial.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 5","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply evidence-based treatment principles for schizophrenia-spectrum disorders, including antipsychotic initiation, adherence planning, relapse prevention, and psychosocial supports.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with schizophrenia has a history of repeatedly discontinuing daily oral antipsychotics. Which treatment strategy most directly addresses this adherence pattern?","choices":{"A":"A long-acting injectable antipsychotic","B":"A higher dose of the same oral formulation","C":"Switching to as-needed dosing","D":"Discontinuing all pharmacotherapy","E":"Adding a second oral antipsychotic"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Long-acting injectable antipsychotics were specifically developed to improve adherence in patients likely to discontinue daily oral medications, offer less day-to-day blood-level variability, and let clinicians quickly recognize nonadherence. As-needed dosing still depends on the patient taking oral tablets, the very step he skips.","boardPrep":"Step 1: long-acting injectables directly target the adherence problem, not efficacy or dosing per se. COMLEX: reinforces adherence-focused treatment planning.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 5","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze treatment resistance, nonadherence, substance use, homelessness, stigma, cognitive impairment, and limited insight as barriers to effective care.","batch":1,"stem":"What proportion of homeless Americans have a diagnosis of schizophrenia?","choices":{"A":"Less than 1 percent","B":"About 5 percent","C":"About 80 percent","D":"About 15 to 45 percent","E":"Nearly 100 percent"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Patients with a diagnosis of schizophrenia account for 15 to 45 percent of homeless Americans, reflecting the disorder's substantial impact on housing stability and functional independence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: know the striking overrepresentation of schizophrenia among homeless populations. COMLEX: reinforces homelessness as a named social determinant/barrier to care.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 1","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze treatment resistance, nonadherence, substance use, homelessness, stigma, cognitive impairment, and limited insight as barriers to effective care.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with schizophrenia insists nothing is wrong with him despite ongoing hallucinations and repeatedly refuses treatment. Which barrier to care does this best represent?","choices":{"A":"Stigma toward mental illness","B":"Limited insight into illness","C":"Unstable housing status","D":"Active substance use","E":"Isolated cognitive impairment"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Poor awareness that one's experiences and behavior are pathological — limited insight — is classically associated with schizophrenia and directly drives treatment refusal, distinct from stigma (external judgment), homelessness, or substance use. Cognitive impairment in schizophrenia affects attention, executive function and memory, but the barrier here is his conviction that nothing is wrong, which is poor insight.","boardPrep":"Step 1: limited insight is a core, classically described barrier to schizophrenia treatment engagement. COMLEX: distinguishes insight from cognitive impairment as related but separate barriers.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 1","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze treatment resistance, nonadherence, substance use, homelessness, stigma, cognitive impairment, and limited insight as barriers to effective care.","batch":1,"stem":"An estimated 40 to 50 percent of patients with schizophrenia become noncompliant with long-term antipsychotic treatment within what timeframe?","choices":{"A":"1 to 2 weeks","B":"1 to 2 months","C":"1 to 2 years","D":"5 to 10 years","E":"3 to 6 months"},"correct":"C","explanation":"An estimated 40 to 50 percent of patients become noncompliant with long-term antipsychotic treatment within 1 to 2 years (not within the first weeks or months, and well before 5 to 10 years), underscoring nonadherence as a major, common barrier to sustained treatment success.","boardPrep":"Step 1: know the roughly 40-50% noncompliance rate within 1-2 years as a key epidemiologic fact. COMLEX: reinforces adherence-planning as an ongoing, not one-time, clinical task.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 1","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare management strategies for acute agitation, catatonia, suicidal risk, aggression, negative symptoms, and cognitive impairment.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with schizophrenia has a history of a prior hospitalization for suicidality and ongoing suicidal ideation despite trials of other antipsychotics. Which agent has particular evidence for reducing suicidal ideation in this population?","choices":{"A":"Haloperidol","B":"Clozapine","C":"Quetiapine","D":"Aripiprazole","E":"Chlorpromazine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A large pharmacologic study found clozapine may have particular efficacy in reducing suicidal ideation in schizophrenia patients with prior hospitalizations for suicidality, distinguishing it from the other listed antipsychotics.","boardPrep":"Step 1: clozapine has a specific, named suicide-risk-reduction indication in schizophrenia. COMLEX: reinforces clozapine's unique role beyond treatment-resistant positive symptoms alone.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 5","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare management strategies for acute agitation, catatonia, suicidal risk, aggression, negative symptoms, and cognitive impairment.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with schizophrenia sits immobile, mute, and exhibits waxy flexibility. Which class of medication is the recommended first-line treatment for this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Benzodiazepines first-line","B":"High-potency first-generation antipsychotics","C":"Anticholinergics first-line","D":"Stimulants first-line","E":"Beta blockers first-line"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Catatonia is classically treated first-line with benzodiazepines (e.g., a lorazepam challenge), which can produce rapid and sometimes dramatic improvement, whereas antipsychotics — especially high-potency first-generation agents — can worsen catatonia and are not first-line.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lorazepam challenge is both diagnostic and therapeutic first-line for catatonia. COMLEX: catatonia management is a distinct, high-yield clinical presentation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 5","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare management strategies for acute agitation, catatonia, suicidal risk, aggression, negative symptoms, and cognitive impairment.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with a history of prior assaultiveness, dangerous behavior while hospitalized, and delusions with themes of harming others is being evaluated. Which outcome is this combination most predictive of?","choices":{"A":"Improved treatment adherence","B":"Spontaneous remission","C":"Homicidal or violent behavior","D":"Reduced relapse risk","E":"Improved social functioning"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A history of previous violence, dangerous inpatient behavior, and delusions or hallucinations involving violent themes are recognized predictors of homicidal or violent activity in patients with schizophrenia, informing safety planning. These are risk factors for violence, so they predict neither better adherence, remission, lower relapse nor better social functioning.","boardPrep":"Step 1: know the named predictors of violence/homicide risk in schizophrenia for safety-planning questions. COMLEX: reinforces risk-factor-based safety assessment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 5","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply evidence-based treatment principles for schizophrenia-spectrum disorders, including antipsychotic initiation, adherence planning, relapse prevention, and psychosocial supports.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old with newly diagnosed schizophrenia is started on an oral antipsychotic and shows mild improvement by week 2. His psychiatrist recommends continuing the same medication rather than switching. Which of the following best supports this decision?","choices":{"A":"All patients improve identically regardless of early response","B":"Switching medications always produces faster remission than continuing","C":"Early mild improvement predicts imminent relapse","D":"Early mild improvement often predicts steady further gains","E":"Antipsychotics require a 6-month trial before any response is expected"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Patients who show even a mild degree of improvement during an adequate 4- to 6-week antipsychotic trial often continue to improve steadily for 3 to 6 months (Kaplan ch. 5), supporting a decision to continue rather than switch, unlike patients who show no response at all over an adequate trial.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an adequate antipsychotic trial is 4 to 6 weeks, and even mild improvement during it predicts continued gains over 3 to 6 months. COMLEX: reinforces individualized trial-duration decision-making.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 5 — first-episode psychosis maintenance planning","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply evidence-based treatment principles for schizophrenia-spectrum disorders, including antipsychotic initiation, adherence planning, relapse prevention, and psychosocial supports.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old with chronic schizophrenia has had 2 prior relapses due to medication discontinuation. His psychiatrist wants to reduce his relapse risk. Which combination of strategies best reflects the evidence-based approach?","choices":{"A":"Oral medication, with no psychosocial component","B":"A long-acting injectable plus family-oriented therapy","C":"Psychosocial therapy, discontinuing all pharmacotherapy","D":"As-needed oral dosing during acute symptom flares","E":"A benzodiazepine as the sole maintenance therapy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A long-acting injectable antipsychotic directly addresses his documented nonadherence-driven relapse pattern, while family-oriented psychosocial therapy has evidence for reducing relapse and rehospitalization rates — a combination reflecting the fact that patients receiving both pharmacologic and psychosocial treatment do better than either alone. As-needed oral dosing during flares still depends on the oral adherence that has failed twice, while long-acting injectables were developed for patients likely to stop daily oral medication.","boardPrep":"Step 1: combining a long-acting injectable with psychosocial support directly targets this patient's specific relapse pattern. COMLEX: reinforces integrated pharmacologic-plus-psychosocial treatment planning.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 5 — first-episode psychosis maintenance planning","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply evidence-based treatment principles for schizophrenia-spectrum disorders, including antipsychotic initiation, adherence planning, relapse prevention, and psychosocial supports.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old with schizophrenia has residual social withdrawal, poor eye contact, and difficulty perceiving others' emotions despite adequate antipsychotic treatment. Which specific psychosocial intervention is best suited to address these particular symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Social skills training for him","B":"Psychodynamic insight-oriented group therapy","C":"Electroconvulsive therapy","D":"A higher dose of his antipsychotic","E":"Crisis intervention and case management"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Social skills training directly addresses poor eye contact, unusual response delays, odd facial expressions, and inaccurate perception of others' emotions through role-playing, video modeling, and homework practice, and can reduce relapse and hospitalization — distinct from the other interventions listed, which target different symptom domains.","boardPrep":"Step 1: social skills training targets the specific interpersonal/behavioral deficits described here — a frequently tested intervention-to-symptom matching. COMLEX: reinforces targeted, symptom-specific psychosocial intervention selection.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 5 — first-episode psychosis maintenance planning","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze treatment resistance, nonadherence, substance use, homelessness, stigma, cognitive impairment, and limited insight as barriers to effective care.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old with schizophrenia has been homeless for the past year, has an active cannabis use disorder, and expresses concern about being seen taking medication in front of others at his shelter. His clinician is trying to prioritize the most actionable barrier to his care. Which of the following best frames the approach?","choices":{"A":"Substance use comes first, since stable housing depends on sobriety","B":"Homelessness comes first, since medication cannot work without housing","C":"These barriers are not clinically significant, and no intervention is needed","D":"Stigma comes first, since his concern about being seen outweighs other barriers","E":"Multiple barriers are present, and each needs its own targeted intervention"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Homelessness, substance use, and stigma are each independently recognized barriers to effective schizophrenia care, and patients frequently face several simultaneously; addressing his care requires targeted intervention for each barrier rather than assuming only one is relevant.","boardPrep":"Step 1: barriers to care in schizophrenia (homelessness, substance use, stigma, cognitive impairment, limited insight) often co-occur and require a comprehensive, multi-pronged approach. COMLEX: reinforces addressing social determinants of health alongside psychiatric symptoms.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 1 — timeline-based differentiation of psychotic disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze treatment resistance, nonadherence, substance use, homelessness, stigma, cognitive impairment, and limited insight as barriers to effective care.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old with schizophrenia has impaired working memory and attention that persist despite good adherence and well-controlled positive symptoms. His family asks why he still struggles at his part-time job. Which of the following best explains this?","choices":{"A":"His symptoms indicate treatment failure requiring an entirely different antipsychotic","B":"His difficulties are best explained by medication nonadherence","C":"His difficulties reflect ongoing untreated positive symptoms","D":"Cognitive impairment persists independent of positive-symptom control","E":"His difficulties will fully resolve simply by increasing his antipsychotic dose"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cognitive impairment is a recognized, distinct barrier to functional recovery in schizophrenia that can persist even when positive symptoms are well controlled and adherence is good, rather than necessarily indicating treatment failure, nonadherence, or a dose-response issue.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cognitive impairment is a named barrier to effective functional recovery independent of positive-symptom control. COMLEX: reinforces distinguishing barrier categories rather than defaulting to a single explanation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 1 — timeline-based differentiation of psychotic disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze treatment resistance, nonadherence, substance use, homelessness, stigma, cognitive impairment, and limited insight as barriers to effective care.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old with schizophrenia has had positive symptoms persist despite two adequate, sequential antipsychotic trials at appropriate doses and durations. Which of the following most accurately describes his situation and the recommended next step?","choices":{"A":"He meets resistance criteria, and clozapine is now indicated","B":"He needs a third trial, and a similar drug is preferred","C":"He should be presumed nonadherent, and no further workup done","D":"He should switch to a benzodiazepine used as monotherapy","E":"No options remain for him, and further treatment is futile"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Failure to respond to two adequate, sequential antipsychotic trials defines treatment resistance, at which point clozapine — shown to have the most evident advantage in patients who previously responded poorly to other antipsychotics — should be considered, rather than assuming nonadherence or exhausting further first-line options.","boardPrep":"Step 1: two failed adequate trials = treatment resistance = clozapine consideration, a classic algorithm. COMLEX: reinforces evidence-based escalation pathways in treatment-resistant schizophrenia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 1 — timeline-based differentiation of psychotic disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare management strategies for acute agitation, catatonia, suicidal risk, aggression, negative symptoms, and cognitive impairment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old man with schizophrenia has excellent premorbid functioning, preserved capacity to experience affect, minimal negative symptoms, and good abstract thinking, but has recently realized his career aspirations are unlikely to be achieved. Which risk is paradoxically elevated in this profile?","choices":{"A":"His paradoxically elevated homicide risk","B":"His risk of medication-induced parkinsonism","C":"His paradoxically elevated suicide risk","D":"His paradoxically elevated risk of tardive dyskinesia","E":"His paradoxically elevated risk of catatonia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Patients with the best prognostic features — few negative symptoms, preserved affect, better abstract thinking — combined with high premorbid expectations and awareness of declining function are paradoxically at the highest risk of suicide, unlike the other listed risks, which are not specifically tied to this \"best prognosis\" profile.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the classic \"insightful, high-functioning, disappointed\" profile predicts elevated suicide risk, not violence risk — a frequently tested distractor pairing. COMLEX: reinforces recognizing atypical high-risk suicide profiles.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 5 — suicide and violence risk factor interpretation and safety planning","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare management strategies for acute agitation, catatonia, suicidal risk, aggression, negative symptoms, and cognitive impairment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old with schizophrenia becomes acutely agitated and combative on the inpatient unit. The treating clinician notices they feel unusually fearful in the patient's presence. What is the most appropriate interpretation of this feeling, and the recommended action?","choices":{"A":"The fear is irrelevant, and it should be ignored","B":"The fear signals a need, and restraint should follow","C":"The fear should prompt an immediate antipsychotic dose increase","D":"The fear reflects countertransference, analyzed after the session","E":"The fear is a meaningful cue, and an attendant should now be present"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Clinician-experienced fear in the presence of a patient with schizophrenia should be taken seriously as an internal clue that the patient may be on the verge of acting out violently, warranting termination of the interview or conducting it with an attendant available, rather than being dismissed or acted on solely with restraint or medication changes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: clinician-felt fear is an explicitly named, actionable safety signal in schizophrenia care. COMLEX: reinforces provider self-awareness as part of safety assessment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 5 — suicide and violence risk factor interpretation and safety planning","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare management strategies for acute agitation, catatonia, suicidal risk, aggression, negative symptoms, and cognitive impairment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old with chronic schizophrenia and prominent negative symptoms has not responded adequately to antipsychotic adjustment alone. Which combination of interventions is most appropriate for his negative symptoms and residual cognitive impairment?","choices":{"A":"A benzodiazepine added to reduce his social avoidance","B":"Psychosocial therapy added to his existing antipsychotic regimen","C":"Immediate electroconvulsive therapy as the first-line option","D":"Discontinuing his antipsychotic entirely in favor of psychotherapy","E":"A stimulant added to overcome his apathy and low drive"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Psychosocial therapies, including social skills training, group therapy, and family therapy, have evidence for reducing negative symptoms and improving overall functioning when added to antipsychotic treatment, unlike benzodiazepines, ECT as a first-line negative-symptom treatment, or discontinuing antipsychotics; a stimulant is not an established negative-symptom treatment and risks worsening psychosis, since amphetamines can themselves induce psychotic symptoms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: negative symptoms and cognitive impairment respond best to combined pharmacologic-plus-psychosocial treatment, not medication adjustment alone. COMLEX: reinforces evidence-based negative-symptom management strategy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 5 — suicide and violence risk factor interpretation and safety planning","isHighYield":true}],"26":[{"id":"sdl26_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate indications for first-generation antipsychotics, second-generation antipsychotics, long-acting injectables, and clozapine by receptor profile, therapeutic indications, potency, formulation, and high-yield adverse effects.","batch":1,"stem":"A psychiatry resident is asked to name the second-generation antipsychotic drug that is reserved specifically for patients who have failed substantial trials of at least two other antipsychotic agents. Which drug is this?","choices":{"A":"Haloperidol","B":"Risperidone","C":"Quetiapine","D":"Aripiprazole","E":"Clozapine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Clozapine is limited to treatment-refractory schizophrenia because of its risk of agranulocytosis and seizures, which prevent more widespread first-line use despite superior efficacy in refractory cases. Haloperidol is a first-generation high-potency agent used first-line for acute psychosis, not reserved for refractory cases. Risperidone, quetiapine, and aripiprazole are standard second-generation first-line agents without a treatment-refractory restriction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Clozapine = last-resort drug for treatment-resistant schizophrenia, reserved after two failed antipsychotic trials given here. COMLEX: Clinically, weekly ANC monitoring for the first 6 months is the mandated safety requirement before clozapine can even be dispensed.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 3 — builds on mesolimbic/mesocortical/nigrostriatal/tuberoinfundibular dopamine pathways to now classify the drugs that act on them","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate indications for first-generation antipsychotics, second-generation antipsychotics, long-acting injectables, and clozapine by receptor profile, therapeutic indications, potency, formulation, and high-yield adverse effects.","batch":1,"stem":"A student is comparing chlorpromazine and haloperidol on a receptor-binding potency chart. Chlorpromazine is classified as low clinical potency while haloperidol is classified as high clinical potency. Which formulation feature distinguishes these two first-generation agents?","choices":{"A":"Chlorpromazine requires a much lower milligram dose to achieve equivalent antipsychotic effect","B":"Haloperidol requires a much lower milligram dose to achieve equivalent antipsychotic effect","C":"Haloperidol has a substantially longer average half-life than chlorpromazine","D":"Chlorpromazine's sole approved formulation is a long-acting injectable","E":"Haloperidol cannot be administered parenterally for acute agitation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Clinical potency describes the milligram dose needed for equivalent antipsychotic effect, not efficacy — haloperidol's minimum effective dose is 2 mg versus chlorpromazine's 100 mg, making it far more potent per milligram. Chlorpromazine requiring a lower dose reverses this relationship. Half-life is not the defining feature of the potency classification. Chlorpromazine is available in oral and parenteral forms, not only injectable. Haloperidol has a well-established parenteral formulation used for acute agitation. Katzung's Table 29-4 gives minimum effective doses of 2 mg (haloperidol) and 100 mg (chlorpromazine); the Kaplan ch. 21 text instead equates about 5 mg of haloperidol with 100 mg of chlorpromazine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: High-potency (haloperidol, fluphenazine) = low mg dose, high EPS; low-potency (chlorpromazine, thioridazine) = high mg dose, more anticholinergic/sedating/hypotensive. COMLEX: Antipsychotic dose-equivalence comparisons are a recurring clinical-pharmacology testing point.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 3 — builds on mesolimbic/mesocortical/nigrostriatal/tuberoinfundibular dopamine pathways to now classify the drugs that act on them","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate indications for first-generation antipsychotics, second-generation antipsychotics, long-acting injectables, and clozapine by receptor profile, therapeutic indications, potency, formulation, and high-yield adverse effects.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient asks why some antipsychotics are described by their \"D2:5-HT2A receptor binding ratio\" rather than by potency alone. Which statement best explains the clinical significance of this ratio?","choices":{"A":"The ratio reflects how strongly an agent binds histamine receptors, predicting sedation","B":"The ratio predicts anticholinergic adverse effects but has no relationship to extrapyramidal symptoms","C":"A lower D2:5-HT2A ratio favors serotonin blockade and marks second-generation agents with lower EPS risk","D":"The ratio determines oral bioavailability and the dosing interval of each agent","E":"A lower D2:5-HT2A ratio predicts more EPS, since serotonin blockade worsens motor effects"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Agents such as clozapine, risperidone, olanzapine, and quetiapine have a low D2:5-HT2A ratio (relatively more serotonin than dopamine blockade), which is thought to underlie their comparatively lower EPS burden versus the first-generation, high-ratio agents like fluphenazine and haloperidol. A higher ratio, not lower, is the pattern for high-EPS first-generation drugs. The ratio is a receptor-binding, not bioavailability, concept. It compares D2 with 5-HT2A affinity, so it does not describe histamine binding (which predicts sedation) or muscarinic binding (which predicts anticholinergic effects), and its main clinical meaning is precisely the EPS difference.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Serotonin-dopamine antagonism (low D2:5-HT2A ratio) = signature receptor mechanism distinguishing atypical from typical agents on EPS risk. COMLEX: Receptor-ratio comparisons are a classic pharm-table board question format.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 3 — builds on mesolimbic/mesocortical/nigrostriatal/tuberoinfundibular dopamine pathways to now classify the drugs that act on them","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze D2 receptor blockade and serotonin-dopamine antagonism to predict effects on positive symptoms, negative symptoms, EPS, prolactin, and metabolic risk.","batch":1,"stem":"A pharmacology lecture explains that blockade of D2 receptors in the mesolimbic pathway is the mechanism thought to underlie antipsychotic efficacy for a specific symptom domain. Which symptom domain is this?","choices":{"A":"Negative symptoms, such as avolition, alogia, and flat affect","B":"Positive symptoms, such as hallucinations and delusions","C":"Cognitive symptoms, such as impaired working memory and executive function","D":"Extrapyramidal symptoms, such as bradykinesia and cogwheel rigidity","E":"Endocrine symptoms, such as galactorrhea and amenorrhea"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Excess mesolimbic dopamine activity is the classic hypothesized driver of positive psychotic symptoms, and D2 blockade in this pathway is credited with their improvement. Negative and cognitive symptoms are attributed more to mesocortical hypoactivity, which typical D2 blockade does not correct. Extrapyramidal symptoms result from nigrostriatal, not mesolimbic, blockade. Endocrine effects like galactorrhea result from tuberoinfundibular blockade.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Mesolimbic = positive symptoms (excess DA, blockade helps); mesocortical = negative/cognitive symptoms (deficient DA, blockade can worsen). COMLEX: This pathway-to-symptom mapping is a recurring high-yield mechanism question.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 2 — links D2/5-HT2A blockade back to the positive, negative, and cognitive symptom domains it is meant to treat","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze D2 receptor blockade and serotonin-dopamine antagonism to predict effects on positive symptoms, negative symptoms, EPS, prolactin, and metabolic risk.","batch":1,"stem":"A pharmacology review notes that most second-generation antipsychotics cause little or no rise in prolactin, but that one widely used second-generation agent, along with its active metabolite paliperidone, raises prolactin much as older typical antipsychotics do. Which agent is it?","choices":{"A":"Quetiapine","B":"Aripiprazole","C":"Risperidone","D":"Olanzapine","E":"Brexpiprazole"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Katzung states that the older typical antipsychotics, as well as risperidone and paliperidone, produce elevations of prolactin, whereas newer antipsychotics such as olanzapine, quetiapine, aripiprazole, and brexpiprazole cause no or minimal increases, reflecting their weaker D2 antagonism; paliperidone is 9-hydroxyrisperidone, risperidone's active metabolite. Quetiapine and olanzapine bind D2 receptors loosely enough at usual doses to spare prolactin, although olanzapine carries high metabolic risk. Aripiprazole and brexpiprazole are partial D2 agonists, and Katzung suggests switching to an agent such as aripiprazole when hyperprolactinemia cannot be managed by lowering the dose.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperprolactinemia comes from tuberoinfundibular D2 blockade and causes galactorrhea, amenorrhea, infertility, sexual dysfunction, and over time osteoporosis; among second-generation agents, risperidone and paliperidone are the main offenders. COMLEX: for antipsychotic-induced hyperprolactinemia, lower the dose or switch to a prolactin-sparing agent such as aripiprazole.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — endocrine monitoring needs for antipsychotic therapy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze D2 receptor blockade and serotonin-dopamine antagonism to predict effects on positive symptoms, negative symptoms, EPS, prolactin, and metabolic risk.","batch":1,"stem":"A resident is told that clozapine and olanzapine achieve antipsychotic efficacy at only 30-50% striatal D2 receptor occupancy, while first-generation agents require at least 60% occupancy. Which mechanism best explains why lower D2 occupancy is sufficient for these two drugs?","choices":{"A":"Concurrent high occupancy of 5-HT2A receptors reduces the D2 occupancy needed for efficacy","B":"Concurrent high occupancy of muscarinic receptors reduces the D2 occupancy needed for efficacy","C":"Concurrent high occupancy of histamine H1 receptors reduces the D2 occupancy needed for efficacy","D":"Concurrent high occupancy of alpha-1 receptors reduces the D2 occupancy needed for efficacy","E":"Concurrent low affinity for D4 receptors reduces the D2 occupancy needed for efficacy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Clozapine and olanzapine's high concurrent 5-HT2A receptor occupancy is the mechanism proposed to explain why they achieve efficacy at a lower threshold of D2 occupancy than first-generation agents. Muscarinic, H1, and alpha-1 blockade drive anticholinergic, sedative, and hypotensive adverse effects respectively, not the efficacy-occupancy relationship. Low D4 affinity is not the explanation offered for this specific occupancy phenomenon.","boardPrep":"Step 1: 60% D2 occupancy threshold for typical-drug efficacy, 80%+ for EPS; atypicals need only 30-50% because of added 5-HT2A blockade. COMLEX: Aripiprazole is the exception — very high D2 occupancy on PET without EPS, because it is a partial agonist, not a full antagonist.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 2 — links D2/5-HT2A blockade back to the positive, negative, and cognitive symptom domains it is meant to treat","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare high-potency and low-potency typical antipsychotics by EPS, anticholinergic effects, sedation, orthostasis, and QT risk.","batch":1,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman who has taken a high daily dose of a low-potency phenothiazine for many years reports a gradual brownish tint to her vision. Fundoscopy shows retinal pigment deposits resembling retinitis pigmentosa. Her prescriber notes that this drug is the only antipsychotic known to cause retinal deposits and that its daily dose is capped at 800 mg to reduce this risk. Which drug is she taking?","choices":{"A":"Haloperidol","B":"Fluphenazine","C":"Chlorpromazine","D":"Perphenazine","E":"Thioridazine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Katzung states that thioridazine is the only antipsychotic drug that causes retinal deposits, which in advanced cases may resemble retinitis pigmentosa and are usually associated with browning of vision, and that its maximum daily dose is limited to 800 mg to reduce this complication; thioridazine also carries the greatest QT-prolonging risk among the phenothiazines. Chlorpromazine, the other classic low-potency phenothiazine, causes deposits in the anterior eye, the cornea and lens, rather than the retina. Haloperidol is a high-potency butyrophenone, and fluphenazine and perphenazine are higher-potency phenothiazines; none of them is linked to pigmentary retinopathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: thioridazine = retinal deposits (retinitis pigmentosa-like, browning of vision, dose cap 800 mg/day) and QT prolongation; chlorpromazine = corneal and lens deposits and photosensitivity. COMLEX: patients on long-term low-potency phenothiazines need eye examinations and ECG monitoring.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — cardiovascular and other monitoring needs for antipsychotic therapy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare high-potency and low-potency typical antipsychotics by EPS, anticholinergic effects, sedation, orthostasis, and QT risk.","batch":1,"stem":"Compared with a high-potency agent like haloperidol, a low-potency agent like chlorpromazine is generally associated with which adverse-effect pattern?","choices":{"A":"More extrapyramidal symptoms but less sedation and anticholinergic effects","B":"Equal extrapyramidal symptoms but more sedation and anticholinergic effects","C":"Less extrapyramidal symptoms but more sedation and anticholinergic effects","D":"More extrapyramidal symptoms and more sedation and anticholinergic effects","E":"Less extrapyramidal symptoms and less sedation and anticholinergic effects"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Low-potency agents like chlorpromazine trade lower EPS toxicity for greater sedative, anticholinergic, and hypotensive burden, an inverse relationship: chlorpromazine has medium EPS and high sedation, haloperidol very high EPS and low sedation. Claiming more EPS with less sedation reverses the known trade-off. Equal EPS is inconsistent with the marked difference in EPS toxicity ratings between the two drugs. Claiming both effects increase or both decrease ignores the well-established inverse relationship.","boardPrep":"Step 1: High-potency = high EPS, low sedation/anticholinergic/hypotension; low-potency = the reverse — this inverse trade-off is one of the most tested pharm relationships in psychiatry. COMLEX: Choose potency based on which adverse-effect profile the specific patient can tolerate least.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 1 — extends the sedation/anticholinergic/adverse-effect comparison framework used for anxiolytics to typical antipsychotics","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare high-potency and low-potency typical antipsychotics by EPS, anticholinergic effects, sedation, orthostasis, and QT risk.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient is prescribed thioridazine, a low-potency phenothiazine, at a high dose and develops nonspecific T-wave abnormalities on ECG. This finding is most consistent with which known cardiac risk of this drug?","choices":{"A":"Accelerated atrioventricular nodal conduction with reentry","B":"QT-interval prolongation with torsades risk","C":"Left ventricular hypertrophy with diastolic dysfunction","D":"Restrictive pericarditis with constrictive physiology","E":"Pulmonary hypertension with right heart strain"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Thioridazine in doses exceeding 300 mg daily is almost always associated with T-wave abnormalities reflecting QT-interval effects, and its overdoses are linked to torsades de pointes, conduction block, and sudden death. It does not accelerate AV conduction; if anything, high doses risk conduction block. It has no established association with ventricular hypertrophy, restrictive pericarditis, or pulmonary hypertension.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Thioridazine and ziprasidone carry the highest QT-prolongation risk among antipsychotics; avoid combining with other QT-prolonging drugs (Class 1A/3 antiarrhythmics, pimozide). COMLEX: QTc monitoring is only clinically meaningful once values exceed 500 ms on repeated tracings.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 -> Obj 1 — extends the sedation/anticholinergic/adverse-effect comparison framework used for anxiolytics to typical antipsychotics","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply antipsychotic selection principles to schizophrenia, acute mania, bipolar depression, psychotic depression, agitation, autism-related irritability, and treatment-resistant schizophrenia.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with bipolar I disorder in an acute manic episode needs rapid symptom control. Which of the following antipsychotic agents is FDA-approved for treatment of the manic phase of bipolar disorder?","choices":{"A":"Olanzapine","B":"Clozapine","C":"Fluphenazine","D":"Thioridazine","E":"Molindone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Olanzapine, along with aripiprazole, chlorpromazine, quetiapine, risperidone, and ziprasidone, is FDA-approved for treatment of the manic phase of bipolar disorder. Clozapine is reserved for treatment-refractory schizophrenia and is not an approved first-line mania treatment. Fluphenazine, thioridazine, and molindone are older first-generation agents without this specific FDA mania indication.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Second-generation agents (olanzapine, quetiapine, risperidone, ziprasidone, aripiprazole) plus chlorpromazine carry FDA mania indications; know this drug list. COMLEX: Olanzapine-fluoxetine combination and quetiapine specifically carry the bipolar depression indication.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 1 — extends evidence-based antipsychotic initiation principles to indication-specific drug selection across mood and psychotic disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply antipsychotic selection principles to schizophrenia, acute mania, bipolar depression, psychotic depression, agitation, autism-related irritability, and treatment-resistant schizophrenia.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with treatment-resistant depression and psychotic features (delusions of guilt) is being managed with an antidepressant plus adjunctive antipsychotic. Which principle best guides the choice to add an antipsychotic in this setting?","choices":{"A":"Psychotic depression responds fully to antidepressant monotherapy without any antipsychotic augmentation","B":"Antipsychotics are contraindicated whenever depressive symptoms coexist with psychotic features","C":"Antipsychotic augmentation is reserved specifically for depression with a comorbid substance use disorder, not for psychotic depression","D":"Psychotic depression requires an antipsychotic in addition to an antidepressant for full symptom control","E":"Antipsychotic augmentation should be delayed until after a second antidepressant trial has failed"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Major depressive disorder with psychotic features (psychotic depression) is a specific indication for combining an antidepressant with an antipsychotic, since antidepressant monotherapy is inadequate for the psychotic component. Claiming full response to monotherapy is incorrect for this specific presentation. Antipsychotics are not contraindicated here; they are indicated. Restricting augmentation to substance-use comorbidity or delaying it until an antidepressant trial fails misstates the immediate indication that psychotic depression itself provides.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Psychotic depression = antidepressant + antipsychotic (or ECT) — a classic pharmacotherapy-selection vignette. COMLEX: ECT is also a first-line option, particularly with high suicide risk or catatonic features.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 1 — extends evidence-based antipsychotic initiation principles to indication-specific drug selection across mood and psychotic disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply antipsychotic selection principles to schizophrenia, acute mania, bipolar depression, psychotic depression, agitation, autism-related irritability, and treatment-resistant schizophrenia.","batch":1,"stem":"A child with autism spectrum disorder has severe irritability with self-injurious behavior. Which class of medication is used to specifically target this symptom domain rather than the core social-communication deficits of autism?","choices":{"A":"Benzodiazepines","B":"Stimulants","C":"Atypical antipsychotics","D":"Mood stabilizers","E":"Anticholinergics"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Atypical antipsychotics such as risperidone and aripiprazole are FDA-approved specifically for irritability associated with autism spectrum disorder, targeting aggression and self-injury rather than the disorder's core deficits. Benzodiazepines are not indicated for this symptom domain and risk paradoxical disinhibition in children. Stimulants target comorbid attention symptoms, not irritability. Mood stabilizers and anticholinergics lack an established indication for autism-related irritability specifically.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Risperidone and aripiprazole are the two FDA-approved agents for irritability in autism spectrum disorder — a frequently tested indication distinct from schizophrenia. COMLEX: Monitor for the same metabolic and EPS risks in pediatric patients as in adults.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 1 — extends evidence-based antipsychotic initiation principles to indication-specific drug selection across mood and psychotic disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Interpret clozapine indications and monitoring needs, including agranulocytosis, myocarditis, seizures, metabolic effects, and treatment-refractory illness.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient is started on clozapine for treatment-refractory schizophrenia. Which monitoring schedule is required because of the drug's risk of a serious, potentially fatal hematologic adverse effect?","choices":{"A":"Monthly absolute neutrophil counts for the first year of therapy","B":"Absolute neutrophil counts checked when fever or sore throat develops during treatment, with no routine screening otherwise","C":"Weekly platelet counts for the first 6 months, then every 3 weeks thereafter","D":"A single baseline absolute neutrophil count obtained just prior to starting therapy, with no further monitoring","E":"Weekly absolute neutrophil counts for the first 6 months, then every 3 weeks thereafter"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Because clozapine causes agranulocytosis in up to 2% of treated patients (Katzung ch. 29), patients require weekly blood counts for the first 6 months of treatment and every 3 weeks thereafter (Katzung, SDL 26 reading). The Kaplan schizophrenia chapter (SDL 24–25 reading) instead describes weekly monitoring for 6 months, every 2 weeks for the next 6 months, and monthly after that. Monthly monitoring for only one year is less frequent than required and does not match the mandated schedule. Symptom-triggered-only monitoring misses the often asymptomatic early drop in neutrophils. The monitored parameter is the neutrophil count, not the platelet count. A single baseline count is inadequate since agranulocytosis develops during treatment, not before it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Clozapine agranulocytosis = weekly ANC x 6 months, then every 3 weeks — a frequently tested exact monitoring schedule. COMLEX: Discontinuing clozapine abruptly is dangerous for other reasons (rapid, severe psychotic relapse), so the drug should not be stopped except for a genuine medical emergency like agranulocytosis or myocarditis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 2 — connects treatment-resistance and nonadherence barriers to clozapine as the definitive treatment-refractory option","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Interpret clozapine indications and monitoring needs, including agranulocytosis, myocarditis, seizures, metabolic effects, and treatment-refractory illness.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient on clozapine develops fever, tachycardia, and elevated troponin within the first month of therapy. Which adverse effect should be suspected, requiring immediate drug discontinuation?","choices":{"A":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","B":"Agranulocytosis","C":"Torsades de pointes","D":"Acute myocarditis","E":"Serotonin syndrome"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Clozapine is specifically associated with myocarditis, presenting with fever, tachycardia, and elevated cardiac biomarkers, and the drug must be discontinued if myocarditis manifests, since it is a true medical emergency. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome presents with muscle rigidity, hyperthermia, and a markedly elevated creatine kinase rather than this troponin-elevation pattern. Agranulocytosis can also present with fever in the first months of clozapine therapy, but it is detected by absolute neutrophil count monitoring and does not raise troponin. QT prolongation with torsades is more classically linked to thioridazine and ziprasidone, not clozapine's signature early cardiac risk. Serotonin syndrome requires a serotonergic precipitant and presents with a different symptom triad (autonomic instability, neuromuscular hyperactivity, altered mental status).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Clozapine myocarditis = early (often first weeks), fever + tachycardia + troponin rise, mandates immediate discontinuation, listed alongside agranulocytosis as a true clozapine emergency. COMLEX: De novo seizures also occur in 2-5% of clozapine-treated patients and are managed with an anticonvulsant, not automatic discontinuation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 2 — connects treatment-resistance and nonadherence barriers to clozapine as the definitive treatment-refractory option","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Interpret clozapine indications and monitoring needs, including agranulocytosis, myocarditis, seizures, metabolic effects, and treatment-refractory illness.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with schizophrenia who has failed adequate trials of two different antipsychotic agents at substantial doses is being evaluated for clozapine. Which statement about clozapine's efficacy in this scenario is accurate?","choices":{"A":"Thirty to fifty percent of previously treatment-refractory patients respond to clozapine","B":"Fewer than five percent of previously treatment-refractory patients respond to clozapine","C":"Clozapine requires combination with a second antipsychotic agent to be effective","D":"Clozapine response requires at least twelve months of therapy before any benefit appears","E":"Clozapine is equally effective as first-generation agents but carries no additional risk"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Approximately 30-50% of patients previously refractory to standard doses of other antipsychotics respond to clozapine, justifying its increased risk profile in this population. A response rate under 5% would not justify the drug's use given its monitoring burden. Clozapine is used as monotherapy, not requiring routine combination with a second antipsychotic. Refractory patients are typically given a substantial trial (often up to 6 months) but response is not defined as requiring a full year before any benefit. Clozapine carries meaningfully greater risk (agranulocytosis, myocarditis, seizures) than standard agents, so it is not equally risky.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Clozapine = 30-50% response rate in patients refractory to 2-3 substantial antipsychotic trials; also the only antipsychotic FDA-approved specifically to reduce suicide risk in schizophrenia. COMLEX: High-dose olanzapine (30-45 mg/d) is a secondary refractory-illness option when clozapine cannot be used.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 -> Obj 2 — connects treatment-resistance and nonadherence barriers to clozapine as the definitive treatment-refractory option","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Predict medication adherence and safety implications of oral, orally disintegrating, intramuscular, and long-acting injectable antipsychotic formulations.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with chronic schizophrenia has a history of covert nonadherence with oral antipsychotics, leading to repeated relapses. Which formulation strategy best addresses this specific adherence problem?","choices":{"A":"Switching to an orally disintegrating tablet taken at home unsupervised","B":"Switching to a long-acting injectable antipsychotic given monthly by a clinician","C":"Increasing the oral daily dose without otherwise changing the formulation","D":"Switching to a liquid oral formulation taken at home unsupervised daily","E":"Adding a second oral antipsychotic also taken at home unsupervised"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Long-acting injectable (LAI) formulations, administered by a clinician on a fixed schedule, directly solve covert nonadherence because administration is observed and does not depend on the patient remembering or choosing to take daily oral doses. Orally disintegrating tablets, liquid formulations, and simply increasing the oral dose or adding a second oral drug all remain unsupervised, at-home strategies that do nothing to confirm the medication was actually taken.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LAI antipsychotics (haloperidol decanoate, fluphenazine decanoate, and newer risperidone/olanzapine/aripiprazole/paliperidone LAIs) directly target nonadherence in noncompliant patients. COMLEX: Newer LAIs are often better tolerated than older depot injectables for long-term maintenance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 5 — links formulation choice (oral vs IM vs LAI) back to the acuity/safety framework for agitation and chronic maintenance","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Predict medication adherence and safety implications of oral, orally disintegrating, intramuscular, and long-acting injectable antipsychotic formulations.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient in the emergency department is acutely agitated and psychotic, refusing all oral medication. Which route of antipsychotic administration is most appropriate for rapid symptom control in this immediate setting?","choices":{"A":"Intramuscular short-acting antipsychotic","B":"Long-acting injectable antipsychotic","C":"Orally disintegrating tablet","D":"Oral liquid concentrate","E":"Transdermal patch"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Intramuscular formulations of short-acting agents like haloperidol allow rapid initiation of treatment in an acutely agitated, refusing patient, since parenteral administration bypasses the need for oral cooperation and has faster onset. Long-acting injectables are designed for chronic maintenance dosing over weeks, not rapid acute control, and their pharmacokinetics make them inappropriate for emergency titration. An orally disintegrating tablet still requires oral cooperation, which this patient is refusing. An oral liquid concentrate is absorbed quickly but still has to be swallowed, and giving it hidden in a drink would bypass consent. A transdermal antipsychotic patch (asenapine) exists, but it takes days to reach steady state and is a maintenance formulation, not an option for acute agitation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Short-acting IM antipsychotics (often paired with a benzodiazepine) are first-line for acute agitation when oral routes are refused. COMLEX: Never substitute an LAI for acute agitation control — its onset is far too slow for an emergency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 5 — links formulation choice (oral vs IM vs LAI) back to the acuity/safety framework for agitation and chronic maintenance","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Predict medication adherence and safety implications of oral, orally disintegrating, intramuscular, and long-acting injectable antipsychotic formulations.","batch":1,"stem":"A clinic is deciding between an oral daily antipsychotic and a long-acting injectable for a newly diagnosed patient who has consistently taken every prescribed dose, has an engaged household willing to help, and keeps every scheduled visit. Which factor most appropriately favors continuing the oral formulation in this specific patient?","choices":{"A":"Oral formulations generally produce faster symptom resolution than injectable formulations","B":"Long-acting injectables are contraindicated in any patient with a first psychotic episode","C":"Oral formulations eliminate the need for any future medication monitoring","D":"Reliable adherence and support make added injectable burden unnecessary","E":"Long-acting injectables cannot be used until a patient has failed oral therapy at least once"},"correct":"D","explanation":"When a patient already demonstrates reliable adherence with adequate family and clinical support, the formulation decision reasonably favors the simpler, less invasive oral route, since LAIs primarily solve an adherence problem this patient does not have. Oral formulations do not have inherently faster onset than injectables; onset depends on the specific agent and route used. LAIs are not contraindicated in first-episode psychosis; they are increasingly used there when appropriate. Oral formulations still require ongoing monitoring for adverse effects and response. LAIs are not restricted to only patients who have already failed oral therapy; adherence risk alone is a valid indication.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Formulation choice should be individualized to the patient's adherence risk and support system, not applied uniformly. COMLEX: Discussing formulation preference collaboratively with the patient improves long-term engagement in care.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 5 — links formulation choice (oral vs IM vs LAI) back to the acuity/safety framework for agitation and chronic maintenance","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate indications for first-generation antipsychotics, second-generation antipsychotics, long-acting injectables, and clozapine by receptor profile, therapeutic indications, potency, formulation, and high-yield adverse effects.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man with newly diagnosed schizophrenia is started on an antipsychotic. His psychiatrist explains that first- and second-generation agents are similarly effective for one symptom domain, but the newer drug class has advantages for two others. Which statement correctly summarizes this efficacy comparison?","choices":{"A":"Both classes are similarly effective for positive symptoms, but second-generation agents have a negative-symptom edge","B":"Both classes are similarly effective for negative symptoms, but first-generation agents have an edge for positive symptoms and cognition","C":"Second-generation agents are superior for positive symptoms, while first-generation agents are superior for negative symptoms","D":"First-generation agents are superior across all three symptom domains","E":"Both classes are similarly ineffective for positive symptoms regardless of dose"},"correct":"A","explanation":"For approximately 70% of patients, first- and second-generation antipsychotics show equal efficacy for positive symptoms, but the evidence favors second-generation drugs for negative symptoms, cognition, lower tardive dyskinesia/EPS risk, and lesser prolactin elevation. Claiming equal negative-symptom efficacy with a first-generation cognitive edge reverses the actual finding. Neither class shows a positive-symptom efficacy advantage over the other in typical practice. First-generation superiority across all domains contradicts the established evidence favoring second-generation drugs for negative symptoms and cognition specifically. Both classes are, in fact, effective for positive symptoms at appropriate doses, not ineffective.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FGA = SGA for positive symptoms; SGA > FGA for negative symptoms, cognition, EPS/TD risk, and prolactin — a core efficacy-versus-safety comparison. COMLEX: This comparison underlies why SGAs are generally favored as first-line, despite higher cost historically.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 3 — Dopamine Pathway Basis of Antipsychotic Effects","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q20","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate indications for first-generation antipsychotics, second-generation antipsychotics, long-acting injectables, and clozapine by receptor profile, therapeutic indications, potency, formulation, and high-yield adverse effects.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman with schizophrenia has been stable on risperidone for 3 years but develops mild, intermittent orofacial dyskinetic movements. Her psychiatrist wants to switch her to whichever second-generation option carries the least risk of inducing or exacerbating this abnormal movement pattern. Which agent should be selected?","choices":{"A":"Risperidone","B":"Paliperidone","C":"Quetiapine","D":"Aripiprazole","E":"Olanzapine"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Quetiapine and clozapine are the second-generation agents with the least likelihood of causing tardive dyskinesia, making quetiapine the appropriate switch target when TD develops on another agent. Continuing risperidone at the same dose does not address the emerging TD risk. Paliperidone, risperidone's active metabolite, carries a similar TD risk profile to risperidone itself, not a reduced one. Switching to aripiprazole or olanzapine instead does not offer the same reduced-TD-risk advantage that switching to quetiapine or clozapine does.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Quetiapine and clozapine carry the lowest TD risk among antipsychotics — the classic switch target once TD emerges. COMLEX: The first management step for TD is discontinuing or reducing the current agent, followed by eliminating other central anticholinergic drugs.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 3 — Dopamine Pathway Basis of Antipsychotic Effects","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q21","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate indications for first-generation antipsychotics, second-generation antipsychotics, long-acting injectables, and clozapine by receptor profile, therapeutic indications, potency, formulation, and high-yield adverse effects.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man with schizophrenia has gained 18 kg and developed a fasting triglyceride:HDL ratio of 5.2 since starting an antipsychotic 8 months ago. Which of the following agents is most strongly associated with this pattern of metabolic adverse effects?","choices":{"A":"Ziprasidone","B":"Olanzapine","C":"Aripiprazole","D":"Lurasidone","E":"Haloperidol"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Olanzapine, along with clozapine, is most frequently associated with large increases in weight and lipids, and diabetes mellitus is most often seen with these two agents specifically, matching this patient's significant weight gain and elevated triglyceride:HDL ratio (above the 3.5 threshold indicating increased atherosclerotic risk). Ziprasidone causes the least weight gain among second-generation drugs. Aripiprazole and lurasidone-class agents typically produce only small increases in weight and lipids. Haloperidol, a first-generation agent, is not characteristically linked to this degree of metabolic syndrome, though it carries its own distinct (EPS-predominant) adverse-effect profile.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Clozapine and olanzapine = highest metabolic risk (weight gain, dyslipidemia, diabetes) among antipsychotics; ziprasidone = lowest weight gain among SGAs. COMLEX: Monitor weight at every visit and fasting glucose/lipids every 3-6 months on high-metabolic-risk agents.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 3 — Dopamine Pathway Basis of Antipsychotic Effects","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q22","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze D2 receptor blockade and serotonin-dopamine antagonism to predict effects on positive symptoms, negative symptoms, EPS, prolactin, and metabolic risk.","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old man with schizophrenia has gained 12 kg over the 6 months since starting olanzapine, with increased appetite and a rise in fasting triglycerides. His psychiatrist explains which receptor actions are thought to underlie this adverse effect. Which mechanism is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Combined histamine H1 and serotonin 5-HT2 receptor blockade","B":"Alpha-1 adrenergic receptor blockade in peripheral vessels","C":"Muscarinic receptor blockade slowing gastrointestinal transit","D":"D2 receptor blockade in the tuberoinfundibular pathway","E":"D2 receptor blockade in the mesolimbic reward pathway"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Katzung's table of antipsychotic adverse effects attributes weight gain to possibly combined H1 and 5-HT2 receptor blockade, and notes that weight gain is very common with clozapine and olanzapine, both of which strongly block these receptors. Alpha-1 blockade produces orthostatic hypotension and impaired ejaculation, not weight gain. Muscarinic blockade causes dry mouth, constipation, urinary retention, and blurred vision. Tuberoinfundibular D2 blockade raises prolactin, producing galactorrhea and amenorrhea. Mesolimbic D2 blockade is the proposed basis of the antipsychotic effect on positive symptoms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: olanzapine and clozapine cause the most weight gain, dyslipidemia, and diabetes risk; ziprasidone causes the least, and aripiprazole, lurasidone, and risperidone usually cause small increases. COMLEX: obtain baseline weight, waist, glucose, and lipids before starting a second-generation antipsychotic and repeat them during treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — metabolic monitoring for antipsychotic therapy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q23","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze D2 receptor blockade and serotonin-dopamine antagonism to predict effects on positive symptoms, negative symptoms, EPS, prolactin, and metabolic risk.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man with schizophrenia has predominant negative symptoms (avolition, alogia, flat affect) that have not improved despite adequate treatment with a high-potency first-generation antipsychotic that effectively controlled his hallucinations. Which mechanism best explains this differential response?","choices":{"A":"High D2 blockade with minimal serotonergic activity fully corrects the mesocortical dopaminergic deficit underlying negative symptoms","B":"High D2 blockade with minimal serotonergic activity worsens the mesolimbic dopaminergic excess underlying positive symptoms","C":"High D2 blockade with minimal serotonergic activity does little to correct the mesocortical dopaminergic deficit underlying negative symptoms, correcting mesolimbic excess","D":"Low D2 blockade with high serotonergic activity does little to correct the mesocortical dopaminergic deficit underlying negative symptoms, a profile inconsistent with a high-potency first-generation agent","E":"High D2 blockade with minimal serotonergic activity has no mechanistic relationship to any dopaminergic pathway"},"correct":"C","explanation":"First-generation agents, with their high D2:5-HT2A ratio and minimal serotonergic component, effectively reduce mesolimbic dopaminergic excess (positive symptoms) but do little to address the mesocortical dopaminergic hypoactivity thought to underlie negative symptoms, consistent with this patient's good positive-symptom response but persistent negative symptoms. Claiming full correction of the mesocortical deficit contradicts his ongoing negative symptoms. His hallucinations were controlled, not worsened, ruling out mesolimbic worsening. Describing the agent as low-D2/high-serotonergic misdescribes a first-generation, high-potency drug, which is high-D2/low-serotonergic. Denying any mechanistic relationship to dopaminergic pathways ignores the well-established pathway framework this question is testing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Mesocortical dopamine hypoactivity underlies negative/cognitive symptoms; typical high-D2, low-5-HT2A agents poorly address this domain, explaining SGA's comparative negative-symptom advantage. COMLEX: Persistent negative symptoms despite positive-symptom control is a classic cue to consider a switch to an SGA.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 2 — Positive, Negative, Cognitive, Disorganized & Affective Symptom Domains","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q24","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Analyze D2 receptor blockade and serotonin-dopamine antagonism to predict effects on positive symptoms, negative symptoms, EPS, prolactin, and metabolic risk.","batch":2,"stem":"A positron emission tomography study of a patient taking aripiprazole for schizophrenia shows very high occupancy of striatal D2 receptors, above the roughly 80 percent level at which first-generation antipsychotics usually produce extrapyramidal symptoms. Despite this, the patient has no parkinsonism or dystonia. Which property of aripiprazole best explains this finding?","choices":{"A":"It binds D2 receptors in the mesolimbic tract and spares the striatum","B":"It acts as a partial D2 agonist that keeps some receptor signaling","C":"Its strong antimuscarinic action cancels out the striatal D2 blockade","D":"Its blockade of alpha-1 receptors restores striatal dopamine release","E":"It is cleared within hours, so striatal D2 blockade never persists"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Katzung notes that PET studies with aripiprazole show very high occupancy of D2 receptors, yet the drug does not cause EPS because it is a partial D2 agonist; even at full occupancy it provides some dopaminergic signaling in the nigrostriatal pathway. Binding restricted to mesolimbic receptors contradicts the measured striatal occupancy. Aripiprazole does not have strong antimuscarinic activity; that property protects low-potency agents such as chlorpromazine and thioridazine. Alpha-1 blockade causes orthostasis and has no role in restoring striatal dopamine function. Aripiprazole has a long half-life of about 75 hours, so rapid clearance does not explain the absence of EPS.","boardPrep":"Step 1: first-generation agents need about 60 percent striatal D2 occupancy for efficacy and cause EPS at about 80 percent or more; aripiprazole is a partial D2 agonist with low EPS and prolactin effects. COMLEX: aripiprazole's main movement-related complaint is akathisia-like restlessness rather than parkinsonism, so start at a low dose.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 1 — differentiating the extrapyramidal syndromes, including akathisia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q25","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare high-potency and low-potency typical antipsychotics by EPS, anticholinergic effects, sedation, orthostasis, and QT risk.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with chronic schizophrenia has been maintained on a first-generation antipsychotic requiring a comparatively high milligram dose for equivalent effect for 12 years. He now reports dry mouth, urinary hesitancy, and blurred near vision, in addition to the orthostatic hypotension he has had since starting the drug. Which receptor blockade profile most directly explains this constellation of adverse effects together?","choices":{"A":"Combined D2 and 5-HT2A receptor blockade, typical of second-generation agents","B":"Combined H1 and D2 receptor blockade, typical of high-potency agents","C":"Isolated nigrostriatal D2 blockade without other receptor involvement","D":"Combined 5-HT2A and H1 receptor blockade, typical of atypical agents","E":"Muscarinic and alpha-1 adrenergic receptor blockade, typical of low-potency agents"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Low-potency first-generation agents combine prominent antimuscarinic activity (dry mouth, urinary retention, blurred vision) with alpha-1 adrenergic blockade (orthostatic hypotension), together producing exactly this symptom cluster, consistent with chlorpromazine-type drugs being high in both anticholinergic and hypotensive action. D2/5-HT2A blockade explains EPS and reduced-EPS effects, not anticholinergic or orthostatic symptoms. H1 blockade alone explains sedation, not dry mouth or hypotension. Isolated nigrostriatal D2 blockade explains motor symptoms only, not this autonomic/anticholinergic pattern. Combining 5-HT2A and H1 without adrenergic involvement omits the mechanism actually responsible for his orthostatic symptoms.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low-potency FGAs = high muscarinic + alpha-1 blockade -> anticholinergic triad + orthostasis, the inverse trade-off versus high-potency agents' EPS-predominant profile. COMLEX: Chronic anticholinergic burden in older patients also raises delirium and cognitive-impairment risk, an important geriatric-safety consideration.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 2 — GABA-A Receptor Modulation and Anxiolytic Adverse-Effect Mechanisms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q26","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare high-potency and low-potency typical antipsychotics by EPS, anticholinergic effects, sedation, orthostasis, and QT risk.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man with schizophrenia also has a seizure disorder that began after a traumatic brain injury and is controlled on levetiracetam. Because of cost, his team plans to use a first-generation antipsychotic. Which choice of agent best reflects the potency-related adverse-effect profiles of these drugs?","choices":{"A":"A low-potency agent, since its sedation offsets the seizure risk","B":"A low-potency agent, since its anticholinergic action is protective","C":"A high-potency agent, since low-potency ones are more epileptogenic","D":"Either potency class, since seizure risk is identical across agents","E":"A high-potency agent, since it carries the lowest risk of dystonia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Kaplan and Sadock state that antipsychotics may lower the seizure threshold and that chlorpromazine, thioridazine, and other low-potency drugs are thought to be more epileptogenic than high-potency drugs, a risk that matters most when a patient already has a seizure disorder or brain lesion. They also note that when a first-generation agent is chosen, a high-potency drug is generally favored because low-potency drugs carry more cardiac, hypotensive, epileptogenic, sexual, and allergic adverse effects. Sedation does not protect against seizures. Anticholinergic activity reduces EPS but does not raise the seizure threshold. Seizure risk is not identical across agents, as the potency difference shows. High-potency agents carry the highest, not the lowest, risk of acute dystonia; that is their trade-off.","boardPrep":"Step 1: high-potency first-generation agents such as haloperidol and fluphenazine cause more neurologic adverse effects (EPS, dystonia, NMS); low-potency agents such as chlorpromazine and thioridazine cause more nonneurologic ones (sedation, orthostasis, anticholinergic effects, QT prolongation, seizures). COMLEX: in a patient with epilepsy, avoid low-potency phenothiazines and clozapine where possible and monitor seizure control after any antipsychotic start.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 5 — clozapine's dose-related seizure risk","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q27","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare high-potency and low-potency typical antipsychotics by EPS, anticholinergic effects, sedation, orthostasis, and QT risk.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man with schizophrenia on high-dose thioridazine is found to have a corrected QT interval of 540 ms on a repeat ECG obtained during a routine visit. Which management step is most appropriate given this specific finding?","choices":{"A":"Continue thioridazine at the same dose since QTc values below 600 ms require no action","B":"Increase the thioridazine dose to improve symptom control since QTc monitoring has no clinical utility","C":"Add a Class 1A antiarrhythmic to manage the prolonged QT interval","D":"Discontinue thioridazine and switch to an agent with a lower QT-prolongation risk","E":"Switch to ziprasidone, since its QT-prolonging effect is clinically negligible"},"correct":"D","explanation":"QTc monitoring is considered clinically meaningful once values rise above 500 ms on repeated tracings, and this patient's repeat 540 ms reading on a drug already known for high QT risk warrants discontinuing thioridazine in favor of a lower-risk agent. Continuing at the same dose ignores that his value has already crossed the clinically significant 500 ms threshold. Increasing the dose would worsen, not improve, the QT risk, and the claim that monitoring has no utility contradicts the specific 500 ms threshold at which it does become actionable. Adding a Class 1A antiarrhythmic is explicitly contraindicated with thioridazine because of additive QT-prolonging, quinidine-like effects. Ziprasidone is not an appropriate substitute, since it carries the greatest QT-prolongation risk among the atypical agents and must not be combined with other QT-prolonging drugs; a patient already at 540 ms needs a low-QT-risk agent.","boardPrep":"Step 1: QTc >500 ms on repeated tracings is the actionable threshold for antipsychotic-associated QT prolongation; thioridazine and ziprasidone carry the highest risk among antipsychotics. COMLEX: Never combine thioridazine with other QT-prolonging drugs, including Class 1A/3 antiarrhythmics and pimozide.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 2 — GABA-A Receptor Modulation and Anxiolytic Adverse-Effect Mechanisms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q28","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply antipsychotic selection principles to schizophrenia, acute mania, bipolar depression, psychotic depression, agitation, autism-related irritability, and treatment-resistant schizophrenia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with bipolar I disorder has recurrent depressive episodes but has never tolerated lithium because of chronic kidney disease. Her psychiatrist proposes an antipsychotic-based regimen specifically approved for bipolar depression. Which combination or agent should be selected?","choices":{"A":"Olanzapine plus fluoxetine, or quetiapine monotherapy","B":"Haloperidol plus fluoxetine, or ziprasidone monotherapy","C":"Chlorpromazine plus fluoxetine, or aripiprazole monotherapy","D":"Fluphenazine plus sertraline, or risperidone monotherapy","E":"Clozapine plus fluoxetine, or haloperidol monotherapy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Olanzapine combined with fluoxetine and quetiapine monotherapy are FDA-approved antipsychotic-based treatments for bipolar depression (lurasidone, cariprazine, and lumateperone are newer approved options), making them the appropriate choice for this lithium-intolerant patient. Haloperidol, a first-generation agent, has no bipolar-depression indication, and ziprasidone is approved for mania, not depression. Chlorpromazine is approved for the manic phase, and aripiprazole is used for mania and maintenance rather than bipolar depression. Fluphenazine has no bipolar-depression indication, and risperidone is used for mania rather than depression. Clozapine is reserved for treatment-refractory illness, and haloperidol again lacks a bipolar-depression indication.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Olanzapine-fluoxetine combination and quetiapine monotherapy = the classic antipsychotic-based options approved for bipolar depression (lurasidone, cariprazine, and lumateperone are newer additions), distinct from the broader mania-approved drug list. COMLEX: Lithium intolerance (e.g., renal impairment) is a common clinical trigger for choosing an antipsychotic-based mood-stabilizing strategy instead.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 1 — Evidence-Based Antipsychotic Initiation & Treatment Principles","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q29","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply antipsychotic selection principles to schizophrenia, acute mania, bipolar depression, psychotic depression, agitation, autism-related irritability, and treatment-resistant schizophrenia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man presents with his first psychotic episode, severe agitation, and disorganized behavior requiring inpatient stabilization. His treatment team also notes prominent negative symptoms are likely to emerge as his acute symptoms resolve. Which antipsychotic selection principle should guide his initial regimen?","choices":{"A":"A first-generation high-potency agent, given its faster control of agitation and stronger D2 blockade","B":"Clozapine, given its superior efficacy for both positive and negative symptoms in any first episode","C":"No antipsychotic until negative symptoms are confirmed, using benzodiazepines for agitation meanwhile","D":"A low-potency first-generation agent, given its sedating profile for the severe agitation","E":"A second-generation agent, given comparable positive-symptom efficacy and a better negative-symptom and EPS profile"},"correct":"E","explanation":"For a first-episode patient with anticipated negative symptoms, a second-generation agent is generally preferred, since it offers comparable positive-symptom control with a better track record for negative symptoms, cognition, and lower EPS/tardive dyskinesia risk. A first-generation high-potency agent does not offer this negative-symptom or EPS advantage, making it a less optimal first choice here. Clozapine is reserved for treatment-refractory illness after prior antipsychotic trials fail, not as first-line therapy for a first episode. Withholding all antipsychotic treatment pending confirmed negative symptoms would leave his acute agitation and disorganization untreated, which is inappropriate. Choosing a low-potency agent specifically for its sedating profile does not address either his positive or anticipated negative symptoms and adds unnecessary adverse-effect burden.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Second-generation agents are generally favored first-line for first-episode psychosis given their negative-symptom and EPS advantages over FGAs. COMLEX: First-episode patients need particularly strong psychosocial/case-management support, since denial of illness and nonadherence are common early on.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 1 — Evidence-Based Antipsychotic Initiation & Treatment Principles","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q30","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply antipsychotic selection principles to schizophrenia, acute mania, bipolar depression, psychotic depression, agitation, autism-related irritability, and treatment-resistant schizophrenia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman with schizophrenia has failed substantial, adequate trials of risperidone and olanzapine, each given for over 8 weeks at therapeutic doses without meaningful improvement. Which agent represents the appropriate next step given this treatment history?","choices":{"A":"Aripiprazole","B":"Quetiapine","C":"Chlorpromazine","D":"Clozapine","E":"Haloperidol"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Patients who become refractory to two or three antipsychotic agents given in substantial doses become candidates for clozapine (or high-dose olanzapine), making clozapine the standard next step after this patient's two adequate, high-dose trial failures. Switching to a third standard second-generation agent such as aripiprazole or quetiapine does not reflect the evidence-based escalation to clozapine once refractoriness to two adequate trials is established. A first-generation agent, whether low-potency chlorpromazine or high-potency haloperidol, offers no particular advantage for treatment-refractory illness and was not the class shown to help this population.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Refractoriness to 2-3 adequate antipsychotic trials at substantial doses = the defined threshold for a clozapine trial. COMLEX: 30-50% of such refractory patients respond to clozapine, which is why the increased monitoring risk is considered justified.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 1 — Evidence-Based Antipsychotic Initiation & Treatment Principles","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q31","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Interpret clozapine indications and monitoring needs, including agranulocytosis, myocarditis, seizures, metabolic effects, and treatment-refractory illness.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old man on clozapine for treatment-refractory schizophrenia presents at week 10 of therapy with a new generalized tonic-clonic seizure. There is no history of a prior seizure disorder. Which management approach is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Immediately and permanently discontinue clozapine because of the seizure","B":"Switch him directly to a first-generation antipsychotic without further workup","C":"Reduce the clozapine dose by half and continue monitoring without an anticonvulsant","D":"Withhold any treatment and observe, since a single seizure is not clinically significant on clozapine","E":"Add an anticonvulsant while continuing clozapine, since seizures can usually be controlled"},"correct":"E","explanation":"De novo seizures occur in 2-5% of clozapine-treated patients, and an anticonvulsant is able to control seizures in most cases, allowing clozapine to be continued rather than automatically stopped, unlike the mandatory discontinuation required for agranulocytosis or myocarditis. Permanently discontinuing clozapine is not the standard first response to a single seizure when it can usually be managed with an anticonvulsant, particularly given his treatment-refractory history. Switching antipsychotic classes without addressing the seizure itself abandons an agent that may still be his most effective option. Reducing the dose alone, without an anticonvulsant, is not the described standard management. Observing without intervention ignores that this is a clinically significant new seizure requiring active management.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Clozapine-associated seizures (2-5% incidence) are managed with an anticonvulsant while continuing clozapine in most cases, distinct from myocarditis/agranulocytosis, which mandate discontinuation. COMLEX: This is a key point testable as \"which clozapine adverse effect does NOT require stopping the drug.\"","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — Treatment Resistance, Nonadherence & Barriers to Care","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Interpret clozapine indications and monitoring needs, including agranulocytosis, myocarditis, seizures, metabolic effects, and treatment-refractory illness.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman on clozapine for 3 years, well-controlled on her regimen, suddenly stops taking the medication after running out of her prescription while traveling. Within days she experiences a marked, quick return of psychotic symptoms, worse than her original presentation. Which principle explains this pattern of relapse?","choices":{"A":"Clozapine can be stopped and restarted at any time, since supersensitivity psychosis never follows it","B":"This relapse reflects a new psychiatric process that began by coincidence as the drug ran out","C":"Abrupt discontinuation of clozapine mainly risks agranulocytosis, which emerges once dosing stops","D":"Clozapine should not be stopped abruptly, since this can cause a rapid, severe psychotic relapse","E":"All antipsychotics carry the same abrupt-discontinuation relapse risk as clozapine does"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Clozapine should not be stopped abruptly unless clinically necessary because of adverse effects like myocarditis or agranulocytosis, since discontinuation is specifically associated with a rapid, severe return of psychotic symptoms, distinguishing it from other antipsychotic agents in this respect. Claiming no relapse risk contradicts the well-documented rebound phenomenon. Attributing her relapse to an unrelated process ignores its clear temporal link to stopping the drug. Agranulocytosis is a risk of continued clozapine use, not of stopping it, so it does not explain this discontinuation-triggered relapse. While abrupt discontinuation of any antipsychotic carries some relapse risk, clozapine's rebound is described as more rapid and severe than with other agents, making it a specifically recognized risk rather than a generic class effect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Clozapine discontinuation risk = rapid, severe psychotic relapse, a distinct clinical teaching point from its hematologic/cardiac risks. COMLEX: Counsel patients explicitly never to stop clozapine abruptly on their own, including during travel or supply gaps.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — Treatment Resistance, Nonadherence & Barriers to Care","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q33","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Interpret clozapine indications and monitoring needs, including agranulocytosis, myocarditis, seizures, metabolic effects, and treatment-refractory illness.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman is started on clozapine and, over the following months, gains significant weight with a fasting triglyceride:HDL ratio rising to 4.1. Which monitoring interval is recommended for fasting blood sugar and lipid measurement in a patient on clozapine?","choices":{"A":"Once yearly for fasting glucose and lipids","B":"Every 3 to 6 months for fasting glucose and lipids, not contingent on baseline","C":"When the patient reports hyperglycemia symptoms, for fasting glucose and lipids","D":"Every 3 to 6 months for fasting glucose and lipids, but contingent on an abnormal baseline","E":"Weekly for fasting glucose and lipids, matching the neutrophil-count schedule"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Management of clozapine-associated weight gain, insulin resistance, and dyslipidemia includes monitoring weight at every visit along with fasting blood sugar and lipid measurement at 3- to 6-month intervals, and this patient's elevated triglyceride:HDL ratio (above the 3.5 threshold indicating increased atherosclerotic risk) reflects exactly the abnormality this monitoring schedule is designed to catch early. Once-yearly monitoring is less frequent than recommended and could miss a clinically significant metabolic shift for months. Symptom-triggered-only monitoring misses the often asymptomatic early stages of dyslipidemia and insulin resistance. Restricting monitoring only to patients with an abnormal baseline defeats the purpose of routine surveillance in all clozapine-treated patients. The weekly interval described applies to the unrelated agranulocytosis (ANC) monitoring schedule, not to metabolic monitoring.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Clozapine metabolic monitoring = weight every visit, fasting glucose/lipids every 3-6 months, distinct from the weekly ANC schedule for agranulocytosis. COMLEX: A fasting triglyceride:HDL ratio above 3.5 signals increased atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease risk requiring intervention.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — Treatment Resistance, Nonadherence & Barriers to Care","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q34","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Predict medication adherence and safety implications of oral, orally disintegrating, intramuscular, and long-acting injectable antipsychotic formulations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old man with chronic schizophrenia has had three hospitalizations in the past year, each preceded by a period during which his family reports he \"stopped taking his pills\" without telling anyone. He otherwise has stable housing and a supportive family willing to assist with his care. Which formulation change most directly targets the specific pattern driving his relapses?","choices":{"A":"A long-acting injectable antipsychotic, since it removes reliance on daily self-administered oral dosing that he has repeatedly stopped covertly","B":"An orally disintegrating tablet, since it removes reliance on daily self-administered oral dosing that he has repeatedly stopped covertly","C":"A higher oral daily dose of his current agent, since it removes reliance on daily self-administered oral dosing that he has repeatedly stopped covertly","D":"A second oral agent added to his current regimen, since it removes reliance on daily self-administered oral dosing that he has repeatedly stopped covertly","E":"Referral for inpatient hospitalization, since it removes reliance on daily self-administered oral dosing that he has repeatedly stopped covertly"},"correct":"A","explanation":"His relapse pattern is driven specifically by covert nonadherence to daily oral medication, and a long-acting injectable directly removes that dependence by providing clinician-administered dosing on a fixed schedule, matching the described rationale exactly. An orally disintegrating tablet still requires daily self-administration and does not solve covert nonadherence any more than a standard tablet would. Increasing the oral dose does nothing if he is not taking the medication at all during relapse periods. Adding a second oral agent compounds the same adherence problem rather than solving it. Repeated hospitalization addresses acute crises but does not prevent the next relapse cycle the way a maintenance LAI formulation would.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Repeated relapse from covert nonadherence is the classic clinical indication for switching to an LAI antipsychotic. COMLEX: Good social support (as in this patient) is not itself a substitute for solving a demonstrated adherence problem.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 5 — Acuity, Safety Risk & Initial Workup Across the Psychosis Spectrum","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q35","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Predict medication adherence and safety implications of oral, orally disintegrating, intramuscular, and long-acting injectable antipsychotic formulations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman is being discharged after her first psychotic episode. She and her treatment team are discussing long-term formulation strategy, and she expresses strong anxiety about needles and a clear preference to avoid injections if at all possible, while stating she feels confident she can take a daily pill reliably with reminders from her partner. Which principle should guide the team's formulation decision?","choices":{"A":"A long-acting injectable should be started regardless of her stated preference, since it is generally preferred for a first-episode patient","B":"Her stated preference should be overridden because first-episode patients cannot reliably self-report their own adherence confidence","C":"Patient preference and demonstrated confidence in oral adherence support a trial of oral therapy, given her needle anxiety and reliable home support","D":"An injectable is required by definition for any patient with anxiety about oral formulations, even when a workable oral adherence plan exists","E":"Formulation choice should be made unilaterally by the prescriber without patient input, undermining the collaborative approach that supports long-term engagement"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Formulation decisions should be individualized, incorporating the patient's expressed preferences and their own realistic assessment of their adherence support system, which here favors a trial of oral therapy given her needle anxiety, partner support, and stated confidence. Mandating an LAI regardless of her preference ignores the collaborative, individualized nature of this decision. Dismissing a patient's self-report of adherence confidence as unreliable is not a standard basis for overriding informed patient preference. An injectable is not required simply because a patient reports anxiety about oral formulations; that reasoning is reversed. Excluding patient input entirely from formulation decisions is inconsistent with a collaborative treatment approach and works against long-term engagement in care.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Formulation choice should be individualized and collaborative, weighing adherence risk, support system, and patient preference together, not applied as a rigid default. COMLEX: Shared decision-making around formulation improves therapeutic alliance and long-term treatment engagement.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 5 — Acuity, Safety Risk & Initial Workup Across the Psychosis Spectrum","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl26_q36","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Predict medication adherence and safety implications of oral, orally disintegrating, intramuscular, and long-acting injectable antipsychotic formulations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with chronic schizophrenia is being transitioned from an oral antipsychotic to a long-acting injectable formulation of the same agent. Which safety principle should guide this specific transition?","choices":{"A":"The oral agent should be stopped immediately on the day of the first injection with no overlap period, risking a treatment gap","B":"Oral overlap therapy is typically continued for a defined period while the injectable formulation reaches therapeutic levels","C":"The injectable dose should be calculated without reference to the patient's prior oral dose or response to that agent","D":"No monitoring is needed after the transition since the injectable formulation is inherently safer than the oral form of the same drug","E":"The transition is appropriate for a first psychotic episode but not for a chronic, previously stable patient like this one"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because long-acting injectable formulations take time to reach therapeutic plasma levels, oral overlap therapy is typically continued for a defined period during the transition to avoid a treatment gap and relapse risk. Stopping the oral agent immediately with no overlap risks subtherapeutic coverage while the injectable formulation is still reaching steady state. The injectable dose is calculated with reference to the patient's established oral response and tolerability, not independent of it. The injectable formulation is not inherently free of monitoring needs; ongoing safety and response monitoring remains necessary regardless of route. LAIs are appropriate for both first-episode and chronic, previously stable patients when clinically indicated, not restricted to only first-episode use.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LAI transitions require oral overlap coverage until the injectable reaches therapeutic levels — an important practical safety point distinct from the adherence-benefit rationale for choosing an LAI in the first place. COMLEX: Always verify oral tolerability of the specific agent before committing a patient to a long-acting injectable formulation of it.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 5 — Acuity, Safety Risk & Initial Workup Across the Psychosis Spectrum","isHighYield":false}],"27":[{"id":"sdl27_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute dystonia, akathisia, drug-induced parkinsonism, tardive dyskinesia, neuroleptic malignant syndrome, and serotonin syndrome by timing, mechanism, and clinical findings.","batch":1,"stem":"A 24-year-old man received an intramuscular dose of high-potency haloperidol 12 hours ago for acute agitation. He now presents with sustained, painful contraction of his neck muscles pulling his head to one side. Which adverse effect is this?","choices":{"A":"Akathisia, from subjective inner restlessness","B":"Acute dystonia, from between-dose drops in drug levels","C":"Drug-induced parkinsonism, from chronic nigrostriatal blockade","D":"Tardive dyskinesia, from chronic receptor supersensitivity","E":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome, from rapid receptor blockade"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Acute dystonia presents early (often within hours to a few days) after starting or increasing a high-potency antipsychotic, especially with intramuscular dosing, as sustained muscle contractions such as torticollis, oculogyric crisis, or trismus. Akathisia presents as subjective restlessness rather than a sustained painful contraction. Drug-induced parkinsonism develops over days to weeks and presents as rigidity, bradykinesia, and tremor rather than an acute sustained spasm. Tardive dyskinesia is a late-onset, typically choreoathetoid movement disorder occurring after months of treatment, not hours. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome presents with generalized rigidity, hyperthermia, and autonomic instability, not an isolated focal muscle spasm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Acute dystonia = earliest EPS, hours to days, high-potency/IM route highest risk, young men most affected. COMLEX: Treat urgently with IM/IV diphenhydramine or benztropine to relieve symptoms and prevent care-avoidance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 2 — extends D2/serotonin receptor blockade mechanisms to the specific movement and autonomic syndromes they produce","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute dystonia, akathisia, drug-induced parkinsonism, tardive dyskinesia, neuroleptic malignant syndrome, and serotonin syndrome by timing, mechanism, and clinical findings.","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has been on a high-potency first-generation antipsychotic for 9 months. She now demonstrates darting tongue movements, lip-smacking, and facial grimacing that worsen with stress and disappear during sleep. Which diagnosis best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Acute dystonia","B":"Acute akathisia","C":"Drug-induced parkinsonism","D":"Tardive dyskinesia","E":"Serotonin syndrome"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Tardive dyskinesia is a delayed effect that rarely occurs until after 6 months of treatment, and presents as choreoathetoid orofacial movements including tongue darting, lip-puckering, and facial grimacing, worsened by stress and absent during sleep, matching this presentation exactly. Acute dystonia occurs early, within hours to days, not after 9 months. Akathisia is subjective/objective restlessness, not orofacial choreoathetoid movement. Drug-induced parkinsonism presents as rigidity and bradykinesia rather than these hyperkinetic movements. Serotonin syndrome presents with autonomic instability, myoclonus, and altered mental status, not this isolated late-onset orofacial movement pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tardive dyskinesia = late (>6 months), choreoathetoid orofacial movements, worse with stress, absent in sleep — occurs in 10-20% of patients treated over a year. COMLEX: Clozapine and quetiapine carry the lowest TD risk and are the switch targets once TD is recognized.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 2 — extends D2/serotonin receptor blockade mechanisms to the specific movement and autonomic syndromes they produce","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute dystonia, akathisia, drug-induced parkinsonism, tardive dyskinesia, neuroleptic malignant syndrome, and serotonin syndrome by timing, mechanism, and clinical findings.","batch":1,"stem":"A 30-year-old man on an antipsychotic develops fever of 39.8°C, generalized muscle rigidity, altered mental status, and elevated creatine phosphokinase. His white blood cell count is elevated and blood pressure fluctuates rapidly. Which diagnosis is most consistent with this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","B":"Acute dystonia","C":"Malignant hyperthermia","D":"Serotonin syndrome","E":"Drug-induced parkinsonism"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome presents with muscular rigidity, hyperthermia, autonomic instability with fluctuating blood pressure and pulse, and laboratory findings of elevated WBC count and creatine phosphokinase, exactly matching this presentation, and it is a true medical emergency that can occur at any point during antipsychotic treatment. Acute dystonia is a focal, not generalized, muscle contraction without fever or CPK elevation. Malignant hyperthermia produces the same rigidity, hyperthermia, and CPK rise, but it is triggered by volatile anesthetics or succinylcholine during anesthesia, not by an antipsychotic. Serotonin syndrome requires a serotonergic precipitant (not stated here) and its classic evolution begins with diarrhea and restlessness rather than this rigidity-fever-CPK triad, though the two syndromes can be difficult to distinguish clinically. Drug-induced parkinsonism causes rigidity and bradykinesia but does not cause fever, elevated CPK, or autonomic instability.","boardPrep":"Step 1: NMS = rigidity + hyperthermia + autonomic instability + elevated CPK/WBC, a life-threatening emergency (mortality 10–20% in one Kaplan ch. 21 passage, 20–30% or higher in another, highest with depot agents) requiring immediate antipsychotic discontinuation. COMLEX: Treat with dantrolene, bromocriptine, or amantadine plus supportive cooling and hydration.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 2 — extends D2/serotonin receptor blockade mechanisms to the specific movement and autonomic syndromes they produce","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply management strategies for EPS, including dose reduction, anticholinergics, antihistamines, beta blockers, benzodiazepines, amantadine, VMAT2 inhibitors, and medication switching.","batch":1,"stem":"A 45-year-old man develops subjective restlessness and an inability to sit still shortly after his antipsychotic dose was increased. Beyond reducing the antipsychotic dose, which class of medication is considered most efficacious for this specific symptom?","choices":{"A":"Anticholinergic agents","B":"Beta-blockers","C":"VMAT2 inhibitors","D":"Antihistamines","E":"Dopamine agonists"},"correct":"B","explanation":"For akathisia, the three necessary treatment steps are reducing medication dosage, attempting treatment with appropriate drugs, and considering a medication switch, and beta-adrenergic receptor antagonists such as propranolol are the most efficacious specific drug class for this symptom, although anticholinergics, benzodiazepines, and cyproheptadine may help some patients. Anticholinergic agents are first-line for dystonia and parkinsonism, not the most efficacious specific choice for akathisia. VMAT2 inhibitors (valbenazine, deutetrabenazine) are specifically approved for tardive dyskinesia, not akathisia. Antihistamines like diphenhydramine are used for dystonia and parkinsonism. Dopamine agonists are not a standard akathisia treatment and are instead used adjunctively in neuroleptic malignant syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Beta-blockers (propranolol) = most efficacious specific drug class for akathisia; benzodiazepines and cyproheptadine are secondary options. COMLEX: Middle-aged women are at increased risk of akathisia, with a similar time course to neuroleptic-induced parkinsonism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 1 — links receptor-profile-based drug differentiation to the medication-switching strategy used to manage EPS","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply management strategies for EPS, including dose reduction, anticholinergics, antihistamines, beta blockers, benzodiazepines, amantadine, VMAT2 inhibitors, and medication switching.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient develops shuffling gait, cogwheel rigidity, and stooped posture within 6 weeks of starting a high-potency antipsychotic. Which medication class is the standard first-line treatment for this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Beta-adrenergic antagonists","B":"VMAT2 inhibitors","C":"Alpha-2 adrenergic agonists","D":"Anticholinergic agents","E":"Vitamin E supplementation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This presentation describes neuroleptic-induced parkinsonism, and anticholinergic agents such as benztropine (or alternatively amantadine or diphenhydramine) are the standard treatment, typically withdrawn after 4-6 weeks to assess whether tolerance has developed. Beta-blockers target the tremulous restlessness of akathisia, not the bradykinesia and rigidity of parkinsonism. VMAT2 inhibitors target the choreoathetoid movements of tardive dyskinesia, a distinct movement disorder from parkinsonism. Alpha-2 adrenergic agonists like clonidine are used for akathisia, not parkinsonism. Vitamin E has been studied as an adjunct for tardive dyskinesia, not for parkinsonism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Neuroleptic-induced parkinsonism (5-90 days onset) = anticholinergics (benztropine, trihexyphenidyl), amantadine, or diphenhydramine first-line. COMLEX: Elderly and female patients are at highest risk, and symptoms can persist up to 3 months after antipsychotic withdrawal in this population.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 1 — links receptor-profile-based drug differentiation to the medication-switching strategy used to manage EPS","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply management strategies for EPS, including dose reduction, anticholinergics, antihistamines, beta blockers, benzodiazepines, amantadine, VMAT2 inhibitors, and medication switching.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with schizophrenia develops progressive tardive dyskinesia despite dose reduction of her current first-generation antipsychotic. Beyond a VMAT2 inhibitor, which strategy is a recognized approach to managing her tardive dyskinesia?","choices":{"A":"Switching to a different high-potency first-generation agent at the same dose","B":"Adding an anticholinergic agent to suppress the dyskinetic movements","C":"Switching to clozapine or another second-generation agent with low TD risk","D":"Increasing the current first-generation antipsychotic dose to suppress symptoms","E":"Adding a beta-adrenergic antagonist to target the dyskinetic movements"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Once tardive dyskinesia is recognized, options include reducing or stopping the current antipsychotic, or switching to clozapine or another second-generation drug associated with lower TD risk, since clozapine has minimal TD risk and may even improve preexisting symptoms because of its low D2 affinity and high 5-HT2A antagonism. Switching to a different first-generation agent does not address the underlying D2-blockade mechanism driving TD. Adding an anticholinergic is the treatment for parkinsonism and dystonia, not TD, and can actually exacerbate TD in some cases. Increasing the dose of the causative first-generation drug typically worsens, not improves, TD. Beta-blockers target akathisia, not the choreoathetoid movements of TD.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TD management = reduce/stop the offending DRA or switch to clozapine/an SDA with low TD risk; valbenazine and deutetrabenazine (VMAT2 inhibitors) are FDA-approved pharmacologic options. COMLEX: Paradoxically, withdrawing the antipsychotic often transiently worsens TD symptoms before any improvement.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 1 — links receptor-profile-based drug differentiation to the medication-switching strategy used to manage EPS","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze metabolic, endocrine, cardiovascular, neurologic, and hematologic monitoring needs for antipsychotic therapy.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient is about to start a second-generation antipsychotic. Per FDA recommendations for monitoring patients on this drug class, which baseline assessment should be obtained in addition to weight, BMI, waist circumference, and blood pressure?","choices":{"A":"Fasting plasma glucose and fasting lipid profile, per FDA guidance","B":"Serum lithium level, relevant to lithium therapy monitoring","C":"Thyroid-stimulating hormone, absent from this baseline panel","D":"Urine toxicology screen, absent from this baseline panel","E":"Serum prolactin, an endocrine parameter absent from this panel"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The FDA has recommended that all patients prescribed second-generation antipsychotics be assessed at baseline for personal/family history of obesity, diabetes, dyslipidemia, hypertension, and cardiovascular disease, along with weight/BMI, waist circumference, blood pressure, fasting plasma glucose, and fasting lipid profile. A serum lithium level is not part of antipsychotic metabolic monitoring; it applies to lithium therapy specifically. Thyroid-stimulating hormone is not part of the FDA baseline panel for SGAs. A urine toxicology screen is not part of this specific metabolic monitoring panel. Serum prolactin is a relevant endocrine parameter but is not part of the FDA's specific metabolic-syndrome baseline panel.","boardPrep":"Step 1: FDA SGA metabolic monitoring = personal/family history + weight/BMI + waist circumference + BP + fasting glucose + fasting lipids, obtained at baseline and periodically thereafter. COMLEX: Olanzapine and clozapine carry the highest risk of weight gain and drug-induced diabetes among the second-generation agents.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 5 — extends clozapine-specific hematologic/cardiac monitoring to the broader monitoring needs of antipsychotic therapy as a class","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze metabolic, endocrine, cardiovascular, neurologic, and hematologic monitoring needs for antipsychotic therapy.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient on a dopamine receptor antagonist antipsychotic calls the clinic reporting a new sore throat and fever. Which laboratory test should be obtained immediately given this specific presentation?","choices":{"A":"Fasting lipid panel, addressing metabolic risk","B":"Baseline electrocardiogram, addressing cardiac conduction risk","C":"Serum prolactin level, addressing endocrine effects","D":"Hemoglobin A1C, addressing long-term glycemic control","E":"CBC with differential, addressing hematologic risk"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Although routine CBCs are not indicated for most patients on dopamine receptor antagonists, a sore throat and fever in this context should prompt an immediate CBC to rule out agranulocytosis, a life-threatening hematologic complication occurring in about 1 in 10,000 persons treated with these agents, with a mortality rate up to 30% if missed. A fasting lipid panel addresses metabolic, not hematologic, risk and would not evaluate this acute presentation. A baseline ECG addresses cardiac conduction risk, not this hematologic emergency. A serum prolactin level addresses endocrine effects, not this specific presentation. Hemoglobin A1C addresses long-term glycemic control, not an acute infectious/hematologic concern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sore throat/fever on an antipsychotic = immediate CBC to rule out agranulocytosis, a hematologic emergency requiring drug discontinuation if counts are low. COMLEX: This symptom-triggered CBC differs from clozapine's separate, mandatory scheduled ANC monitoring.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 5 — extends clozapine-specific hematologic/cardiac monitoring to the broader monitoring needs of antipsychotic therapy as a class","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze metabolic, endocrine, cardiovascular, neurologic, and hematologic monitoring needs for antipsychotic therapy.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient is being started on an antipsychotic known to cause cardiac conduction changes. Per standard pretreatment workup, which test should be obtained before initiating this medication?","choices":{"A":"A pretreatment fasting lipid panel","B":"A pretreatment abdominal ultrasound","C":"A pretreatment electrocardiogram","D":"A pretreatment pulmonary function test","E":"A pretreatment renal biopsy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"With agents known to cause cardiac conduction changes, a pretreatment electrocardiogram should be obtained before initiating treatment to establish a baseline and identify any preexisting conduction abnormality that could increase risk. A fasting lipid panel addresses metabolic, not cardiac conduction, risk and does not substitute for an ECG in this specific context. An abdominal ultrasound has no established role in antipsychotic pretreatment workup. A pulmonary function test is not indicated for cardiac conduction risk assessment. A renal biopsy is an invasive procedure with no role in routine antipsychotic pretreatment screening.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pretreatment ECG is specifically indicated for antipsychotics with known cardiac conduction effects (e.g., thioridazine, ziprasidone), to establish a baseline before starting therapy. COMLEX: Lithium and clozapine additionally require pretreatment thyroid, renal, and hepatic function testing given their distinct organ-specific risks.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 -> Obj 5 — extends clozapine-specific hematologic/cardiac monitoring to the broader monitoring needs of antipsychotic therapy as a class","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute dystonia, akathisia, drug-induced parkinsonism, tardive dyskinesia, neuroleptic malignant syndrome, and serotonin syndrome by timing, mechanism, and clinical findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old man taking sertraline for depression is started on tramadol by another provider for back pain. Two days later he presents with diarrhea, agitation, hyperreflexia, myoclonus, and a temperature of 38.9°C. Which diagnosis best explains this evolution of symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Serotonin syndrome","B":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","C":"Acute dystonia","D":"Tardive dyskinesia","E":"Drug-induced parkinsonism"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Serotonin syndrome results from excess serotonergic activity, classically from combining an SSRI with another serotonergic agent, and evolves in order from diarrhea and restlessness to extreme agitation, hyperreflexia, autonomic instability, myoclonus, seizures, and hyperthermia as severity worsens, matching this patient's presentation and the known tramadol-SSRI interaction risk. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome is linked to antipsychotic (dopamine-blocking) drugs, not this serotonergic drug combination, and this patient is not described as taking an antipsychotic. Acute dystonia is a focal muscle contraction from antipsychotic dopamine blockade, unrelated to this serotonergic drug interaction. Tardive dyskinesia is a late-onset antipsychotic-associated movement disorder, not an acute serotonergic reaction. Drug-induced parkinsonism results from antipsychotic D2 blockade, not serotonergic excess, and does not explain this diarrhea/hyperreflexia/myoclonus pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Serotonin syndrome from combining serotonergic agents (SSRI + tramadol, MAOI, or triptan) — diarrhea/agitation/hyperreflexia progressing to myoclonus/hyperthermia is the classic evolution. COMLEX: Treatment is removing the offending agents plus supportive care; cyproheptadine is a specific serotonin antagonist used in more severe cases.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 2 — D2 Receptor Blockade and Serotonin-Dopamine Antagonism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute dystonia, akathisia, drug-induced parkinsonism, tardive dyskinesia, neuroleptic malignant syndrome, and serotonin syndrome by timing, mechanism, and clinical findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman on chronic haloperidol therapy for 3 years develops fine, rhythmic tongue movements and rare finger tapping, first noticed by her daughter but not bothering the patient. There is no rigidity, fever, or autonomic instability. Which diagnosis and typical course best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Drug-induced parkinsonism, since chronic D2 blockade causes this tremor pattern","B":"Tardive dyskinesia, since most mild cases like this one eventually remit over time","C":"Acute dystonia, since this reflects sustained involuntary muscle contraction","D":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome, since involuntary movements can precede fever","E":"Tardive dyskinesia, since the large majority of cases resolve completely within a few weeks"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This minimal, mild choreoathetoid presentation after years of antipsychotic treatment, with movements sometimes missed by the patient themselves, is classic mild tardive dyskinesia, and between 5 and 40 percent of all TD cases eventually remit, with 50-90 percent of mild cases (like this one) remitting compared to a lower rate in severe cases. Drug-induced parkinsonism presents as rigidity and bradykinesia, not choreoathetoid finger/tongue movements, and this patient has no rigidity. Acute dystonia is a sustained painful spasm occurring early in treatment, not this mild chronic presentation after years of therapy. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome requires fever, rigidity, and autonomic instability, none of which are present here. Claiming nearly all cases remit fully within weeks misstates the actual remission statistics, which describe a partial and variable remission rate over a longer timeframe, not a near-universal rapid resolution.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Mild TD often goes unnoticed by the patient; 50-90% of mild cases eventually remit versus a lower overall remission rate (5-40%) across all severities. COMLEX: TD is less likely to remit in elderly patients than in younger patients, an important prognostic point.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 2 — D2 Receptor Blockade and Serotonin-Dopamine Antagonism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute dystonia, akathisia, drug-induced parkinsonism, tardive dyskinesia, neuroleptic malignant syndrome, and serotonin syndrome by timing, mechanism, and clinical findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old man received his first dose of intramuscular haloperidol 18 hours ago in the emergency department for acute agitation. He now has intermittent oculogyric crisis and cannot fully close his jaw. There is no fever, and vital signs are normal aside from mild tachycardia from distress. Which mechanism best explains this specific presentation and its typical trigger?","choices":{"A":"Progressive postsynaptic dopamine receptor supersensitivity from months of chronic blockade, the mechanism proposed for tardive dyskinesia","B":"Excess serotonergic activity from a drug-drug interaction, the mechanism underlying serotonin syndrome","C":"Dopaminergic hyperactivity in the basal ganglia as CNS drug levels fall between doses, the classic dystonia mechanism","D":"Excessively rapid blockade of postsynaptic dopamine receptors, the mechanism proposed for neuroleptic malignant syndrome","E":"Impaired sweating leading to hyperthermia and rigidity, also part of the neuroleptic malignant syndrome mechanism"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Acute dystonia's mechanism is thought to be dopaminergic hyperactivity in the basal ganglia occurring as CNS levels of the antipsychotic begin to fall between doses, consistent with this patient's oculogyric crisis and trismus appearing hours after a single high-potency IM dose. Postsynaptic dopamine receptor supersensitivity from chronic blockade is the proposed mechanism for tardive dyskinesia, a late-onset syndrome, not this acute presentation after a single dose. Excess serotonergic activity describes serotonin syndrome, which is not suggested by this isolated focal dystonic presentation without autonomic or gastrointestinal symptoms. Excessively rapid blockade of postsynaptic dopamine receptors is the proposed mechanism for neuroleptic malignant syndrome, which this patient does not have (no fever, no generalized rigidity, no autonomic instability). Impaired sweating leading to hyperthermia and rigidity also describes the NMS mechanism, not this focal dystonic presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Acute dystonia mechanism = dopaminergic hyperactivity as antipsychotic CNS levels fall between doses, distinct from TD's chronic-supersensitivity mechanism and NMS's rapid-blockade mechanism. COMLEX: Higher incidence in young men given high-potency medications at high doses, particularly via the IM route.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 2 — D2 Receptor Blockade and Serotonin-Dopamine Antagonism","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply management strategies for EPS, including dose reduction, anticholinergics, antihistamines, beta blockers, benzodiazepines, amantadine, VMAT2 inhibitors, and medication switching.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman on a high-potency first-generation antipsychotic develops mild parkinsonian rigidity and bradykinesia. Her psychiatrist starts benztropine and plans to reassess in several weeks. What is the rationale for the planned reassessment timing?","choices":{"A":"Anticholinergics should be withdrawn after 4 to 6 weeks because roughly half of patients develop tolerance","B":"Anticholinergics should be continued indefinitely without reassessment once started","C":"Anticholinergics should be withdrawn after 4 to 6 weeks because their effectiveness lasts 6 weeks total","D":"Anticholinergics should be withdrawn after 24 to 48 hours to assess whether tolerance to parkinsonian effects has developed","E":"Anticholinergics should be continued permanently once dystonia has occurred"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Anticholinergics should be withdrawn after 4-6 weeks specifically to assess whether tolerance to the parkinsonian effects has developed, since about half of patients with neuroleptic-induced parkinsonism require continued treatment while the other half no longer need it once tolerance develops. Continuing indefinitely without ever reassessing ignores that many patients develop tolerance and no longer need the anticholinergic. Claiming anticholinergics are only effective for 6 weeks total misstates the actual rationale, which is testing for tolerance, not a fixed drug-efficacy window. A 24-48 hour reassessment window is far too short to meaningfully assess tolerance development, which occurs over weeks. Never withdrawing an anticholinergic simply because dystonia occurred at some point conflates dystonia (a different syndrome, treated acutely) with the ongoing parkinsonism reassessment described here. This 4-to-6-week interval is from the course reading (Kaplan ch. 21); Katzung (ch. 29, SDL 26) instead suggests attempting withdrawal every 3 to 4 months.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anticholinergic trial-and-withdrawal at 4-6 weeks tests for developed tolerance to parkinsonian EPS; about 50% of patients no longer need continued treatment. COMLEX: In elderly patients, parkinsonian symptoms can persist up to 3 months after antipsychotic discontinuation, requiring longer anticholinergic coverage.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — First- vs Second-Generation Antipsychotic Receptor Profiles","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply management strategies for EPS, including dose reduction, anticholinergics, antihistamines, beta blockers, benzodiazepines, amantadine, VMAT2 inhibitors, and medication switching.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man on a first-generation antipsychotic has developed moderate tardive dyskinesia. His psychiatrist reduces the antipsychotic dose, but his choreoathetoid movements temporarily worsen before any improvement is seen. Which principle explains this temporary worsening?","choices":{"A":"This worsening indicates the diagnosis was incorrect, since true tardive dyskinesia would not fluctuate with dosing changes","B":"Symptom exacerbation is common when the dopamine antagonist is withheld, a known unmasking phenomenon","C":"This worsening indicates the dose reduction was too small, since large dose changes are needed to unmask any underlying movements","D":"This worsening indicates a new drug allergy has developed, typically presenting with skin rash or anaphylactic symptoms","E":"This worsening indicates the movements are now permanent, since transient exacerbation does not precede lasting improvement"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Patients frequently experience an exacerbation of tardive dyskinesia symptoms when the dopamine receptor antagonist is withheld or its dose reduced, a well-recognized phenomenon in TD management, whereas substituting a serotonin-dopamine antagonist may limit abnormal movements without worsening the underlying disease progression. This transient worsening does not indicate a misdiagnosis; it is an expected part of the withdrawal process for TD specifically. It does not indicate the dose reduction was ineffective; rather, it reflects the known unmasking phenomenon that occurs with any meaningful dose reduction. It is not evidence of a new drug allergy, which would present differently (e.g., rash, anaphylaxis). It does not mean the movements are now permanently irreversible; the two- to three-tier remission statistics for TD (5-40% overall, higher in mild cases) show that meaningful improvement remains possible over time.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TD symptoms often transiently worsen when the causative antipsychotic is withdrawn or reduced — a genuine finding, not evidence of misdiagnosis or treatment failure. COMLEX: Switching to an SDA (rather than simply stopping) may limit abnormal movements without this same unmasking exacerbation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — First- vs Second-Generation Antipsychotic Receptor Profiles","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply management strategies for EPS, including dose reduction, anticholinergics, antihistamines, beta blockers, benzodiazepines, amantadine, VMAT2 inhibitors, and medication switching.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old man develops moderate akathisia on his current antipsychotic. His psychiatrist reduces the dose to the minimally adequate level, but his restlessness persists significantly. Beta-blockers are contraindicated because of his asthma. Which of the following is an appropriate next pharmacologic option per the standard management sequence for akathisia?","choices":{"A":"A trial of a VMAT2 inhibitor such as valbenazine, specifically approved for tardive dyskinesia","B":"A trial of high-dose vitamin E supplementation, studied as an adjunct for tardive dyskinesia","C":"A trial of bromocriptine to overcome dopamine receptor blockade, used in neuroleptic malignant syndrome","D":"A trial of a benzodiazepine or anticholinergic agent, a recognized secondary treatment","E":"A trial of dantrolene for muscle relaxation, used in neuroleptic malignant syndrome and malignant hyperthermia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"When beta-blockers cannot be used, benzodiazepines and anticholinergic drugs are recognized secondary options that may benefit some patients with akathisia, fitting into the standard three-step sequence of dose reduction, appropriate drug trial, and consideration of medication switching. VMAT2 inhibitors are specifically approved for tardive dyskinesia, not akathisia. Vitamin E has been studied as an adjunct specifically for tardive dyskinesia, not akathisia. Bromocriptine is used in neuroleptic malignant syndrome to overcome excessive dopamine blockade, not for akathisia management. Dantrolene is a muscle relaxant used in neuroleptic malignant syndrome (and malignant hyperthermia), not a standard akathisia treatment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Akathisia management sequence = dose reduction -> beta-blocker (first-line) -> anticholinergics/benzodiazepines/cyproheptadine as alternatives -> consider switching agents. COMLEX: In some cases of akathisia, no treatment is fully effective, and a change in antipsychotic remains the most reliable option.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — First- vs Second-Generation Antipsychotic Receptor Profiles","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze metabolic, endocrine, cardiovascular, neurologic, and hematologic monitoring needs for antipsychotic therapy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman with schizophrenia has been on olanzapine for 6 months. At her follow-up visit, her psychiatrist orders fasting glucose, a lipid panel, and calculates her BMI, noting she has gained 14 kg. Which underlying rationale best explains why this specific monitoring panel is prioritized for this particular agent?","choices":{"A":"Olanzapine, along with clozapine, carries the highest agranulocytosis risk among antipsychotics","B":"Olanzapine, along with clozapine, carries the highest QT-prolongation risk among antipsychotics","C":"Olanzapine, along with clozapine, requires weekly hematologic monitoring for its first six months","D":"Olanzapine, along with clozapine, is contraindicated in any patient with a family history of diabetes","E":"Olanzapine, along with clozapine, accounts for most cases of antipsychotic-associated weight gain and diabetes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Olanzapine and clozapine appear to account for most cases of antipsychotic-associated weight gain and drug-induced diabetes mellitus among the second-generation agents, which is why fasting glucose, lipid, and weight/BMI monitoring are specifically prioritized for this patient, consistent with her significant weight gain on olanzapine. Agranulocytosis risk is specifically a clozapine issue, not shared by olanzapine to the same degree, and is not the rationale for this particular monitoring panel. QT-prolongation risk is more specifically associated with agents like thioridazine and ziprasidone, not the primary concern driving this metabolic panel for olanzapine. Only clozapine, not olanzapine, requires the mandatory weekly hematologic (ANC) monitoring schedule. A family history of diabetes is a risk factor to document at baseline, not an absolute contraindication to olanzapine use.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Olanzapine and clozapine = highest metabolic risk among SGAs (weight gain, dyslipidemia, diabetes), driving the FDA-recommended baseline and periodic metabolic monitoring panel. COMLEX: Patients with preexisting diabetes on these agents need additional HbA1C and, in some cases, insulin-level monitoring.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 5 — Clozapine Monitoring & Treatment-Refractory Illness","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze metabolic, endocrine, cardiovascular, neurologic, and hematologic monitoring needs for antipsychotic therapy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman on risperidone develops breast enlargement, galactorrhea, and amenorrhea over several months. Which endocrine monitoring parameter is most directly relevant to identifying and managing this specific adverse effect?","choices":{"A":"Fasting lipid panel","B":"Hemoglobin A1C","C":"Serum prolactin level","D":"Thyroid-stimulating hormone","E":"Serum cortisol level"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Blockade of tuberoinfundibular dopamine receptors increases prolactin secretion, causing breast enlargement, galactorrhea, and amenorrhea, exactly as seen here, and risperidone is notably the one second-generation agent still significantly associated with prolactin elevation (unlike most other SDAs). A fasting lipid panel addresses metabolic risk, not this endocrine presentation. Hemoglobin A1C addresses long-term glycemic control, not prolactin-mediated symptoms. Thyroid-stimulating hormone assesses thyroid function, unrelated to this specific dopaminergic mechanism. Serum cortisol assesses adrenal/HPA-axis function, not the tuberoinfundibular dopamine pathway responsible for this presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Risperidone is the SDA exception still significantly linked to hyperprolactinemia; other SDAs are largely prolactin-sparing. COMLEX: Switching to a prolactin-sparing agent such as aripiprazole is the standard management once symptomatic hyperprolactinemia is identified.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 5 — Clozapine Monitoring & Treatment-Refractory Illness","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Analyze metabolic, endocrine, cardiovascular, neurologic, and hematologic monitoring needs for antipsychotic therapy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man is being started on thioridazine, an agent recognized to cause cardiac rhythm changes. Before the first dose, his psychiatrist orders an initial electrocardiogram and schedules follow-up tracings at intervals going forward. Which cardiovascular monitoring principle underlies this specific plan?","choices":{"A":"A pretreatment ECG is obtained routinely for antipsychotics regardless of individual cardiac conduction risk","B":"A pretreatment ECG is mainly useful for comparison after an overdose has occurred","C":"A pretreatment ECG is obtained specifically to screen for structural heart disease","D":"A pretreatment ECG sets a baseline for agents with known conduction risk and guides periodic rechecks","E":"A pretreatment ECG is required specifically for patients already diagnosed with a cardiac arrhythmia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"With agents known to cause cardiac conduction changes such as thioridazine, a pretreatment ECG should be obtained before initiating treatment to establish a baseline, with periodic reassessment as clinically indicated, rather than being a universal requirement for every antipsychotic regardless of individual risk. Claiming ECGs are obtained routinely for all antipsychotics regardless of risk overstates the actual, risk-targeted recommendation. Limiting ECG use to only after an overdose has occurred misses the entire preventive, baseline purpose of pretreatment monitoring. The ECG's purpose here is specifically conduction-risk assessment related to the antipsychotic, not a general screen for unrelated structural heart disease. Restricting pretreatment ECGs only to patients with a preexisting arrhythmia diagnosis ignores that the recommendation is based on the drug's known conduction-changing properties, not the patient's prior cardiac history alone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pretreatment ECG is targeted to antipsychotics with known cardiac conduction risk (thioridazine, ziprasidone), establishing a baseline before treatment with periodic reassessment. COMLEX: This targeted approach mirrors lithium and clozapine's own distinct pretreatment monitoring requirements (thyroid, renal, hepatic, hematologic) for their specific organ-system risks.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 5 — Clozapine Monitoring & Treatment-Refractory Illness","isHighYield":true}],"28":[{"id":"sdl28_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate Cluster A and Cluster B personality disorders using enduring patterns of cognition, affect, interpersonal functioning, impulse control, functional impairment, and deviation from cultural expectations.","batch":1,"stem":"A psychiatry resident is asked to summarize the core distinction between Cluster A and Cluster B personality disorders. Which statement best captures this distinction?","choices":{"A":"Cluster A involves anxious and fearful avoidance; Cluster B involves odd or eccentric cognition and withdrawal","B":"Cluster A involves odd or eccentric cognition and withdrawal; Cluster B involves dramatic and erratic affect with impulsivity","C":"Cluster A and Cluster B are both defined primarily by anxious and fearful interpersonal patterns","D":"Cluster A involves impulse dyscontrol and self-harm; Cluster B involves anxious and fearful avoidance","E":"Cluster A and Cluster B are distinguished mainly by age of onset and by gender ratio"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cluster A personality disorders (paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal) are characterized by odd, eccentric cognition and social detachment, while Cluster B disorders (antisocial, borderline, histrionic, narcissistic) are characterized by dramatic, emotional, erratic affect and poor impulse control, both representing enduring patterns that deviate from cultural expectations and impair functioning. Reversing the pairing misassigns each cluster's defining feature. Anxious, fearful patterns describe Cluster C, not Cluster A or B. Impulse dyscontrol is the Cluster B, not Cluster A, hallmark, and social withdrawal is the Cluster A, not Cluster B, hallmark. Age of onset is not the distinguishing feature between the clusters; both are diagnosed based on enduring adult patterns, not a specific onset age difference.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cluster A = \"Weird\" (odd/eccentric), Cluster B = \"Wild\" (dramatic/erratic), Cluster C = \"Worried\" (anxious/fearful) — the classic three-cluster mnemonic. COMLEX: All personality disorder diagnoses require the pattern be pervasive, stable, and traceable to adolescence or early adulthood, not explained by another condition or substance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 2 — extends the empathy/identification framework and its link to borderline personality disorder pathology to the broader Cluster A/B differentiation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate Cluster A and Cluster B personality disorders using enduring patterns of cognition, affect, interpersonal functioning, impulse control, functional impairment, and deviation from cultural expectations.","batch":1,"stem":"A 26-year-old man has a lifelong pattern of unusual behavior, poor relationships, and beliefs or traits that cannot be attributed to any co-occurring illness or drug effect. Which requirement applies broadly across all Cluster A and Cluster B personality disorder diagnoses?","choices":{"A":"The pattern must not be better accounted for by another mental illness or drug use","B":"The pattern must first appear after age 40, once adult personality has fully matured","C":"The pattern must include at least one documented psychiatric hospitalization","D":"The pattern must be confined to occupational settings such as the workplace","E":"The pattern must resolve completely within one year of its first appearance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Across both Cluster A and Cluster B personality disorders, a shared diagnostic requirement is that the unusual behaviors or traits are not explained by another condition or substance, distinguishing personality pathology from secondary psychiatric presentations. Personality disorders characteristically begin by adolescence or early adulthood, not after age 40, so requiring first appearance after age 40 misstates the actual onset pattern. A documented psychiatric hospitalization is not a diagnostic requirement for any personality disorder. Personality disorders by definition affect functioning across multiple settings (social, occupational, personal), not one setting exclusively. Personality disorders are enduring and pervasive by definition, not conditions expected to resolve completely within a year.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Personality disorder diagnosis requires an enduring, pervasive pattern beginning by adolescence/early adulthood, causing distress or impairment, and not better explained by another condition or substance. COMLEX: This exclusion criterion is what separates a true personality disorder from a substance-induced or medical-condition-related personality change.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 2 — extends the empathy/identification framework and its link to borderline personality disorder pathology to the broader Cluster A/B differentiation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate Cluster A and Cluster B personality disorders using enduring patterns of cognition, affect, interpersonal functioning, impulse control, functional impairment, and deviation from cultural expectations.","batch":1,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman has intense, unstable relationships, marked mood swings, and impulsive self-destructive behavior. Her presentation is far more consistent with a Cluster B disorder than a Cluster A disorder based on which core feature?","choices":{"A":"Prominent social withdrawal and flat affect","B":"Prominent magical thinking and ideas of reference","C":"Prominent suspiciousness and distrust of others","D":"Prominent affective instability and impulsivity","E":"Prominent eccentric speech and behavior"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This patient's intense mood swings, unstable relationships, and impulsive self-destructive behavior reflect the affective instability and impulse dyscontrol that define Cluster B, distinguishing her from the odd cognition and social detachment that define Cluster A. Social withdrawal and flat affect describe schizoid (Cluster A) presentations, not this patient's pattern. Magical thinking and ideas of reference describe schizotypal (Cluster A) presentations. Suspiciousness and distrust describe paranoid (Cluster A) presentations. Eccentric speech and behavior are Cluster A features generally, not what distinguishes this patient's Cluster B presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cluster B's defining axis is affective instability + impulse dyscontrol, versus Cluster A's odd cognition + social detachment axis. COMLEX: Recognizing which cluster-defining feature dominates a vignette is the fastest way to narrow a personality disorder differential before considering the specific diagnosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 2 — extends the empathy/identification framework and its link to borderline personality disorder pathology to the broader Cluster A/B differentiation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply diagnostic features to distinguish paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal, antisocial, borderline, histrionic, and narcissistic personality disorders in clinical cases.","batch":1,"stem":"A 30-year-old man refuses to confide in coworkers, doubts the loyalty of his friends, and interprets a colleague's harmless comment as a hidden insult, leading to a prolonged grudge. He has no hallucinations, delusions, or disorganized speech. Which personality disorder best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Paranoid personality disorder","B":"Schizoid personality disorder","C":"Schizotypal personality disorder","D":"Antisocial personality disorder","E":"Narcissistic personality disorder"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Paranoid personality disorder is characterized by pervasive distrust and suspiciousness, reluctance to confide in others for fear it will be used against them, doubting others' loyalty, and reading hidden threatening meanings into benign remarks while holding prolonged grudges, exactly matching this presentation, without any psychotic symptoms. Schizoid personality disorder is characterized by disinterest in relationships and flat affect, not active suspicion or grudge-holding. Schizotypal personality disorder requires odd behavior, magical thinking, or perceptual disturbances beyond simple suspiciousness, which are not described here. Antisocial personality disorder is characterized by disregard for others' rights and deceitfulness, not primarily suspicion. Narcissistic personality disorder is characterized by grandiosity and need for admiration, not primarily distrust of others.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Paranoid PD = pervasive distrust/suspiciousness without psychosis; overlaps conceptually with schizotypal PD but lacks the odd behavior/perceptual disturbance. COMLEX: Psychotherapy focused on supporting rather than challenging the patient's beliefs is the standard approach, since confrontation tends to damage the therapeutic alliance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 1 — applies DSM-5 diagnostic logic (syndrome, duration, impairment, exclusion) to distinguishing these seven specific personality disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply diagnostic features to distinguish paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal, antisocial, borderline, histrionic, and narcissistic personality disorders in clinical cases.","batch":1,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman has a pattern of frantic efforts to avoid abandonment, unstable and intense relationships, an unstable self-image, chronic feelings of emptiness, and recurrent self-harming behavior. Which personality disorder best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Histrionic personality disorder","B":"Narcissistic personality disorder","C":"Borderline personality disorder","D":"Antisocial personality disorder","E":"Schizotypal personality disorder"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Borderline personality disorder is defined by frantic efforts to avoid abandonment, unstable and intense relationships, unstable self-image, chronic emptiness, and recurrent suicidal or self-harming behavior, matching this presentation directly, along with the characteristic splitting defense of seeing others as all good or all bad. Histrionic personality disorder centers on attention-seeking and excessive emotionality rather than abandonment fears and self-harm as the core features. Narcissistic personality disorder centers on grandiosity and need for admiration, not abandonment fears or self-harm. Antisocial personality disorder centers on disregard for others' rights and deceitfulness, not this pattern of unstable relationships and self-harm. Schizotypal personality disorder centers on odd cognition and perceptual disturbance, not this affective/relational instability pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Borderline PD = fear of abandonment + unstable relationships/self-image + impulsive self-harm + splitting, the classic \"stable instability\" pattern. COMLEX: DBT (dialectical behavior therapy) is the best-studied psychotherapy for borderline personality disorder.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 1 — applies DSM-5 diagnostic logic (syndrome, duration, impairment, exclusion) to distinguishing these seven specific personality disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply diagnostic features to distinguish paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal, antisocial, borderline, histrionic, and narcissistic personality disorders in clinical cases.","batch":1,"stem":"A 40-year-old man believes he is uniquely brilliant and deserving of special treatment, requires excessive admiration, exploits colleagues for his own advancement, and reacts with intense anger when criticized. Which personality disorder best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Antisocial personality disorder","B":"Narcissistic personality disorder","C":"Histrionic personality disorder","D":"Borderline personality disorder","E":"Paranoid personality disorder"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Narcissistic personality disorder is characterized by grandiose self-image, need for excessive admiration, sense of entitlement, exploitation of others for personal advancement, and fragile self-esteem that lashes out when criticized, matching this presentation exactly. Antisocial personality disorder centers on disregard for societal norms and the rights of others generally, including deceit and illegal behavior, not primarily grandiosity and need for admiration. Histrionic personality disorder centers on attention-seeking and excessive emotionality, not grandiosity and entitlement specifically. Borderline personality disorder centers on abandonment fears and affective instability, not this grandiose, exploitative pattern. Paranoid personality disorder centers on suspicion and distrust, not grandiosity or need for admiration.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Narcissistic PD = grandiosity + need for admiration + entitlement + lack of empathy + fragile self-esteem vulnerable to criticism. COMLEX: Psychoanalytic and group therapy are the typical psychotherapy approaches; lithium or SSRIs may be used adjunctively for associated mood symptoms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 10 -> Obj 1 — applies DSM-5 diagnostic logic (syndrome, duration, impairment, exclusion) to distinguishing these seven specific personality disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare Cluster A personality disorders with psychotic disorders by distinguishing suspiciousness, social detachment, eccentricity, magical thinking, ideas of reference, altered perception, and functional impairment.","batch":1,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman has odd beliefs, ideas of reference, and unusual speech patterns spanning many years, but has never had a sustained episode of frank delusions or hallucinations requiring hospitalization. Which feature most reliably distinguishes her presentation as schizotypal personality disorder rather than schizophrenia?","choices":{"A":"The absence of sustained psychosis, with any psychotic symptoms brief and fragmentary","B":"The presence of ideas of reference, which point away from a psychotic disorder","C":"Her age, since schizophrenia is rarely diagnosed before age 30","D":"The presence of unusual speech, a feature exclusive to personality disorders","E":"The absence of any genetic link between schizotypal disorder and schizophrenia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Patients with schizotypal personality disorder are distinguished from those with schizophrenia by the absence of sustained psychosis; if psychotic symptoms do appear, they are brief and fragmentary rather than the prolonged, defining psychotic episodes of schizophrenia. Ideas of reference can occur in both schizotypal personality disorder and schizophrenia, so their mere presence does not distinguish the two. Schizophrenia has no such age-30 diagnostic cutoff; it can be diagnosed at various ages. Unusual speech also occurs in schizophrenia (e.g., disorganized speech), so it is not exclusive to schizotypal personality disorder. Schizotypal personality disorder is actually genetically linked to schizophrenia, not free of any genetic connection, so this option misstates the relationship entirely.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Schizotypal PD is on the schizophrenia spectrum genetically, but is distinguished from schizophrenia clinically by the absence of sustained, prolonged psychotic episodes. COMLEX: When brief psychotic symptoms do occur in schizotypal PD, they should prompt reassessment rather than automatic reclassification as schizophrenia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 4 — extends the medical/psychiatric mimics-of-psychosis framework to Cluster A personality pathology as a psychosis-spectrum mimic","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare Cluster A personality disorders with psychotic disorders by distinguishing suspiciousness, social detachment, eccentricity, magical thinking, ideas of reference, altered perception, and functional impairment.","batch":1,"stem":"A 24-year-old man avoids social interaction, has a flat affect, and takes pleasure in very few activities, but denies any suspiciousness, magical thinking, or perceptual disturbances. Which functional feature helps confirm this presentation is schizoid personality disorder rather than the negative symptoms of schizophrenia?","choices":{"A":"The presence of flat affect, which points to a personality disorder over schizophrenia","B":"The absence of any overlap between schizoid traits and negative symptoms","C":"His age, since schizophrenia rarely begins before the age of 25 in men","D":"The presence of social avoidance, which is absent from schizophrenia","E":"A lifelong, stable pattern without decline from a higher prior level of functioning"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Schizoid personality disorder overlaps with the negative symptoms of schizophrenia (blunted affect, social withdrawal), but is distinguished as a personality disorder by being a lifelong, stable pattern rather than representing a deterioration from a previous, higher level of functioning, which is more characteristic of a psychotic illness like schizophrenia. Flat affect is not exclusive to personality disorders; it is a hallmark negative symptom of schizophrenia as well, so its mere presence does not distinguish the two. The two conditions do overlap substantially in their negative-symptom-like presentation, so claiming complete absence of overlap misstates the actual clinical relationship. Peak onset of schizophrenia in men is 10 to 25 years (Kaplan ch. 5), or late teens to early 20s (First Aid 2025 p577), so his age does not argue against it. Social avoidance also occurs in schizophrenia (negative symptoms) and other conditions, so it is not exclusive to schizoid personality disorder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Schizoid PD overlaps with schizophrenia's negative symptoms but is a stable, lifelong trait pattern rather than a deterioration from a prior baseline — a key differentiator for any negative-symptom-like presentation. COMLEX: Schizoid PD is also distinguished from autism spectrum disorder by autism's more severe social impairment and stereotyped behaviors/interests.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 4 — extends the medical/psychiatric mimics-of-psychosis framework to Cluster A personality pathology as a psychosis-spectrum mimic","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare Cluster A personality disorders with psychotic disorders by distinguishing suspiciousness, social detachment, eccentricity, magical thinking, ideas of reference, altered perception, and functional impairment.","batch":1,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman has a lifelong pattern of extreme suspiciousness of others' motives, distrust of her partner's fidelity, and reluctance to confide in anyone, but has never experienced eccentric behavior, odd speech, or magical thinking. Which feature distinguishes her presentation as paranoid personality disorder rather than schizotypal personality disorder?","choices":{"A":"The presence of suspiciousness, which schizotypal personality disorder does not include","B":"Her age, since schizotypal personality disorder is rarely diagnosed after age 30","C":"The absence of any genetic link between paranoid and schizotypal personality disorders","D":"The absence of odd behavior, eccentricity, or magical thinking beyond her suspiciousness","E":"The presence of relationship difficulties, which are specific to paranoid personality disorder"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Patients with paranoid personality disorder are suspicious but lack the odd behavior, eccentric speech, and magical thinking that characterize patients with schizotypal personality disorder, making the absence of these additional oddities the key distinguishing feature here. Suspiciousness itself is not exclusive to paranoid personality disorder; schizotypal personality disorder also includes suspiciousness/paranoia as one of its criteria, so its mere presence does not distinguish the two. There is no age-30 diagnostic cutoff for schizotypal personality disorder. Paranoid and schizotypal personality disorders do overlap conceptually and share some genetic and clinical features, so claiming a complete absence of any link misstates their actual relationship. Relationship difficulties occur in both disorders, not paranoid personality disorder exclusively, so this does not serve as a distinguishing feature.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Paranoid PD = suspicion without the odd behavior/magical thinking/eccentricity of schizotypal PD; the two disorders overlap and are sometimes difficult to fully separate clinically. COMLEX: Both disorders can occur in the same patient, and clinicians should still distinguish which features dominate the presentation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 -> Obj 4 — extends the medical/psychiatric mimics-of-psychosis framework to Cluster A personality pathology as a psychosis-spectrum mimic","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze defense mechanisms commonly associated with Cluster A and Cluster B presentations, including projection, splitting, denial, displacement, reaction formation, regression, and intellectualization.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with paranoid personality disorder accuses her therapist of secretly judging her, when in fact the therapist has expressed no such judgment. Which defense mechanism does this represent?","choices":{"A":"Splitting","B":"Projection","C":"Displacement","D":"Reaction formation","E":"Intellectualization"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In projection, patients attribute their own unacknowledged feelings to others, and paranoid personality disorder is classically associated with this defense, exactly as this patient attributes her own unrecognized judgmental feelings to her therapist. Splitting involves dividing people into all-good or all-bad categories, which is not what is described here (no evidence of idealization/devaluation). Displacement redirects an emotion from its original target onto a safer substitute, not attributing one's own feeling to another person. Reaction formation involves converting an unacceptable impulse into its opposite, not attributing a feeling to another person. Intellectualization involves using excessive abstract thinking to avoid emotional engagement, not misattributing feelings to others.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Projection = classic defense of paranoid personality disorder; the clinician's technique of \"counterprojection\" (acknowledging the patient's perceptions without confirming or arguing) is the standard therapeutic response. COMLEX: Confrontation of a paranoid patient's projection typically damages rapport and should be avoided.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — extends the transference/countertransference defense-mechanism framework to the broader defenses seen in Cluster A/B presentations","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze defense mechanisms commonly associated with Cluster A and Cluster B presentations, including projection, splitting, denial, displacement, reaction formation, regression, and intellectualization.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with borderline personality disorder describes one nurse on the inpatient unit as \"the only one who truly understands me\" while calling another nurse \"completely incompetent and uncaring,\" despite both providing similar care. Which defense mechanism does this represent?","choices":{"A":"Projection","B":"Displacement","C":"Regression","D":"Denial","E":"Splitting"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In splitting, the patient divides people toward whom they are or have been ambivalent into purely good or purely bad categories, exactly as this patient idealizes one nurse while uniformly disparaging another despite similar care, a defense classically associated with borderline personality disorder. Projection involves attributing one's own feelings to another person, not dividing people into good/bad categories. Displacement involves redirecting an emotion from its original target to a safer substitute, which is not what is happening here. Regression involves reverting to an earlier developmental stage of functioning, not polarized views of two different people. Denial involves refusing to acknowledge an external reality, not this polarized categorization of two people.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Splitting = classic borderline personality disorder defense, seeing others (and the self) as all good or all bad; highly disruptive on inpatient units when staff are split into idealized and devalued camps. COMLEX: Staff should anticipate splitting, discuss it as a team, and gently confront the patient that no one is entirely good or bad.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — extends the transference/countertransference defense-mechanism framework to the broader defenses seen in Cluster A/B presentations","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze defense mechanisms commonly associated with Cluster A and Cluster B presentations, including projection, splitting, denial, displacement, reaction formation, regression, and intellectualization.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with narcissistic personality disorder, when confronted about hurting a close friend's feelings, responds with a lengthy, detached, abstract discussion of interpersonal ethics and philosophy rather than expressing any personal remorse or emotional reaction. Which defense mechanism does this represent?","choices":{"A":"Splitting","B":"Reaction formation","C":"Intellectualization","D":"Displacement","E":"Rationalization"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Intellectualization involves using excessive abstract or theoretical thinking to distance oneself from an emotionally threatening situation, exactly as this patient retreats into abstract philosophical discussion rather than engaging with the emotional reality of having hurt his friend. Splitting involves dividing people into all-good or all-bad categories, not retreating into abstract reasoning. Reaction formation involves converting an unacceptable impulse into its behavioral opposite, which is not what is described here. Displacement involves redirecting an emotion from its original target onto a safer substitute person or object, not avoiding the emotion through abstraction. Rationalization offers self-serving, logical-sounding excuses to justify a behavior, whereas this patient retreats into abstract theory to avoid the emotion altogether.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Intellectualization = using abstract, theoretical language to avoid engaging with an emotionally threatening reality, distinct from isolation of affect, which recalls the event's facts in detail while stripping away the associated emotion. COMLEX: This defense is common in narcissistic personality disorder, where genuine emotional engagement threatens the patient's fragile self-esteem.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 -> Obj 3 — extends the transference/countertransference defense-mechanism framework to the broader defenses seen in Cluster A/B presentations","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate Cluster A and Cluster B personality disorders using enduring patterns of cognition, affect, interpersonal functioning, impulse control, functional impairment, and deviation from cultural expectations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old man has two coexisting patterns: pervasive distrust of others with prolonged grudges (present since adolescence), and separately, episodes of intense anger and impulsive self-harm following perceived abandonment by romantic partners (also since adolescence). His psychiatrist notes both a Cluster A and a Cluster B pattern may be present. Which combination of features supports this dual formulation?","choices":{"A":"Suspiciousness and distrust as a Cluster C pattern, plus abandonment-driven instability and self-harm as a Cluster A pattern","B":"A single pattern of odd cognition that accounts for both the distrust and the self-harm","C":"Suspiciousness and distrust as a Cluster A pattern, plus abandonment-driven instability and self-harm as a Cluster B pattern","D":"A single pattern of impulse dyscontrol that accounts for both the distrust and the self-harm","E":"Neither pattern, since personality disorders from different clusters cannot coexist in one patient"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This patient displays two genuinely distinct, coexisting symptom clusters: pervasive suspiciousness and distrust (the Cluster A, paranoid-type pattern) and separately, affective instability with impulsive self-harm tied to abandonment fears (the Cluster B, borderline-type pattern), and personality disorders can and do co-occur, supporting a dual formulation. Reversing which pattern belongs to which cluster misassigns the defining features. Suspiciousness/distrust is not explained by odd cognition alone in a way that also accounts for his abandonment-triggered self-harm; these are mechanistically distinct symptom domains. Impulse dyscontrol alone does not explain his separate, enduring suspiciousness pattern, which is not primarily an impulse-control problem. Personality disorders frequently co-occur in the same patient (comorbidity is common), so claiming they cannot coexist is incorrect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Personality disorder comorbidity is common and clinically important; a patient can meet criteria for disorders in more than one cluster simultaneously. COMLEX: When formulating a personality disorder differential, look for which specific symptom domains map to which cluster rather than forcing a single diagnosis to explain all findings.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — Empathy, Identification & Borderline Personality Pathology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate Cluster A and Cluster B personality disorders using enduring patterns of cognition, affect, interpersonal functioning, impulse control, functional impairment, and deviation from cultural expectations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman avoids close relationships, takes pleasure in almost no activities, and is unbothered by others' criticism of her, though she has never displayed odd speech, magical thinking, or affective instability. Her presentation is most consistent with which cluster, and why?","choices":{"A":"Cluster A, because her presentation reflects social detachment and blunted emotional response","B":"Cluster B, because her presentation reflects social detachment and blunted emotional response","C":"Cluster A, because her presentation reflects impulsive and dramatic behavior","D":"Cluster B, because her presentation reflects magical thinking and eccentric cognition","E":"Neither cluster, because indifference to criticism does not occur in personality disorders"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This presentation of social detachment, anhedonia, and indifference to others' opinions (without odd cognition, magical thinking, or affective instability) is most consistent with the schizoid pattern within Cluster A, which is defined by odd cognition and social withdrawal rather than the dramatic, erratic affect of Cluster B. Assigning this presentation to Cluster B misapplies that cluster's defining feature (affective instability/impulsivity), which is absent here. Describing this patient as having impulsive, dramatic behavior contradicts the stem, which describes detachment and blunted response, not impulsivity. Magical thinking and eccentric cognition are not described in this stem and are also not Cluster B features. Indifference to criticism is actually a described feature of schizoid personality disorder specifically (being \"hard to please\" and \"unbothered by others' comments\"), so claiming it cannot occur in any personality disorder is incorrect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Schizoid PD (Cluster A) = social detachment + restricted emotional expression + indifference to praise or criticism, without the odd perceptual/cognitive features of schizotypal PD. COMLEX: Distinguish schizoid PD from avoidant PD (Cluster C) — avoidant patients desire relationships but fear rejection, while schizoid patients genuinely lack interest in closeness.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — Empathy, Identification & Borderline Personality Pathology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate Cluster A and Cluster B personality disorders using enduring patterns of cognition, affect, interpersonal functioning, impulse control, functional impairment, and deviation from cultural expectations.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man has a lifelong pattern of exploiting others, disregarding rules, and lacking remorse after harming people for personal gain, alongside superficial charm. His psychiatrist notes this fits Cluster B based on which specific functional domain, distinct from the affective instability seen in borderline personality disorder?","choices":{"A":"Odd cognition and eccentric behavior across settings","B":"Impulse dyscontrol and disregard for the rights of others","C":"Social detachment and restricted emotional expression","D":"Anxious and fearful avoidance of social situations and criticism","E":"Magical thinking and ideas of reference"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This patient's exploitation of others, disregard for rules, and lack of remorse reflect the impulse dyscontrol and disregard for others' rights that characterize antisocial personality disorder, a distinct Cluster B functional domain from the affective instability and abandonment fears that define borderline personality disorder, even though both fall within Cluster B. Odd cognition and eccentric behavior are Cluster A features, not what is described in this antisocial presentation. Social detachment and restricted emotional expression are also Cluster A (schizoid) features, not antisocial features. Anxious, fearful avoidance describes Cluster C, not this patient's exploitative, remorseless pattern. Magical thinking and ideas of reference are schizotypal (Cluster A) features, unrelated to this antisocial presentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cluster B contains distinct functional domains — antisocial PD's impulse dyscontrol/disregard for others differs from borderline PD's affective instability/abandonment fears, even though both share the cluster's general \"dramatic/erratic\" label. COMLEX: Antisocial PD requires a documented conduct disorder history before age 15 and age ≥18 at diagnosis — the CC/AA mnemonic (conduct disorder in children, antisocial PD in adults).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — Empathy, Identification & Borderline Personality Pathology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply diagnostic features to distinguish paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal, antisocial, borderline, histrionic, and narcissistic personality disorders in clinical cases.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman constantly seeks to be the center of attention, uses her physical appearance to draw notice, displays rapidly shifting and shallow emotions, and speaks in a vague, impressionistic way that lacks detail. She denies self-harm, abandonment fears, or grandiosity. Which personality disorder best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Borderline personality disorder","B":"Narcissistic personality disorder","C":"Antisocial personality disorder","D":"Schizotypal personality disorder","E":"Histrionic personality disorder"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Histrionic personality disorder is characterized by excessive attention-seeking, using physical appearance to draw attention, rapidly shifting and shallow emotions, and vague, impressionistic speech lacking detail, matching this presentation, and importantly this patient explicitly lacks the self-harm and abandonment fears of borderline personality disorder or the grandiosity of narcissistic personality disorder. Borderline personality disorder is ruled out by her explicitly denying self-harm and abandonment fears, which are its core features. Narcissistic personality disorder is ruled out by her explicitly denying grandiosity, its core feature. Antisocial personality disorder centers on disregard for others' rights and deceit, not attention-seeking through appearance and shallow emotionality. Schizotypal personality disorder centers on odd cognition and perceptual disturbance, not this attention-seeking, dramatic pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Histrionic PD = attention-seeking + seductive/provocative behavior + shallow, rapidly shifting emotions + vague speech lacking detail; distinguishing it from borderline PD (which adds self-harm/abandonment fears) and narcissistic PD (which adds grandiosity) is a classic board distinction. COMLEX: Psychoanalytic therapy is the typical treatment approach for histrionic personality disorder.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — DSM-5 Diagnostic Logic Applied to Personality Pathology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply diagnostic features to distinguish paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal, antisocial, borderline, histrionic, and narcissistic personality disorders in clinical cases.","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old man has committed several thefts for financial gain, always careful to avoid arrest, but shows no evidence of a rigid, pervasive pattern of deceit, impulsivity, aggression, or disregard for others across multiple life domains, and had no documented conduct disorder before age 15. Applying DSM-5 diagnostic logic, which conclusion is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Antisocial personality disorder, since repeated theft for gain meets the core criteria","B":"Narcissistic personality disorder, since theft for gain reflects a sense of entitlement","C":"Criminal behavior without a personality disorder, since pervasive maladaptive traits are absent","D":"Borderline personality disorder, since repeated theft reflects impulsive self-damaging behavior","E":"Kleptomania, since the thefts form a recurrent pattern he cannot resist"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Antisocial personality disorder differs from mere illegal behavior in that it involves many areas of a person's life through rigid, maladaptive, and persistent personality traits, plus a documented conduct disorder history before age 15; when illegal behavior is only for gain without these pervasive traits, DSM-5 classifies it as criminal behavior not associated with a personality disorder, exactly fitting this patient. Diagnosing antisocial personality disorder based on theft alone ignores the requirement for pervasive personality traits and the conduct-disorder-before-15 criterion, neither of which is present here. Narcissistic and borderline personality disorder diagnoses require their own specific core features (grandiosity, or affective instability/abandonment fears respectively), not simply a theft history. Kleptomania is excluded because these thefts are committed for financial gain, whereas kleptomanic thefts are of objects not needed for personal use or monetary value. Automatically equating any illegal behavior with antisocial personality disorder ignores DSM-5's explicit distinction between pervasive personality pathology and situational criminal conduct.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Antisocial PD requires more than illegal behavior for gain — it requires a pervasive pattern across life domains plus documented conduct disorder before age 15 (CC/AA mnemonic). COMLEX: This distinction matters clinically and legally, since not every person who commits a crime meets criteria for a personality disorder.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — DSM-5 Diagnostic Logic Applied to Personality Pathology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply diagnostic features to distinguish paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal, antisocial, borderline, histrionic, and narcissistic personality disorders in clinical cases.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old man commits a burglary. His history shows high novelty-seeking behavior alongside low harm-avoidance, no fear of consequences, and a pattern of exploiting others without guilt across many years, beginning with a documented conduct disorder at age 12. Which personality disorder is most consistent with this specific temperament pattern?","choices":{"A":"Borderline personality disorder","B":"Antisocial personality disorder","C":"Narcissistic personality disorder","D":"Histrionic personality disorder","E":"Paranoid personality disorder"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Antisocial personality disorder is associated with high novelty-seeking and low harm-avoidance temperament, along with the required documented conduct disorder before age 15 and a pervasive pattern of exploitation without guilt, all present in this patient. Although individuals with borderline personality disorder can sometimes commit crimes, they tend to display high novelty-seeking with high, not low, harm-avoidance, distinguishing them temperamentally from antisocial personality disorder. Narcissistic personality disorder rarely involves serious criminality of this kind as a core feature. Histrionic personality disorder also rarely includes serious criminality and aggressiveness as a core feature. Paranoid personality disorder centers on suspicion and distrust, not this exploitative, criminally-patterned temperament profile.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Antisocial PD temperament = high novelty-seeking + low harm-avoidance, versus borderline PD's high novelty-seeking + high harm-avoidance when criminality is present — a subtle but testable temperament-based distinction. COMLEX: Narcissistic, histrionic, and paranoid personality disorders rarely feature serious criminality, helping rule them out when significant illegal behavior is central to the presentation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — DSM-5 Diagnostic Logic Applied to Personality Pathology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare Cluster A personality disorders with psychotic disorders by distinguishing suspiciousness, social detachment, eccentricity, magical thinking, ideas of reference, altered perception, and functional impairment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has been described since early adulthood as distrustful, quick to suspect coworkers of using him, and unwilling to forgive slights. He often wonders whether his wife is faithful, but when asked directly he acknowledges he has no proof and that he could be wrong. He holds no firm belief in any specific plot, has no hallucinations, and keeps a steady job. Which feature best supports paranoid personality disorder rather than delusional disorder, persecutory type?","choices":{"A":"Pervasive suspiciousness about the motives of other people","B":"Preserved work functioning outside the area of his mistrust","C":"An absence of auditory or visual hallucinations of any kind","D":"The absence of a fixed delusion despite pervasive mistrust","E":"Onset of his suspiciousness during his early adult years"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Kaplan and Sadock state that paranoid personality disorder can usually be differentiated from delusional disorder by the absence of fixed delusions; this man's mistrust is pervasive but he can consider that he may be wrong and holds no fixed belief about a specific plot. Pervasive suspiciousness is shared by both conditions, so it does not separate them. Delusional disorder also typically leaves functioning intact outside the delusion, so steady work does not distinguish them. Prominent hallucinations are absent in both, so their absence does not help. Early adult onset defines a personality disorder, but it does not exclude delusional disorder, and the text notes paranoid personality disorder may be a premorbid antecedent of delusional disorder, persecutory type.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cluster A personality disorders lack fixed delusions, hallucinations, and formal thought disorder; delusional disorder has at least 1 month of nonbizarre fixed delusions with otherwise preserved functioning. COMLEX: if a paranoid patient's belief becomes fixed and unshakable, reassess for delusional disorder, and diagnose both when they coexist.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — delusional disorder among the primary psychotic disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare Cluster A personality disorders with psychotic disorders by distinguishing suspiciousness, social detachment, eccentricity, magical thinking, ideas of reference, altered perception, and functional impairment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old man has been socially withdrawn and emotionally flat since adolescence, with no history of prior higher functioning or acute deterioration. His family reports he has \"always been this way.\" Applying the framework for distinguishing Cluster A personality disorders from psychotic disorders, which feature most supports a personality disorder rather than a primary psychotic illness?","choices":{"A":"The presence of flat affect, which is thought to occur in personality disorders and not schizophrenia","B":"His age, since primary psychotic disorders are rarely diagnosed before age 30","C":"The presence of social withdrawal, which is thought to occur in personality disorders and not schizophrenia","D":"The family's description of him, since family reports cannot support a psychotic diagnosis","E":"The lifelong stability of the pattern, without decline from a higher prior level of functioning"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A key feature distinguishing a Cluster A personality disorder (like schizoid personality disorder) from a primary psychotic illness such as schizophrenia is that the personality disorder pattern is lifelong and stable, without a distinct deterioration from a previous, higher level of functioning, unlike schizophrenia's more typical course of decline from an earlier baseline. Flat affect is not exclusive to personality disorders; it is also a hallmark negative symptom of schizophrenia, so its presence alone does not distinguish the two. There is no age-30 cutoff for diagnosing a primary psychotic disorder. Social withdrawal similarly occurs in both schizoid personality disorder and the negative symptoms of schizophrenia, so it alone does not distinguish them. A family's negative description of a patient's presentation has no bearing on ruling in or out a psychotic disorder; that determination rests on clinical features and course, not family sentiment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The stability-versus-deterioration distinction (lifelong trait pattern vs. decline from a prior baseline) is the key framework for separating Cluster A personality pathology from a primary psychotic illness presenting with similar negative-symptom-like features. COMLEX: A careful longitudinal history, including collateral information about baseline functioning, is essential to make this distinction accurately.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 4 — Medical/Psychiatric Mimics of Primary Psychosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare Cluster A personality disorders with psychotic disorders by distinguishing suspiciousness, social detachment, eccentricity, magical thinking, ideas of reference, altered perception, and functional impairment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man is being evaluated for possible schizotypal personality disorder versus autism spectrum disorder, both of which can present with social isolation, eccentricity, and unusual language patterns. Which specific feature helps distinguish autism spectrum disorder from schizotypal personality disorder in this evaluation?","choices":{"A":"More severe social impairment, restricted and stereotyped interests, and a primary language disorder","B":"The presence of social isolation, which occurs in autism but not in schizotypal personality disorder","C":"The presence of unusual language, which occurs in schizotypal personality disorder but not in autism","D":"The patient's age, since autism spectrum disorder is not diagnosed for the first time in adults","E":"The complete absence of overlapping features between the two conditions"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Autism spectrum disorder is distinguished from schizotypal personality disorder by more severely impaired social interactions, more restricted and stereotyped behaviors and interests, and the primacy and severity of an accompanying language disorder with compensatory efforts, since the two conditions otherwise share overlapping features of social isolation, eccentricity, and unusual language. Social isolation occurs in both conditions, not autism spectrum disorder exclusively, so its mere presence does not distinguish them. Unusual language patterns also occur in both conditions, not schizotypal personality disorder exclusively. Autism spectrum disorder, while typically identified in childhood, can be recognized or diagnosed in adulthood as well, so claiming it can never be recognized in adulthood is incorrect. The two conditions explicitly share overlapping presenting features (social isolation, eccentricity, language peculiarities), so claiming a complete absence of overlap misstates their actual clinical relationship.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Schizotypal PD vs. autism spectrum disorder is distinguished by relative severity — autism involves more severe social impairment, more restricted/stereotyped interests, and a more primary, severe language disorder. COMLEX: This distinction matters because treatment approaches (psychotherapy focus, developmental/behavioral supports) differ meaningfully between the two conditions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 4 — Medical/Psychiatric Mimics of Primary Psychosis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze defense mechanisms commonly associated with Cluster A and Cluster B presentations, including projection, splitting, denial, displacement, reaction formation, regression, and intellectualization.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with borderline personality disorder, during a period of intense stress after a perceived rejection, reports feeling \"like I'm watching myself from outside my own body\" and describes the situation as feeling unreal. Which defense mechanism best explains this specific experience?","choices":{"A":"Reaction formation","B":"Projective identification","C":"Isolation of affect","D":"Dissociation","E":"Acting out"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Dissociation is an unconscious defense mechanism involving separating a mental or behavioral process from the rest of psychic activity, and patients with borderline personality disorder may demonstrate dissociation during stress, including depersonalization (feeling detached from oneself, as described here) or derealization. Reaction formation converts an unacceptable impulse into its opposite behavior, not a detached, unreal experience of the self. Projective identification involves projecting an aspect of the self onto another person and coercing them to identify with it, which is not what is described in this internal, detached experience. Isolation of affect involves separating an idea or memory from its associated emotion while retaining detailed factual recall, not this depersonalized \"watching myself\" experience. Acting out involves directly expressing an unconscious conflict through action, not an internal perceptual/experiential change like depersonalization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dissociation (depersonalization/derealization) is a recognized borderline personality disorder defense during acute stress, distinct from splitting, which involves categorizing other people rather than one's own sense of self. COMLEX: Grounding techniques and validating the patient's distress, without reinforcing the dissociative experience as literal reality, are standard supportive approaches.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — Transference/Countertransference & Unconscious Defense Mechanisms","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze defense mechanisms commonly associated with Cluster A and Cluster B presentations, including projection, splitting, denial, displacement, reaction formation, regression, and intellectualization.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with borderline personality disorder in an interview becomes intensely aggressive, screaming and cursing at the clinician without any apparent guilt or reflective awareness of the behavior as it occurs. Which defense mechanism best explains this presentation, and what is the recommended clinician response?","choices":{"A":"Acting out; the clinician should interpret the unconscious meaning of the outburst as it happens","B":"Acting out; the clinician should set clear limits, such as leaving the room if the outburst continues","C":"Projection; the clinician should set clear limits, such as leaving the room if the outburst continues","D":"Isolation of affect; the clinician should interpret the unconscious meaning of the outburst as it happens","E":"Splitting; the clinician should interpret the unconscious meaning of the outburst as it happens"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In acting out, patients directly express unconscious wishes or conflicts through action, appearing unaccompanied by guilt because the behavior occurs outside reflective awareness, and the recommended clinician response when a patient loses control is to set clear limits, such as stating they will leave if the behavior continues, since the patient will likely mishear interpretive statements in this state. Confronting the unconscious meaning of the behavior in the moment is not the recommended approach during active acting out, since the patient has lost control and cannot process interpretive content at that time. Projection involves attributing one's own feelings to another, not directly expressing a conflict through aggressive action. Isolation of affect involves detached factual recall, the opposite of this emotionally charged aggressive outburst. Splitting involves dividing people into all-good or all-bad categories, not this direct behavioral expression of conflict through screaming and cursing.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Acting out = direct behavioral expression of an unconscious conflict, occurring outside reflective awareness (hence no apparent guilt in the moment). COMLEX: The clinician's priority during active acting out is safety and limit-setting, not interpretation, since the patient cannot meaningfully process interpretive content while out of control.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — Transference/Countertransference & Unconscious Defense Mechanisms","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl28_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Analyze defense mechanisms commonly associated with Cluster A and Cluster B presentations, including projection, splitting, denial, displacement, reaction formation, regression, and intellectualization.","batch":2,"stem":"A patient with borderline personality disorder tells her therapist, \"You're the only person who has ever truly cared about me — I feel like we understand each other perfectly,\" while the therapist notices this closely mirrors feelings the patient once described toward her mother before a bitter falling out. The therapist recognizes the patient may be unconsciously trying to make the therapist feel this same intense specialness. Which defense mechanism is most specifically illustrated by this three-step process?","choices":{"A":"Projective identification","B":"Projection","C":"Splitting","D":"Reaction formation","E":"Regression"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Projective identification, which appears mainly in borderline personality disorder, consists of three steps: the patient projects an aspect of the self onto someone else, then tries to coerce that person into identifying with what was projected, and finally the recipient and the projector feel a sense of oneness or union, matching this therapist's recognition that the patient is unconsciously drawing them into feeling the same intense specialness she once felt toward her mother. Simple projection involves only attributing an unacknowledged feeling to another person, without the additional coercive and identificatory steps that define projective identification specifically. Simple splitting involves dividing people into all-good or all-bad categories, not this three-step interpersonal projection-and-identification process. Reaction formation involves converting an unacceptable impulse into its opposite, which is not what is described here. Regression involves reverting to an earlier developmental stage of functioning, not this specific interpersonal projective process.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Projective identification's three steps (project → coerce → shared identification/union) distinguish it from simple projection, which lacks the coercive interpersonal component. COMLEX: This defense is particularly relevant to countertransference awareness in treating borderline personality disorder, since clinicians may find themselves unconsciously pulled into the patient's internal object-relational pattern.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — Transference/Countertransference & Unconscious Defense Mechanisms","isHighYield":false}]};
